WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.320
- All right, so this is a
kind of emergency episode

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.840
I think it's fair to say, about the
state of mathematics and artificial

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.040
intelligence. A kind of crisis moment

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.640
in the history of mathematics.
Over the last few weeks,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.600
machines claimed to have solved
some of the hardest problems in the

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.100
history of math. Navier-Stokes,
the quasi- Riemann

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:28.660
hypothesis, the unique games conjecture,
and hundreds of other results. So

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:33.220
here with me, we have Steven Brunton,

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:37.120
who's a mathematician, fluid dynamics
expert at University of Washington,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.940
who can speak to Navier-Stokes,
turbulence, all things fluid,

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:45.940
and many other topics. Also, here's Ankur

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.780
Moitra, a mathematician and
theoretical computer scientist at MIT,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.800
visiting Institute for Advanced
Studies in Princeton this year,

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.400
who can speak to many
things, unique games,

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.620
conjecture, and complexity
theory in general,

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.140
and the mathematics of
how the AI learns and

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:09.740
all things theoretical
computer science. And Alex, a

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.220
mathematician, number theorist
at Rutgers, who can speak to

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:17.140
also very many topics,
including Riemann hypothesis

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:20.780
and Lean, the automated
proof and the automated

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.360
verifier and checking and so on, and
so many other topics in mathematics.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:29.500
All three are brilliant. All are
very kind to show up this early

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.980
morning and talk to me,
assemble this kinda

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.060
multi-agent mini swarm of human brains
to try to interpret what happened

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.560
in this very moment. A lot of
mathematicians, brilliant mathematicians,

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:47.280
including Terence Tao and others,
are freaking out. It's a mix of awe,

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:58.560
terror, dread, and few inklings of light,
of hope also. So I apologize to start

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.180
with a question about feeling,
but what have you been

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:07.740
feeling about this historic moment?
Maybe Alex, do you wanna go first?

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.280
- Yeah, I don't know if I would call it a crisis.
There's a lot of opportunity in that crisis.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.420
It's a chance to rethink what it
is we're trying to accomplish,

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.020
and it's the chaos of
opportunity that some

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:24.900
people are reacting negatively
to, some people are reacting

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:28.520
positively to, and sometimes those
reactions are happening in the same person.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.000
And so I think we're entering,
if we're not already in, a

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.440
period of some kind of golden
age of mathematical knowledge

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.460
after millennia of having a

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.440
candle that we are walking around the
woods, and this is as far as we can see.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:45.580
Now we have a flashlight.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.920
- I think we should comment on the magnitude
of this moment for people who are not aware.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.300
So maybe to quote you,
something like something

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.280
like, "Holy shit, if a human did this, it'd be
an instant Fields Medal." Maybe you didn't say,

00:02:58.340 --> 00:02:59.840
"Holy shit," but basically saying,

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:04.260
"This is an incredible result."
So a lot of people wouldn't have

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.760
expected this amount of progress in
this amount of time, so this is truly

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:11.160
shocking. So you're now
speaking as a person who is

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.060
dealing with the feelings you've felt over the
last couple of days, having found this out.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:19.420
So maybe also it'd be
useful to go back to the,

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.600
the feeling you were
having when you first saw

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.800
the report that, in this
case, OpenAI dropped.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:32.460
And the two of you happened to be
on camera loosely on the same day,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.140
maybe a few drinks in because it
was a conference or a get-together.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.140
Maybe, Ankur, can you speak
to what you were feeling?

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:44.640
- It was an interesting night when the
results dropped. Now, it was not entirely

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.660
unexpected because for weeks there'd
been rumors about the two biggest

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.280
problems in my areas that
were in that collection of

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.020
results, so the unique games
conjecture and RL equals L.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:00.240
There were even rumors about matrix
multiplication, so really my first reaction

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.920
was, "Wow, it's all true.
Thank goodness it's

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.920
finally out there because now people
can actually go through it and process

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:10.060
what's happened and figure
out what to do next."

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:14.780
Now, as to your earlier question, you
know, what are we feeling? Well, for me,

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:18.600
it changes on a daily basis,
but I think the biggest, most

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:22.520
overarching feeling is really feeling
like you're getting pulled in two very

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.740
different directions. My work has always
been about asking the right questions,

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.260
and to that extent, these tools are
amazing for me because, you know,

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.240
they increase my ability to learn
about other fields. It's like

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:40.160
having a world expert who's there
to teach you about things in

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.460
your own language. Your ability to
make connections is just magnified.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:47.900
But, you know, one of the parts of my job
that I really love the most is the mentorship

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.980
aspect. Not just training grad students
and postdocs and seeing them do

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.860
great things, but having them come
with me on these journeys. So the

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.760
part that pulls me in the other
direction is the recognition that,

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:05.420
you know, the playbook and the blueprint
for success that we had for so long is

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:06.800
now thrown out the window.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.180
So it becomes a lot harder to
advise students and postdocs

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.840
about, you know, what are things they
should be working on that are gonna make an

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.840
impact, are gonna make a name for
themselves. So I feel very excited,

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:22.160
but maybe more intellectually
lonely than I felt before,

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.800
in part because, you know, a
lot of times great results come

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.640
from asking very humble
questions, at least for me.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.260
And now there's so much
anxiety and dread and

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:39.380
feeling that, wow, you would have
to do something really momentous to

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.140
stand out in the sea of AI
results, that it feels like

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:47.060
we've lost some of that patience in
terms of planting the seeds from which

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:48.980
the great oak trees grow.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:53.140
- Terence Tao, Fields Medal
winner, wrote, "I am

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.520
horrified by the many person-years
of ongoing patient and

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:00.400
deliberately slow research
efforts, particularly by graduate

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:04.340
students and postdocs, toward many
motivating problems in mathematics

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.840
being casually disrupted
or destroyed by such a

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:13.256
release." Much as one cannot unhear
a movie spoiler or a crossword clue,

00:06:14.076 --> 00:06:17.736
one cannot explore a problem as
profitably and richly once one is

00:06:17.816 --> 00:06:21.776
aware of an existing solution,
and I mourn the path

00:06:21.936 --> 00:06:25.916
not taken and the opportunities lost
in the frantic race to develop the

00:06:25.976 --> 00:06:29.856
technology. So I mean, if we're
being realistic, it's more

00:06:30.076 --> 00:06:34.956
dramatic for a PhD student
and a postdoc than,

00:06:36.756 --> 00:06:40.436
than maybe the way you've
articulated. I mean, you're saying it

00:06:40.616 --> 00:06:44.236
quite calmly, but if I was a
PhD student right now, I'd be

00:06:45.776 --> 00:06:48.756
in a state of, like, shock.

00:06:50.876 --> 00:06:54.816
Like, my whole life-- I was going through some of
this. I know a lot of my friends are programmers.

00:06:55.376 --> 00:06:58.736
They're going through this state of
shock in last December when basically

00:06:59.296 --> 00:07:02.916
most of the programming world
switched to AI writing most of the

00:07:02.976 --> 00:07:06.616
programs. So I would approximate
it to a feeling of kind of a

00:07:06.996 --> 00:07:10.636
loss of meaning, kind of
terror, like dark stuff. Um,

00:07:10.996 --> 00:07:14.836
we'll keep exploring that. Uh, Steve, what do
you think? What were you-- what are you feeling?

00:07:15.436 --> 00:07:19.076
- I would say most of my
feelings are pretty hopeful.

00:07:19.616 --> 00:07:23.156
Um, I think I'm an engineer. I like
solving, you know, problems that

00:07:23.916 --> 00:07:28.236
make technology more realistic for humans,
artificial hearts and airplanes. And

00:07:29.216 --> 00:07:33.136
our ability to solve real applied
problems is going to get much,

00:07:33.436 --> 00:07:37.216
much better. Our scope and reach is gonna
increase, and so for that, I'm extremely

00:07:37.236 --> 00:07:42.436
hopeful. But there is a lot of
uncertainty, and I think, you know, some

00:07:42.476 --> 00:07:46.476
of this fear of meaning,
of losing control, of,

00:07:47.396 --> 00:07:51.216
you know, just being kind of cut
out of the process that we all

00:07:51.536 --> 00:07:55.436
love being a part of it
is a little scary. And

00:07:56.896 --> 00:08:00.876
I don't think any of us would
be surprised at either of the

00:08:00.916 --> 00:08:04.396
kind of two paths that might take place
over the next five or 10 years, where

00:08:04.976 --> 00:08:08.756
we really are just dwarfed by you know,

00:08:09.036 --> 00:08:12.956
other intelligence, or it
kind of levels off, and this

00:08:12.996 --> 00:08:16.496
becomes an incredible
tool that we use for our

00:08:16.536 --> 00:08:18.616
curiosity, for our human problem-solving.

00:08:19.536 --> 00:08:23.456
And that uncertainty is massive. Those
are two very different worlds. And so

00:08:23.516 --> 00:08:27.116
uncertainty is difficult for
humans. I think it's also

00:08:27.136 --> 00:08:30.796
difficult for those of us who have
chosen math. We chose math, you know,

00:08:30.856 --> 00:08:34.536
probably because we liked
rules and formalism and,

00:08:35.076 --> 00:08:36.816
you know, uncertainty is uncomfortable.

00:08:37.796 --> 00:08:41.696
I think the humility aspect is really
important, though. This is a really good

00:08:41.776 --> 00:08:45.356
opportunity for us to remember why we're
doing this. Like, what is human purpose?

00:08:45.416 --> 00:08:49.256
It's not to be famous. It's not
to have a Fields Medal. You know,

00:08:49.816 --> 00:08:52.336
solving interesting problems,
helping other people,

00:08:53.156 --> 00:08:57.016
being curious, just wanting
to understand the world those

00:08:57.076 --> 00:09:00.636
are really good things to kind
of get back. Like, what do we

00:09:00.696 --> 00:09:04.676
need to be satisfied humans? And I
think that's a really interesting

00:09:04.716 --> 00:09:08.756
moment for us to think about. Like, I've
been thinking about it a lot lately. Um,

00:09:09.996 --> 00:09:13.496
it is more difficult for young people,
right? Like figuring out how to,

00:09:14.136 --> 00:09:19.416
to find their way. But I actually have more
faith in them than, than us in a sense.

00:09:19.816 --> 00:09:22.096
- Do you think there's something
to what Terry says about

00:09:23.676 --> 00:09:26.616
it's harder to be motivated when
you know a problem has been solved,

00:09:28.876 --> 00:09:35.176
to explore that space when a problem's
been solved like this by an AI system?

00:09:36.116 --> 00:09:38.676
- Yes and no, in the sense that

00:09:40.396 --> 00:09:43.916
I do very much agree that the
path is as important as the

00:09:43.956 --> 00:09:47.536
destination. Like, if you go on a road
trip, the road trip is, you know, is

00:09:47.816 --> 00:09:49.676
usually just as fun as getting there.

00:09:50.816 --> 00:09:54.236
At the same time, if we found
out that another country had

00:09:54.296 --> 00:09:56.796
invented teleportation or time travel,

00:09:57.616 --> 00:10:01.396
I guarantee we would
reinvent it in the next five

00:10:01.436 --> 00:10:05.136
years. Like, if you knew it was possible and
you could reverse engineer a little bit,

00:10:05.996 --> 00:10:09.976
we would be curious, we would dive into it, and
we would figure it out. So knowing there's a

00:10:10.016 --> 00:10:13.676
solution also is an incredible
motivation to get there much faster.

00:10:14.316 --> 00:10:18.596
- Let me ask the question
this way. If this moment

00:10:19.576 --> 00:10:24.456
marks the moment of the trajectory where
AI basically, quote-unquote, "solves math"

00:10:25.455 --> 00:10:26.676
or kills math,

00:10:27.775 --> 00:10:31.636
can you make the case that that is in fact a
trajectory, and then can you make the case that

00:10:31.676 --> 00:10:35.396
that that is not a trajectory?
Can you steelman both paths?

00:10:36.096 --> 00:10:39.616
That this is a path towards
which, like, mathematics

00:10:40.576 --> 00:10:42.356
as we know it is done completely,

00:10:43.176 --> 00:10:47.076
so that's gonna be a thing done
by machines, and then what humans

00:10:47.116 --> 00:10:52.756
do is something fundamentally
different, or there still will be

00:10:54.796 --> 00:10:58.296
something that looks similar
to our current mathematics.

00:10:58.836 --> 00:11:01.656
Can you make a, steelman both cases? Alex.

00:11:02.096 --> 00:11:05.996
- Yeah. So, you know, you said two things, and I'd like
to separate them. One is solve math, and the other

00:11:06.016 --> 00:11:08.656
is destroy math. So, uh-

00:11:08.656 --> 00:11:12.616
... let's make that precise. What
does solve math mean? If you think

00:11:12.676 --> 00:11:16.416
back to when chess engines first came out
and people were like, "Oh, this is gonna be

00:11:16.456 --> 00:11:20.096
amazing. We'll play centaur games."
So this is a human being plus

00:11:20.216 --> 00:11:23.816
a computer system against another
human being and a computer system,

00:11:24.576 --> 00:11:28.296
and that lasted like 10 seconds-
... and then the computers got so

00:11:28.356 --> 00:11:32.096
good that if you did anything but
choose the top move that the computer

00:11:32.136 --> 00:11:33.776
recommended, you would lose.

00:11:33.856 --> 00:11:37.796
And so the human being's role
in that game became, "Yes,

00:11:37.936 --> 00:11:41.276
accept the first move. Yes, accept the
first move." That's no longer fun,

00:11:41.896 --> 00:11:45.236
and we don't see many games
of centaur chess anymore,

00:11:45.756 --> 00:11:49.536
right? And so in fact, I
just had a conversation

00:11:49.616 --> 00:11:53.596
with a colleague named Thomas
Koberda where we axiomatized

00:11:53.676 --> 00:11:57.196
our argument for what the future might
look like, and one of the axioms

00:11:57.556 --> 00:12:01.336
that I'm willing to even accept for the
sake of argument is what I call the

00:12:01.396 --> 00:12:05.336
solved axiom, and the solved
axiom says, let's assume at

00:12:05.356 --> 00:12:09.176
some future point, we're nowhere near
there now, and I don't expect us to be

00:12:09.316 --> 00:12:13.256
there necessarily. We'll see what you
know, where the capabilities taper

00:12:13.316 --> 00:12:17.136
off if, if they do. But let's
assume the solved axiom which

00:12:17.196 --> 00:12:21.088
says any mathematical problem
that can be solved with human

00:12:21.168 --> 00:12:24.648
interacting with AI could also
have been solved by AI alone.

00:12:25.108 --> 00:12:28.788
That's the solved axiom. So that,
that would mean that really

00:12:28.868 --> 00:12:32.808
there's nothing new for a human being
to cont-- you know, you can say to an

00:12:32.868 --> 00:12:36.548
AI system, "Okay, I have problem
X, I wanna think about problem X,"

00:12:36.948 --> 00:12:40.648
but the only way that I will make progress,
any progress that it'll make on problem

00:12:40.928 --> 00:12:44.868
X, will just be press a button. The AI thinks
about it. It doesn't necessarily solve

00:12:44.928 --> 00:12:48.748
it, doesn't mean that it'll solve every
problem. The Riemann hypothesis is not solved,

00:12:49.308 --> 00:12:53.268
okay? That's what I really want the solution
to. I just didn't think in my lifetime I would

00:12:53.328 --> 00:12:58.748
see any kind of progress- ... towards
it that we saw a few days ago. So

00:12:59.248 --> 00:13:03.048
it's not that AI systems are solving every
problem. They're solving every problem that

00:13:03.108 --> 00:13:06.748
could be solved by humans interacting
with AI. That's what that

00:13:06.848 --> 00:13:10.468
axiom would be. So I'm saying,
if we are ever in that world,

00:13:11.148 --> 00:13:14.968
does that necessarily destroy
mathematics? And my strong

00:13:15.028 --> 00:13:18.408
argument is no, I don't think
it destroys mathematics.

00:13:18.908 --> 00:13:20.908
- If that axiom is true,
what's the role of humans?

00:13:21.448 --> 00:13:25.148
- So I don't think that mathematics, pure

00:13:25.188 --> 00:13:28.988
mathematics, not engineering
mathematics, not things that will,

00:13:29.408 --> 00:13:33.248
cure cancer and, and solve, you
know, jet-- everybody has a jet

00:13:33.308 --> 00:13:37.268
pack or whatever other technological advancement
you might imagine. Pure mathematics, you

00:13:37.308 --> 00:13:40.468
know, we know that we're proving theorems
just because we're curious about them.

00:13:41.628 --> 00:13:45.548
I don't think the solution to theorems
living in ones and zeros in data

00:13:45.628 --> 00:13:49.428
centers is valuable to human
beings. I think the value of pure

00:13:49.468 --> 00:13:51.848
mathematics is that it lives
in the heads of human beings.

00:13:52.308 --> 00:13:56.148
- So just to, you know, add to this, I mean,
I'm glad you came right out with the question

00:13:56.168 --> 00:13:57.388
that's on everyone's minds.

00:13:57.388 --> 00:14:01.368
So thank you for that. Um, I
would caution against overfitting

00:14:01.408 --> 00:14:03.388
to one objective function for this, right?

00:14:04.148 --> 00:14:07.988
Because, you know, we used to think of
ourselves as just the smartest things around.

00:14:08.108 --> 00:14:11.808
You know, human mathematicians can
solve, you know, reach the highest

00:14:11.868 --> 00:14:15.748
treetops. But now we're faced with the
fact that many of the problems that we

00:14:15.808 --> 00:14:19.408
thought were, you know, quite a
ways out have been solved by AI.

00:14:20.368 --> 00:14:23.908
But maybe one of the consolations we can
take is actually the fact that we're very

00:14:23.968 --> 00:14:27.148
computationally limited compared
to the way that AI is solving it.

00:14:28.027 --> 00:14:30.788
You know, given the same finish
line of proving the same theorem,

00:14:31.728 --> 00:14:35.828
I can't really hold that many papers
or ideas in my head at one time.

00:14:36.708 --> 00:14:40.088
So in order for me to reach that same
finish line, maybe I have to develop

00:14:40.148 --> 00:14:43.828
more generalizable and
abstractable ways of getting there

00:14:44.428 --> 00:14:48.288
that then help you not just solve
that one problem, but, you know,

00:14:48.408 --> 00:14:52.028
explain connections or other
directions you want to get

00:14:52.088 --> 00:14:56.008
into, right? So it's always amazing
when you look back on papers you've

00:14:56.028 --> 00:14:59.748
written, and you can see these threads,
invisible sometimes, that connect

00:14:59.788 --> 00:15:03.768
different areas of work, because
developing systems of ways of

00:15:03.828 --> 00:15:07.768
understanding problems helps you for
surrounding problems, not just to get

00:15:07.828 --> 00:15:10.788
a Lean, formalized, you
know, one theorem down.

00:15:11.528 --> 00:15:15.408
- But the interesting thing here is those
threads, what you're saying is sometimes

00:15:15.568 --> 00:15:19.468
invisible, are quite visible to
the AI systems now. It seems

00:15:19.508 --> 00:15:23.348
like that's perhaps the actual
very reason why they're

00:15:23.428 --> 00:15:27.408
able to make so much progress,
is they can extract the threads

00:15:27.448 --> 00:15:31.148
and pull on them because they have
the view of the so many papers.

00:15:31.648 --> 00:15:35.628
- I think it depends on what you mean by
threads. You know, for me, the reason

00:15:35.668 --> 00:15:39.128
you prove results is so that you better
understand the world around you.

00:15:39.248 --> 00:15:44.508
Mathematics, to me, is a vehicle for
abstraction and understanding things.

00:15:45.348 --> 00:15:49.208
And you know, it's when you
have a major result, what's

00:15:49.248 --> 00:15:52.928
important is not just the truth
of the statement, but what you've

00:15:53.008 --> 00:15:56.548
learned en route to doing that.
Usually, people's first question is,

00:15:56.628 --> 00:16:00.428
"Okay, you proved something fantastic,
but how did you do it?" And

00:16:00.468 --> 00:16:04.428
then people, once they digest that new
idea, that new definition, that new

00:16:04.468 --> 00:16:08.568
way of looking at things, there are lots
of other problems nearby that fall.

00:16:09.528 --> 00:16:13.488
So somehow you're building structures that
help you attack not just one problem, but

00:16:13.568 --> 00:16:14.988
a whole sea of problems.

00:16:15.848 --> 00:16:19.448
And, you know, it's not that we
need fully formalized, you know,

00:16:19.508 --> 00:16:22.828
results to do that. Sometimes we
reason in very sloppy metaphors

00:16:23.468 --> 00:16:26.368
that, you know, help us
conceptualize the world around us.

00:16:27.188 --> 00:16:31.168
- And how much of this is something Alex
mentioned offline, attention is all you

00:16:31.228 --> 00:16:37.408
need. How much of this is the human ability
to shine a light on particular areas

00:16:37.468 --> 00:16:38.548
that, quote-unquote, matter?

00:16:39.507 --> 00:16:42.648
So the human ability to ask the right
questions, the important questions.

00:16:42.828 --> 00:16:46.568
- That's always been the case. So
within this-- before AI, there's a--

00:16:46.988 --> 00:16:50.788
you can think of, like, here's the axioms, and just
abstractly from the axioms, you can get to logical

00:16:50.828 --> 00:16:54.408
deductions, right? There are theorems
that we could prove from the axioms. And

00:16:54.728 --> 00:16:58.568
there's a much, much, much larger space
of theorems that we could prove than the

00:16:58.648 --> 00:17:00.708
space that human beings
ever actually care about.

00:17:01.548 --> 00:17:05.528
And so, you know, so by,
almost by definition, it's

00:17:05.548 --> 00:17:09.528
like a tautology that the theorems that we'll
get excited about are the theorems that

00:17:09.568 --> 00:17:11.088
we care about as humans.

00:17:11.908 --> 00:17:15.468
So maybe AI can convince us that there's some
interesting structure. Maybe it can say, "Hey,

00:17:15.788 --> 00:17:19.148
look over here. Let me give you this new
definition or something." It hasn't done that yet.

00:17:19.548 --> 00:17:22.888
- And sometimes the mechanism by which we
select the things that we care about,

00:17:23.848 --> 00:17:27.788
it could have an application. So
Navier-Stokes is connected to fluids and all,

00:17:27.908 --> 00:17:35.548
um natural systems. But it could also be
a thing that some kinda mixture of beauty

00:17:36.308 --> 00:17:40.848
and difficulty that just pulls at
us. Maybe there's an intuition.

00:17:41.748 --> 00:17:45.528
You know, the prime numbers are full
of these really complicated problems,

00:17:46.368 --> 00:17:50.088
That maybe don't have an
obvious application in the

00:17:50.108 --> 00:17:53.248
immediate sense, but it pulls
at us somehow and maybe,

00:17:54.028 --> 00:17:57.608
As a symptom of the
pursuit of the hard thing,

00:17:59.428 --> 00:18:03.168
Great applied mathematics
and engineering and

00:18:03.248 --> 00:18:05.468
solutions to real world
problems comes out.

00:18:06.128 --> 00:18:09.828
- So just, you know, as an example, right? A
lot of times when I'm working on a problem,

00:18:10.388 --> 00:18:14.368
I might have some, like, bigger goal that
I want to accomplish, but usually what I

00:18:14.408 --> 00:18:17.968
do is I try to find the simplest,
most embarrassing open problem

00:18:18.368 --> 00:18:19.648
that I still don't know how to solve.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:24.319
You know, that aspect of like digesting
it down to something, boy, how can we

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:28.220
not know this? It, you know, it feels
like it's on the right track because

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:32.060
then when we can solve that example, maybe
we're assembling the set of tools we

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:36.020
need to make attacks on
other problems. So I do

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:38.780
think we're gonna have to become
a lot more deliberate about,

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:43.680
you know, what do we care about?
How do we use these tools from a

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:47.680
metacognition standpoint? You know,
what exactly are we delegating to them

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:51.640
and what are we not delegating to them? And
we're really at the beginning stages where

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:53.860
we're just trying to piece
it together as we go along.

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:58.320
You know, on the one hand, looking for a
proof is kind of like exploring this tree of

00:18:58.360 --> 00:19:02.120
possibilities. You might have
some intuitions based on some

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:06.180
other proofs you've seen about, you
know, which directions to go in. Maybe

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:09.940
I should go down this branch, maybe I should
go down this branch. But sometimes we

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:13.440
encounter, you know, points on the tree
which have really high branching factor.

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:17.220
We just don't know which way to go down. We
don't even have a guess about if we went

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:19.640
down them, what would we discover?

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:24.520
And now in this centaur world
of math, we suddenly, you

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:28.500
know, have something else intelligent that
we can interact with, right? And what is

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:32.520
the right way to use that tool to search
for the beautiful theories, I think will be

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:36.640
challenging and evolving, but it's important
to not lose sight of the fact that

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:38.840
we don't just care about theorems.

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:43.600
So I, you know, I'm a mathematician, I'm
a theoretical computer scientist, but

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:47.080
I've also been reading a little bit
more broadly, you know, about social

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:50.740
sciences just to sort of make my peace with
all of the changes that have been happening.

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:55.040
One of the things that really stuck in my mind
that I liked is there's something called the

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:56.820
doorman fallacy, which
is very interesting.

00:19:57.640 --> 00:20:00.100
So the idea is that you take a complex job

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:07.240
and you boil it down to its simplest, most
visible task. So what does a doorman do?

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:12.500
I guess they hold the door for people
as they're coming and going, right? And

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:16.520
when we lived in New York, we really
enjoyed talking to our doorman. Uh,

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:20.520
you know, they were wonderful people,
but, you know, if you digest it down to

00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:24.400
just that simple job, you can think, "Oh I

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:28.440
can replace them just by a
sliding door that detects

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:32.460
when people are going or coming," and
you miss out on all the aspects of their

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:36.580
job that, you know, add a lot of
value and are harder to articulate.

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:41.400
And I think that's a good way to think about
mathematics in the age of AI too. What does

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:42.420
a mathematician do?

00:20:43.240 --> 00:20:46.840
You know, the simplest and most visible
part of the job is we prove theorems.

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:51.300
And, you know, theorems about conjectures
that were open for hundreds of

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:55.300
years, like Fermat's Last Theorem,
these are very visible things that

00:20:55.340 --> 00:20:59.080
we've spent a lot of time
popularizing. But I don't think that

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:03.040
that's all that a mathematician does. I
like to think about it as a mathematician

00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:06.600
is someone who thinks deeply,
and a byproduct of thinking

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:10.300
deeply is, you know, proving
old conjectures, but it's also

00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:14.220
building new theories, building new
ways of looking at old things, making

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:18.080
new conceptual connections.
And these are parts that

00:21:18.320 --> 00:21:22.200
you know, maybe AI will swallow up
too, but we're not there yet at

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:26.100
this moment. So even though this
is a momentous time, I don't think

00:21:26.140 --> 00:21:29.520
it's the right time to hit the panic button.
I think it's the right time to really

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:33.960
recenter in terms of what our values are
and have these conversations as a field,

00:21:34.520 --> 00:21:38.440
and remember that what we do really has
a plurality of different aesthetics

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:42.080
and goals, and that when
one thing becomes cheap,

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:46.020
like problem-solving for
well-defined things, it makes the

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:49.140
other things that are orthogonal
to it all that much more valuable.

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:53.520
- That's actually really beautiful. The doorman
fallacy, going to Perplexity here, is the mistake

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:57.220
of reducing a person's job to its
most obvious task and assuming that

00:21:57.300 --> 00:22:01.220
automating that task replaces
the value of the whole role. A

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:04.840
hotel decides, quote, "We're
paying someone to open a door. An

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:08.780
automatic door can do
that more cheaply." But

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:12.540
in fact, the doorman also welcomes
guests and recognizes returning

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:16.500
customers, handles luggage
and hails taxis, provides

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:20.240
security and discourages
unwanted visitors, and

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:23.620
signals prestige and makes
guests feel cared for.

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:24.980
- That was my favorite part.

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:28.880
I looked up what a doorman does, and
boy, we have a lot of commonalities with

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:32.380
mathematicians. If I don't signal brand
value, I don't know what I'm doing.

00:22:32.460 --> 00:22:36.279
- Exactly. That's funny.
But I think the task

00:22:36.339 --> 00:22:40.140
for us humans in general,
but also mathematicians,

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:45.460
is to come up with that list, like actually
make more explicit exactly what it

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:49.200
is that humans do. And I
haven't heard any of you

00:22:49.260 --> 00:22:52.420
mention beauty. I apologize
if I've mentioned beauty

00:22:52.660 --> 00:22:56.640
excessively already. But there's
something that mathematicians

00:22:56.720 --> 00:23:00.960
are, from my outsider sort of
fan perspective, are kinda

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:08.280
expositors, scholars of things
beautiful, broadly defined.

00:23:08.600 --> 00:23:12.300
- That's exactly right is until
five seconds ago, there was a

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:15.860
near perfect correlation
between the ability to solve

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:19.680
new deep theorems and what
we actually want as human

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:23.420
mathematicians. And what we
want is exactly what you said,

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:27.380
beauty and understanding
and depth of thought, and

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:31.520
the fact that you can press a button
and get what used to be our proxy,

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:36.000
without any effort, without any
deep thought, and without any,

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:40.540
necessarily knowledge. Like we
have people saying, you know, "Do

00:23:40.600 --> 00:23:45.280
a breakthrough," and then putting
whatever they find on Twitter and

00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:48.400
and now, you know, what are
mathematicians supposed to do with that?

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:52.940
- I would go one step behind answering
questions and doing problem-solving is

00:23:53.060 --> 00:23:56.900
asking the right questions. So like
that is how I think you measure

00:23:57.440 --> 00:24:01.380
good problem-solving is asking the right
sub-questions, asking, you know, a

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:04.280
question that is interesting
and can be solved and can be

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:08.400
interpreted. And so I feel
like the value of asking good

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:12.060
questions has always been
something we value in our

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:15.840
colleagues, and that's gonna
become increasingly more valuable.

00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:20.200
- I agree completely, and just to, you
know, emphasize a point that was sort of

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:21.060
hidden in there

00:24:22.032 --> 00:24:25.672
I think asking the right questions
requires a lot of mathematical insight.

00:24:26.072 --> 00:24:26.652
- Mm-hmm. Absolutely.

00:24:26.672 --> 00:24:30.612
- So, you know, you'll see people make that comment
all the time, you know, "What are we gonna value

00:24:30.632 --> 00:24:34.252
going forward? It's asking the right
question. It's giving great talks."

00:24:34.952 --> 00:24:38.852
But, you know, I wouldn't minimize, not
that you were, but just to be explicit, I

00:24:38.872 --> 00:24:42.752
would not minimize the role in
mathematical insight in both of those

00:24:42.792 --> 00:24:46.652
tasks, because it's when we say that, you
know, talks will become more important,

00:24:46.772 --> 00:24:50.432
I'm not imagining that, you know,
we're all gonna become Twitter

00:24:50.572 --> 00:24:53.392
influencers- ... and TikTok whatevers.

00:24:53.452 --> 00:24:53.792
- I hope not.

00:24:54.292 --> 00:24:57.772
- I hope not. Because, you
know, really what stands out

00:24:57.992 --> 00:25:01.652
is having ideas that get adopted by your

00:25:01.692 --> 00:25:05.672
community that end up becoming
powerful over time. So, you

00:25:05.692 --> 00:25:09.312
know, I wanna tie this back to something that
Lex brought up earlier on, which is, you know,

00:25:09.872 --> 00:25:13.632
he asked, am I being overly optimistic?
Probably, right, compared to,

00:25:13.932 --> 00:25:17.852
you know, how grad students view these
things. But I think one of the things

00:25:17.892 --> 00:25:21.512
that I'm most worried about in the near term
is what's gonna happen to our systems and

00:25:21.572 --> 00:25:22.312
institutions.

00:25:23.312 --> 00:25:27.171
You know, it's not just hiring and tenure
and all these things, but it's really

00:25:27.231 --> 00:25:31.152
that when a grad student starts
off their PhD, you know, they're

00:25:31.212 --> 00:25:34.792
a new entrant to the field. They're
trying to absorb what are the

00:25:34.852 --> 00:25:38.752
techniques in their field, what are the
important problems, and they're trying to

00:25:38.792 --> 00:25:40.932
figure out how to make
a name for themselves.

00:25:41.852 --> 00:25:45.512
And what we used to do is we would give them
a blueprint that was very simple to follow.

00:25:45.632 --> 00:25:49.392
Take a problem people care
about and improve it, and then

00:25:49.432 --> 00:25:52.772
people will know who you are. You
know, you'll get papers in top

00:25:52.832 --> 00:25:56.732
venues. You know, doors will open
for you. But now I think that

00:25:56.772 --> 00:26:00.752
blueprint has changed in a lot of
ways because there are so many

00:26:00.792 --> 00:26:04.412
results that are happening right now
that we're all just overloaded with it,

00:26:05.152 --> 00:26:08.872
and I think it's okay to take time to rethink
that blueprint. We still care about the

00:26:08.912 --> 00:26:12.512
same things, like ideas that are
gonna influence your community,

00:26:13.072 --> 00:26:16.852
but they're just harder to
operationalize in terms of some simple

00:26:16.992 --> 00:26:20.972
rubric that we can give them. So I'm
still optimistic. I just think it's gonna

00:26:21.032 --> 00:26:24.672
take, you know, reworking a lot of our
systems and institutions to do that.

00:26:25.152 --> 00:26:28.812
- By the way, and so to, sorry, to go back to
your point, I don't actually believe the solved

00:26:28.892 --> 00:26:32.471
axiom. I don't think we're there.
I can prove a theorem, but I don't

00:26:32.532 --> 00:26:36.252
understand why the proof works.
There's some magic that happens over

00:26:36.312 --> 00:26:39.892
here, and until I generalize somehow the

00:26:39.932 --> 00:26:43.832
phenomenon and understand it more
deeply, then I'll see why the, the

00:26:43.852 --> 00:26:47.652
proof works the way it does. Maybe that's one
of the reasons why I don't actually believe the

00:26:47.692 --> 00:26:50.832
solved axiom, because the
things that we do are much more

00:26:50.872 --> 00:26:54.392
exploratory and what
constitutes a good question

00:26:54.632 --> 00:26:57.792
beyond just, "Here's the Riemann
hypothesis, make some progress on it."

00:26:57.992 --> 00:27:00.352
- So I think you also brought up a
really important point, this, like,

00:27:01.212 --> 00:27:05.992
generality and generalization of our concepts.
So related to beauty and mathematics,

00:27:06.872 --> 00:27:10.692
I think one of the gold standards
has always been how simple

00:27:10.772 --> 00:27:14.712
can your result be and how much
does it generalize. And, you

00:27:14.732 --> 00:27:18.132
know, out of this infinite space
of theorems that could be proven,

00:27:18.792 --> 00:27:22.732
Finding some structure that is simple and

00:27:22.792 --> 00:27:26.672
generalizes, like, that is, I think,
still the gold standard. And unless I'm

00:27:26.712 --> 00:27:32.452
mistaken, humans are still asking the
questions. So, like, we, you know, prove

00:27:32.592 --> 00:27:36.372
existence uniqueness of
Navier-Stokes with AI, but

00:27:36.472 --> 00:27:39.632
humans are guiding this. Humans are still asking
the questions. And, like, I would love to see

00:27:40.632 --> 00:27:44.552
the next breakthrough if you
had a machine that can prove

00:27:44.592 --> 00:27:48.492
any theorem you pose. I want it to
start telling me what questions

00:27:48.592 --> 00:27:49.372
I should be asking.

00:27:49.792 --> 00:27:53.292
- Do you think it's possible that
that machine just does a for loop-

00:27:54.972 --> 00:27:58.132
... and finds all the
simple and generalizable,

00:27:58.592 --> 00:28:00.832
basically all the E equals MC squared?

00:28:00.992 --> 00:28:04.812
- That's happening today, actually. So, like,
there are absolutely research programs that are

00:28:05.452 --> 00:28:09.392
systematically, automatically discovering
kind of minimal physical laws.

00:28:09.452 --> 00:28:14.672
So this is less mathematical theorems,
more physics governing laws and equations.

00:28:15.072 --> 00:28:19.092
- So observing natural systems and trying
to see in those natural systems-

00:28:19.932 --> 00:28:23.152
... if there's simple physical
laws that model how they operate.

00:28:23.592 --> 00:28:26.792
- Yeah, distilling it down to, like,
the basics. So you can kind of

00:28:26.832 --> 00:28:30.272
recapitulate known theories. If you
observe balls dropping, you can,

00:28:30.932 --> 00:28:34.832
you know, deduce gravity and F equals MA.
If you observe fluids, you can deduce

00:28:34.892 --> 00:28:38.491
Navier-Stokes. And then
taking that to data, we

00:28:38.551 --> 00:28:41.551
haven't written down
simple equations: weather,

00:28:41.952 --> 00:28:44.592
turbulence, solar flares,
things like that.

00:28:45.532 --> 00:28:49.192
And I'm assuming similar things are
happening in math where, you know, there are

00:28:49.232 --> 00:28:53.672
probably these, like, minimal
pieces that can be deduced.

00:28:54.012 --> 00:28:57.872
- Do you think there's a large space of
simple and generalizable things in math

00:28:58.532 --> 00:29:00.552
that could be discovered automatically?

00:29:01.072 --> 00:29:04.932
- So my version of the solved
axiom is that anything that I do

00:29:04.992 --> 00:29:06.152
as a human mathematician,

00:29:07.472 --> 00:29:09.832
I've tried on several occasions
to sit down and say, "Okay,

00:29:10.672 --> 00:29:14.452
you know, LLMs will never do this
that I do." And I'm like, "Well,

00:29:14.972 --> 00:29:18.652
why not? I can ask a question. You
know, I can ask it a question."

00:29:18.692 --> 00:29:21.052
I can ask it, "What
questions should I ask?"

00:29:21.332 --> 00:29:24.992
Right? There's sort of it-- Because
it's such a generalized system,

00:29:25.732 --> 00:29:28.932
uh, I'm not saying that it's good
at all of those things now. But

00:29:29.612 --> 00:29:33.532
my version of the solved axiom is
anything that I can contribute as a

00:29:33.572 --> 00:29:37.152
human being, it could also have
contributed on its own. And so

00:29:37.632 --> 00:29:41.492
what I really mean by that is
in terms of value, what do I

00:29:41.552 --> 00:29:45.172
bring to the community? It
can't be I asked a great

00:29:45.232 --> 00:29:49.112
question. It can't necessarily be in
this world of the solved axiom, which

00:29:49.152 --> 00:29:54.172
again, I don't believe. But even in that
world, it should be something other than I

00:29:54.232 --> 00:29:57.992
asked a great question, I proved a
great theorem. And even in that world,

00:29:58.192 --> 00:30:01.752
I think you need mathematicians, people
with human mathematical expertise.

00:30:02.652 --> 00:30:06.532
- Ankur, do you believe in the solved
axiom? Where on the spectrum of, like,

00:30:07.152 --> 00:30:08.432
how close we can get to it,

00:30:09.652 --> 00:30:13.272
and in that spectrum, what's the
role of the human, do you think?

00:30:13.632 --> 00:30:17.472
- I guess I don't really believe
in the solved axiom. I

00:30:17.512 --> 00:30:21.184
think- You know, maybe it's half
true in some ways, and figuring out

00:30:21.744 --> 00:30:25.664
which half of it is true will be very
interesting and challenging. So I think there'll

00:30:25.704 --> 00:30:29.564
be major surprises and more
to come in terms of what AI

00:30:29.604 --> 00:30:33.564
can solve. But, you know, for
me, when you're facing a new

00:30:33.624 --> 00:30:37.444
technology that's really,
really good, the danger is that

00:30:37.724 --> 00:30:42.004
when you try to conceptualize why it's
working, you do so by anthropomorphizing it.

00:30:43.124 --> 00:30:46.604
So, you know, maybe one of the earlier
lessons here is from deep learning.

00:30:47.384 --> 00:30:51.144
So there are these amazing examples. You
know, you can take neural nets, and you can

00:30:51.204 --> 00:30:54.604
do human-level or better
object recognition.

00:30:55.604 --> 00:31:00.424
But in general, the way that it succeeds
is less robust than how a human does,

00:31:01.264 --> 00:31:04.544
Because there are all these
examples like adversarial examples,

00:31:05.484 --> 00:31:09.244
where you can add very slight patterns to
natural images that can completely fool

00:31:09.844 --> 00:31:12.504
these object detection
systems, but not a human.

00:31:13.584 --> 00:31:17.264
And, you know, I had a former colleague
at MIT, Alex Madry, that had some really

00:31:17.304 --> 00:31:21.264
nice work, which I really enjoyed. It was,
um-- I think the title was something like,

00:31:21.524 --> 00:31:25.424
uh, Adversarial Examples Are Features,
Not Bugs. So they did the following

00:31:25.524 --> 00:31:31.264
cool experiment. So you take a bunch of
natural data, maybe pictures of cats and

00:31:31.324 --> 00:31:35.204
dogs. You train a deep network
to identify them very well.

00:31:35.344 --> 00:31:39.324
You add some adversarial
perturbations to them that, you know,

00:31:39.484 --> 00:31:43.184
make to the AI system a cat
look like a dog and vice

00:31:43.264 --> 00:31:47.084
versa. So it's kind of think like a
small little pattern that changes

00:31:47.124 --> 00:31:50.984
how the object detector works. And
then you create a totally fake data

00:31:51.024 --> 00:31:54.804
set. You know, it's got pictures of
cats with dog patterns on top, and you

00:31:54.864 --> 00:31:58.824
label it as dog, and vice versa
for cats. And amazingly, when you

00:31:58.924 --> 00:32:02.804
train a deep network on this with
all the wrong labels, it's gonna

00:32:02.824 --> 00:32:06.564
learn something that does well on
natural data. So somehow, when we

00:32:06.624 --> 00:32:10.264
recognize what a cat and dog is, we
do it, you know, in a way that we can

00:32:10.304 --> 00:32:16.984
articulate in terms of you know, its legs
and its tail and its shape in general.

00:32:17.644 --> 00:32:21.564
But a lot of times when you
know, deep networks or AI can do

00:32:21.624 --> 00:32:26.344
things that they might be able to find
trapdoors or backdoors to solving the problem

00:32:27.204 --> 00:32:30.484
that use a lot of statistical relationships
in the data that a human doesn't.

00:32:31.364 --> 00:32:35.204
So to me, you know, it's
important to not, you know, draw

00:32:35.244 --> 00:32:38.824
this boundary, you know, very sharply
between what you can do and what

00:32:38.924 --> 00:32:41.544
AI can't do because you're gonna be wrong.

00:32:42.404 --> 00:32:46.284
But that doesn't mean that I don't
believe there will emerge some kind of

00:32:46.344 --> 00:32:49.824
boundary, if only for
the fact that I think we

00:32:49.924 --> 00:32:53.904
over-anthropomorphize the way that they
solve problems. And when I'm looking through

00:32:53.924 --> 00:32:57.784
the OpenAI solutions, they just
don't look like a human has solved

00:32:57.824 --> 00:33:01.764
those problems. So to me, there still seems
to be a very big qualitative difference,

00:33:01.824 --> 00:33:05.764
and that's an opportunity really
for collaboration and centaur

00:33:05.824 --> 00:33:07.244
math more than it is, you know,

00:33:08.064 --> 00:33:12.764
dread about the AI overlords. But, you
know, who knows? Could be wrong next week.

00:33:13.004 --> 00:33:16.924
- What's your intuition about
how much of this is AI just

00:33:17.004 --> 00:33:20.864
tying together a bunch of
thousands of disparate papers

00:33:20.904 --> 00:33:24.824
and pulling at the threads, sorry
for bringing back the metaphor, but

00:33:24.884 --> 00:33:28.084
the threads of the different
papers and then just basically

00:33:28.164 --> 00:33:32.024
articulating the proofs that were already
there in the different papers and you're

00:33:32.084 --> 00:33:36.184
making minor connections? Or
is it actually developing

00:33:37.064 --> 00:33:41.364
new mathematical ideas?
Have you seen glimmers of

00:33:42.224 --> 00:33:47.164
whatever that genius AlphaZero
move in the game of Go?

00:33:47.504 --> 00:33:48.364
- Yeah, move thirty-seven.

00:33:48.684 --> 00:33:49.504
- Move thirty-seven.

00:33:49.884 --> 00:33:53.644
- So I haven't read all seven hundred
of their papers. The ones that I

00:33:53.684 --> 00:33:57.464
have looked at, you know, some people
speak about this dismissively like,

00:33:57.524 --> 00:34:01.384
"Oh, it was in the convex hull."
You do the moves that we know as,

00:34:01.704 --> 00:34:05.564
as humans, and you just do them really
well and do a really good tree search.

00:34:06.324 --> 00:34:09.924
I wouldn't call it the convex hull. I
think Tim Gowers had a really good way of

00:34:10.384 --> 00:34:14.284
talking about it instead as the
semigroup generated by all of these

00:34:14.324 --> 00:34:20.744
things. Yes, it's the same notion of
putting together known moves, but the space

00:34:20.864 --> 00:34:24.824
of known moves, what it can
accomplish is sort of much,

00:34:24.904 --> 00:34:28.684
much bigger than this kind of derogatory

00:34:28.744 --> 00:34:32.504
convex hull. So with--
in terms of the quasi-RH

00:34:32.944 --> 00:34:36.664
the moves that it took are all things
that were known to human beings

00:34:37.364 --> 00:34:41.124
and there's a very long cascade
of them that is kind of

00:34:41.944 --> 00:34:45.924
shocking when you look at it and say, "Oh my God,
that's so beautiful. How did you think to-- How did

00:34:45.964 --> 00:34:49.884
you..." I'm anthropomorphizing it, of course. "How
did you think to do this move after that move

00:34:49.904 --> 00:34:52.744
and after that move?"
And yeah, that does it.

00:34:53.044 --> 00:34:57.004
- Could you articulate to the more
general public the intuition,

00:34:57.284 --> 00:35:01.164
the line between semigroup and convex
hull, the derogatory convex hull,

00:35:01.764 --> 00:35:05.704
and quasi-RH, of course, is the
Quasi-Riemann Hypothesis, that one

00:35:05.744 --> 00:35:06.404
of the big proofs.

00:35:06.604 --> 00:35:10.924
- Yeah. Let me try to do it like this.
You have-- think of like a balloon.

00:35:11.884 --> 00:35:15.064
So the inside of the
balloon is its convex hull.

00:35:15.104 --> 00:35:18.924
And so we have like all of these results
that-- all these ideas that mathematicians

00:35:18.964 --> 00:35:23.604
have thought up, and then anything inside
of that is some linear combination of it.

00:35:23.884 --> 00:35:27.344
But it feels compact. It's like
there's this kind of closed,

00:35:27.444 --> 00:35:31.284
enclosed area, and within that area,
like AI hasn't really made new

00:35:31.344 --> 00:35:35.584
progress 'cause it hasn't moved the balloon
out. It hasn't given us a new idea yet.

00:35:36.144 --> 00:35:39.924
- And it's derogatory because the
intuition there is we could have

00:35:39.984 --> 00:35:43.904
done it, mathematician could have done it
because it's already quite obvious. It's already

00:35:43.944 --> 00:35:47.684
within the space of things that have
already been thought about and developed.

00:35:48.184 --> 00:35:52.044
- Yeah. So it has read every paper. It's
exactly what Ankur was just saying. It has

00:35:52.104 --> 00:35:55.764
read every paper. It
has internalized all of

00:35:55.824 --> 00:35:59.784
that. That's more than any human
brain not even could hold, but has

00:35:59.844 --> 00:36:03.744
time to read and learn and
internalize. So in terms of

00:36:03.784 --> 00:36:05.944
breadth, yes, it beats any human being.

00:36:06.804 --> 00:36:10.744
And then in terms of depth, you
know, has it really generated

00:36:11.044 --> 00:36:14.944
a genuinely new idea? And
you can argue that at least

00:36:14.984 --> 00:36:18.924
in the case of quasi-RH, I would not
say it's a genuinely new idea. It's a,

00:36:19.144 --> 00:36:23.084
it's a beautiful combination of a lot of things that
were very well known. There are a lot of people who

00:36:23.104 --> 00:36:26.984
are kicking themselves that had some
idea in that direction twenty years

00:36:27.044 --> 00:36:30.804
ago, and two years ago, there was
a big breakthrough on cubic theta

00:36:30.844 --> 00:36:34.684
functions and cubic Gauss sums and,
you know, that ended up being the path

00:36:34.724 --> 00:36:41.528
that the AI took to the solution.
But so to contrast that with a

00:36:41.648 --> 00:36:45.608
semigroup, I think of a
semigroup as like tiles. So you

00:36:45.647 --> 00:36:49.608
know, you're just playing tiles on the board,
and what you're allowed to do is whenever

00:36:49.648 --> 00:36:53.568
you have a tile, you can put another tile to
the left, to the right, to the front or to the

00:36:53.608 --> 00:36:57.428
back. And then you can just
start tiling things. And

00:36:57.588 --> 00:37:01.208
that's like a semigroup action.
And the question is, well,

00:37:01.568 --> 00:37:06.468
how far can you reach with that? You
can go all-- I don't know. You know?

00:37:06.548 --> 00:37:10.508
You can go really, really far
out with existing ideas might

00:37:10.568 --> 00:37:14.128
prove a whole lot more than we as
humans have been able to explore.

00:37:14.768 --> 00:37:16.828
- So your intuition is more in the semigroup

00:37:17.968 --> 00:37:24.228
direction, meaning like there is
at least glimmers of expansion

00:37:25.068 --> 00:37:28.248
versus linear combinations
inside the convex hull.

00:37:28.868 --> 00:37:32.828
- You know, how we choose to
interpret what it's doing I think

00:37:32.888 --> 00:37:36.628
is up to us. But throughout the
history of mathematics, there have

00:37:36.668 --> 00:37:40.588
been very seemingly
difficult and deep problems

00:37:41.048 --> 00:37:44.748
that then someone comes up
with a three-page solution

00:37:45.288 --> 00:37:49.248
that is very elementary and could've
been explained to an undergrad and

00:37:49.288 --> 00:37:52.048
could've been discovered twenty years
before when the problem was first posed.

00:37:52.548 --> 00:37:56.188
And had it been discovered twenty
years before, it would be an

00:37:56.248 --> 00:38:00.108
exercise in a textbook somewhere. And
because it-- that solution wasn't

00:38:00.148 --> 00:38:04.088
discovered, and really bright people worked on it for
a long time and made incremental progress and made

00:38:04.108 --> 00:38:07.928
the problem really famous, then by the time
it was actually solved, it was a really big

00:38:07.968 --> 00:38:08.288
deal.

00:38:09.088 --> 00:38:12.948
That happens from time to time in mathematics.
And what I said a year ago when I

00:38:12.968 --> 00:38:16.888
wrote-- So there was this um, International
Congress of Mathematicians this

00:38:16.948 --> 00:38:20.328
summer, and I was writing my proceedings
that they wanted ten months in advance.

00:38:20.868 --> 00:38:24.808
So I said, you know, "What am I gonna write about
math and AI now that will still be relevant,

00:38:25.088 --> 00:38:28.988
Ten months from now?" And I
think what's there is somewhat

00:38:29.048 --> 00:38:32.708
evergreen. I stand by what I wrote. But
one of the things that I wrote there was

00:38:32.748 --> 00:38:36.328
exactly that we're going to discover
that a whole lot more problems

00:38:36.768 --> 00:38:40.668
that we thought were unreachable by
current technology actually are reachable

00:38:40.708 --> 00:38:44.608
by current technology if you can put it
together in just the right way, and that's what

00:38:44.668 --> 00:38:48.568
AI is amazing at. Again, someone
could interpret that as, "Oh, so it's

00:38:48.628 --> 00:38:52.548
inside, you know, these solutions were inside
the convex hull." You wanna call it that, that's

00:38:52.588 --> 00:38:55.048
fine. I think that's, you
know-- It's not invalid.

00:38:55.308 --> 00:38:59.148
- So I like all these metaphors, you know, also
the balloon. So the important thing about the

00:38:59.188 --> 00:39:02.568
balloon is the boundary between what's
inside and outside, and what makes it

00:39:02.608 --> 00:39:06.588
derogatory is the fact that, you
know, you haven't seemingly changed

00:39:06.628 --> 00:39:10.328
the boundary. But I-- you know,
definitely these problems have

00:39:10.388 --> 00:39:11.288
changed the boundary.

00:39:12.088 --> 00:39:16.028
And, you know, maybe a more concrete thing is
just that the types of things that people are

00:39:16.048 --> 00:39:22.028
gonna work on after that drop of
OpenAI problems is forever different.

00:39:23.148 --> 00:39:26.868
You know, there were at least a couple of
results there that are like, you know,

00:39:27.068 --> 00:39:31.048
field-altering results that will change
what types of problems you give to young

00:39:31.108 --> 00:39:35.088
researchers, what areas you want to
explore, because your conception of

00:39:35.128 --> 00:39:36.888
the landscape is just that much different.

00:39:37.288 --> 00:39:41.168
- So not just the-- because it
expanded, but because the very

00:39:41.228 --> 00:39:45.168
expansion changed the direction
of the interesting questions.

00:39:45.548 --> 00:39:49.488
- That's right. So in fact, you know, one of the
problems which probably, you know, maybe it's the

00:39:49.548 --> 00:39:53.108
easiest to explain what kind of effect it
had on people is not actually from the

00:39:53.168 --> 00:40:00.668
OpenAI drop. It was an Anthropic result
that was written up by my colleague Virgie

00:40:00.728 --> 00:40:04.608
Vassilevska Williams and then
Josh Alman. So this was about the

00:40:04.808 --> 00:40:08.768
3SUM problem. And I'd say that
there it has a major effect on

00:40:08.808 --> 00:40:12.388
what's called fine-grained complexity
theory because there was a big

00:40:12.448 --> 00:40:16.408
surprise that, you know, a very basic
problem that you give people in, like,

00:40:16.528 --> 00:40:20.088
coding interviews can actually
be solved asymptotically much

00:40:20.188 --> 00:40:21.868
faster than the solution anyone knew,

00:40:22.688 --> 00:40:26.528
and that was counter to what the community
was betting. In fact, there was a

00:40:26.568 --> 00:40:30.488
whole theory built up around the idea
that if this problem really is that hard,

00:40:30.988 --> 00:40:33.428
then all these other problems
inherit its hardness.

00:40:34.248 --> 00:40:38.148
So now, as a result of that problem being
easier than we thought, you know, having a

00:40:38.268 --> 00:40:42.228
bit better of an algorithm, you
know, yes, that gives you a lot

00:40:42.288 --> 00:40:46.008
new algorithms across the
entire landscape. But it also

00:40:46.068 --> 00:40:49.928
changes the character of the field because
the goal there was to put all those problems

00:40:49.968 --> 00:40:53.028
together and said that we should
be studying them all together.

00:40:53.928 --> 00:40:57.808
But now we're sort of back in the state of affairs
where maybe the right way to study them is

00:40:57.887 --> 00:40:58.788
separately after all.

00:41:00.008 --> 00:41:03.868
So I think these are examples where,
you know, irrespective of how

00:41:03.928 --> 00:41:07.568
much, you know, deep new
generalizable ideas we get out there,

00:41:08.108 --> 00:41:12.068
even just knowing the, you know,
truth of the theorem is gonna change

00:41:12.108 --> 00:41:15.868
the way that people organize research
in that area. And, you know, there's no

00:41:15.928 --> 00:41:18.448
putting the genie back in the
bottle on some of these problems.

00:41:18.828 --> 00:41:22.648
- I mean, imagine one of the seven
hundred was factoring in polynomial

00:41:22.688 --> 00:41:25.428
time. That would absolutely
change-- Everybody would know.

00:41:25.828 --> 00:41:29.728
Like, you can't use your credit card on
Amazon anymore. All of e-commerce is gone.

00:41:29.788 --> 00:41:32.828
All of secure communications
is gone in-- like that.

00:41:33.028 --> 00:41:34.768
- Can you explain factoring
in polynomial time?

00:41:35.208 --> 00:41:39.148
- Yeah. So if you give me
two very large numbers, a

00:41:39.188 --> 00:41:43.128
million digits each, I can
multiply them together and do

00:41:43.168 --> 00:41:46.568
that in a split second. If
you ask me to go backwards,

00:41:47.088 --> 00:41:50.648
say you took two large prime numbers, you multiply
them together, you give me this, the product,

00:41:51.328 --> 00:41:55.288
and you ask me, "Figure out which two numbers..."
You know, just factor. Don't we, don't

00:41:55.308 --> 00:41:59.068
we ask our kids to learn how to factor
in school? You know, it's a lie.

00:41:59.268 --> 00:42:03.248
We do not know how to factor.
And the fact that we don't

00:42:03.288 --> 00:42:07.128
know how to factor is why we have
the capacity, the ability to

00:42:07.488 --> 00:42:11.468
communicate with one another in all
kinds of secure ways. So all of

00:42:11.768 --> 00:42:15.608
modern-- basically the modern world
depends on the fact that we don't

00:42:15.668 --> 00:42:20.668
have an algorithm for taking a large number
and pulling its prime factors out of it.

00:42:21.168 --> 00:42:25.048
- So a lot of e-commerce does because
that's the basis for a lot of the schemes

00:42:25.088 --> 00:42:29.008
in public key encryption. But, you
know, famously, there are quantum

00:42:29.108 --> 00:42:31.148
algorithms for factoring, so-

00:42:31.188 --> 00:42:35.808
... there's a lot of emphasis these
days on, you know, finding post-quantum

00:42:36.648 --> 00:42:38.928
you know, bases for cryptography too.

00:42:38.948 --> 00:42:40.508
- Yeah, lattice-based methods and-

00:42:40.728 --> 00:42:44.548
Yeah, we're already concerned about what will happen when
we have quantum computers large enough to, but we still

00:42:44.588 --> 00:42:48.348
have a little bit of time before
quantum computers get large enough

00:42:48.408 --> 00:42:51.728
to make this an actual
problem. But there's no

00:42:52.168 --> 00:42:56.828
theoretical reason. I mean, okay,
factoring's probably not NP hard but

00:42:57.658 --> 00:43:01.418
You know, I mean, let's say, let's say prove
that P doesn't equal NP. Maybe that doesn't--

00:43:01.718 --> 00:43:05.398
That would be amazing, obviously,
but that doesn't if it proves that P

00:43:05.458 --> 00:43:07.778
equals NP, that would be completely

00:43:08.078 --> 00:43:09.198
- That would be very interesting.

00:43:09.498 --> 00:43:10.618
- That would be very interesting.

00:43:10.878 --> 00:43:14.758
- I'll definitely ask about more
broadly in the context of complexity

00:43:14.838 --> 00:43:18.318
theory, but presumably
OpenAI or any other company

00:43:18.878 --> 00:43:21.598
would not drop that result carelessly.

00:43:22.158 --> 00:43:26.118
- That's the kind of thing that should
go through, yeah, the government and-

00:43:26.378 --> 00:43:30.077
- Sometimes certain mathematical
results quite obviously

00:43:30.178 --> 00:43:34.078
lead to dramatic impact
on the world. Other

00:43:34.118 --> 00:43:39.438
results might take maybe months to
digest, but that one would just,

00:43:40.318 --> 00:43:43.938
yeah, would affect the economic
systems of entire nations, so.

00:43:44.218 --> 00:43:47.778
- How many mathematicians
does the NSA employ?

00:43:48.698 --> 00:43:49.158
- Thousands.

00:43:49.198 --> 00:43:51.058
- Thousands, like something like this.

00:43:51.858 --> 00:43:53.658
I've always done this thought
experiment, you know. What,

00:43:54.678 --> 00:43:58.658
what might they have that they just wouldn't
tell us about? And this was ten years ago, you

00:43:58.698 --> 00:44:00.378
know, that some

00:44:01.198 --> 00:44:04.938
evidence showed that collaborative
teams of mathematicians could solve

00:44:05.178 --> 00:44:08.798
much, much harder problems than any one
individual mathematician if they, like,

00:44:09.438 --> 00:44:15.118
broke it down in the right way. So I don't
know. Do you mind if I just revisit the-

00:44:15.198 --> 00:44:15.258
- Yes

00:44:15.438 --> 00:44:18.998
- ... the balloon for a second? 'Cause
I actually like the semigroup

00:44:19.038 --> 00:44:22.978
analogy better than the
convex hull, but I don't

00:44:23.038 --> 00:44:26.918
think it's as it should be
interpreted as derogatory as

00:44:26.978 --> 00:44:30.678
it is even if it was just
that these machines are

00:44:30.738 --> 00:44:33.578
interpolating human knowledge
rather than extrapolating.

00:44:34.718 --> 00:44:38.638
Because even if there is this convex
hull of ideas and things that could

00:44:38.678 --> 00:44:41.998
be pieced together from bits
that humans have invented,

00:44:43.318 --> 00:44:47.218
we're not doing that fast
enough or, you know, there's a

00:44:47.258 --> 00:44:50.458
lot of opaqueness, and it's--
this balloon is not see-through.

00:44:51.238 --> 00:44:55.218
And so even if you can make this convex
hull much more transparent, kind of

00:44:55.238 --> 00:44:59.218
like lift the fog, give mathematicians
radar so they can see what's, you know,

00:44:59.298 --> 00:45:00.158
over the horizon,

00:45:01.058 --> 00:45:05.018
that's pretty incredible, and I don't think
we should be interpreting that as you

00:45:05.058 --> 00:45:08.078
know, derogatory at all.
I do think it is more,

00:45:08.718 --> 00:45:11.098
more extrapolatory than that, though.

00:45:11.378 --> 00:45:15.338
- Yeah, we should say probably the size
of the convex hull. We don't know

00:45:16.438 --> 00:45:17.998
actually how big it is.

00:45:18.378 --> 00:45:21.778
- Well, that's part of the point. We don't know how
big it is. We don't know what's next to each other.

00:45:21.878 --> 00:45:28.858
Like, there's so much fog of what we know.
Like, And that's exciting to lift that.

00:45:29.458 --> 00:45:32.858
- But human beings do make new
definitions, you know, bring new

00:45:32.918 --> 00:45:36.658
concepts in, and that fundamentally feels

00:45:36.718 --> 00:45:40.658
different from combining existing
ideas and repackaging them.

00:45:40.698 --> 00:45:43.758
When human beings do that, and
they do that from time to time-

00:45:44.178 --> 00:45:47.858
... it really feels like, okay,
there's a new boundary point,

00:45:48.258 --> 00:45:51.798
and now what's the convex hull of
that, of what you could prove? So

00:45:52.238 --> 00:45:55.478
it's like you can take the balloon,
and there are ways to pull on it and-

00:45:55.538 --> 00:45:57.158
- And we know when it expands. Like somehow-

00:45:57.158 --> 00:45:58.998
... we have an intuition for
when it's getting bigger.

00:45:59.058 --> 00:46:00.278
Like, you can recognize that.

00:46:00.938 --> 00:46:04.578
- In many ways, the archetype of what
the researcher looks like is gonna

00:46:04.678 --> 00:46:06.858
change because of this new technology too.

00:46:07.678 --> 00:46:11.578
You know, I'm always amazed that when
you look back historically, how much

00:46:11.918 --> 00:46:16.698
very different fields of math
really spawned from similar ideas.

00:46:17.498 --> 00:46:21.478
And a lot of those connections drove the
development in the beginning, but over time,

00:46:21.558 --> 00:46:25.278
they become a bit more specialized.
And I think certainly the way that we

00:46:25.358 --> 00:46:27.318
train graduate students, you know, we'll

00:46:28.198 --> 00:46:31.738
need to make sure that they're not
just overspecialized in the same way.

00:46:32.458 --> 00:46:36.398
You know, I don't know what the future of hiring will
look like. I don't know what the future of journals will

00:46:36.438 --> 00:46:40.418
look like. But I think people
who think broadly about,

00:46:40.698 --> 00:46:44.618
you know, a bigger swath of
mathematics will be in the

00:46:44.818 --> 00:46:47.798
best position to benefit
from these technologies.

00:46:48.618 --> 00:46:52.598
You know, that would be my guess. Can I
ask a almost silly-sounding question?

00:46:52.698 --> 00:46:58.618
But so a large part for many
decades of research has been

00:47:00.298 --> 00:47:05.238
the paper. If you were to guess in
the years to come, how does that

00:47:05.598 --> 00:47:09.578
change how a human
contributes their idea, maybe

00:47:09.618 --> 00:47:12.958
collaborate, how a team of humans
contribute an idea into the public space?

00:47:14.058 --> 00:47:17.977
- So, I mean, I don't know about in
pure math, but in engineering and

00:47:18.037 --> 00:47:21.158
some of the applied sciences,
we already have a pretty

00:47:22.258 --> 00:47:26.478
rapidly shifting view of what
an academic contribution is. So

00:47:27.518 --> 00:47:31.478
if you write code in part of
a larger infrastructure that

00:47:31.538 --> 00:47:35.158
is reusable, that, you know, supports
larger projects, so think of

00:47:35.218 --> 00:47:38.958
LIGO or some of the big,
you know, billion-dollar

00:47:39.018 --> 00:47:42.678
astronomy projects or, you know,
cancer research projects that

00:47:42.718 --> 00:47:46.358
require hundreds of people
and huge resources already,

00:47:47.858 --> 00:47:51.758
it's not necessarily one 15-page PDF. You

00:47:51.798 --> 00:47:54.978
know, there's lots of
different pieces, code, data,

00:47:55.538 --> 00:47:59.458
reusable bits that can be fit
into a larger framework. And

00:47:59.518 --> 00:48:03.038
in my mind, that is kind
of obviously even more

00:48:03.078 --> 00:48:06.678
relevant with AI, is having,
you know, a little workable

00:48:07.078 --> 00:48:10.658
piece or widget or API or code

00:48:11.038 --> 00:48:14.878
base or data set that can be
leveraged by the community or

00:48:14.918 --> 00:48:16.978
by AI is increasingly valuable,

00:48:17.798 --> 00:48:21.698
even more than just, like, one result
or one paper. And I don't know

00:48:21.758 --> 00:48:23.338
how that looks in, in pure math.

00:48:23.678 --> 00:48:27.198
- Can you comment on your view how
everything's changing? Even just observing

00:48:28.178 --> 00:48:32.258
the insane evolution of even arXiv,

00:48:33.178 --> 00:48:37.138
which is similar in some ways to what's happening
on GitHub, for example. A lot of people

00:48:37.178 --> 00:48:41.158
in the open source movement are really struggling
to know what to do. Some are predicting

00:48:41.198 --> 00:48:42.338
the death of GitHub.

00:48:42.938 --> 00:48:45.778
- Why? Just 'cause there's so much
code being added to GitHub-

00:48:46.058 --> 00:48:47.718
... that it can't sustain the growth?

00:48:47.998 --> 00:48:51.198
- So one of the things that makes the open
source movement successful is there's a kinda

00:48:52.098 --> 00:48:56.018
one or a few folks that really
manage each repository, the

00:48:56.058 --> 00:48:58.178
human beings, and they are, um-

00:48:58.970 --> 00:49:02.769
- psychologically and on many levels are
becoming overwhelmed by the, all the pull

00:49:02.790 --> 00:49:06.410
requests. So it becomes
impossible to manage and they get

00:49:06.470 --> 00:49:08.770
exhausted and there's this whole feeling.

00:49:09.150 --> 00:49:12.890
The open source movement, maybe
similar to the academic environment,

00:49:13.990 --> 00:49:20.990
requires the humans to be motivated. And
if you get worn out by the just similar

00:49:21.190 --> 00:49:23.030
the ideas that Terry Tao mentioned,

00:49:24.290 --> 00:49:28.350
that you just quit. And so that's a failure
mode that you want to kind of avoid.

00:49:28.590 --> 00:49:32.510
- So journals were already in math
under very, very severe strain

00:49:32.590 --> 00:49:36.490
from the number of things before AI,
having nothing to do with AI, just

00:49:36.890 --> 00:49:40.710
there was, you know, more people producing
more knowledge, sending it to journals, and

00:49:40.750 --> 00:49:44.830
journals had to referee it, and referees
are exhausted and being asked to,

00:49:45.650 --> 00:49:49.230
work really hard to understand
these theorems and check them.

00:49:49.670 --> 00:49:53.530
The only thing worse than that system,
I think, is not having that system.

00:49:53.810 --> 00:49:57.530
Because the way that, like, our
mathematical epistemology, the way that we

00:49:57.590 --> 00:50:03.250
arrive at truth, is deliberately slow
and careful and precise. Go back

00:50:03.310 --> 00:50:06.930
to Perelman's solution of the
Poincaré conjecture. He puts these

00:50:06.990 --> 00:50:08.230
papers on the archive,

00:50:09.170 --> 00:50:13.150
and then people around the world are, you know,
shocked by this, and so they invite him to come

00:50:13.210 --> 00:50:17.070
give lectures. He gives he comes to Stony
Brook, he comes to NYU, he comes to Princeton.

00:50:17.430 --> 00:50:20.930
He's going all around the
country and the world, and

00:50:21.290 --> 00:50:24.750
he's giving three-hour long impromptu

00:50:24.930 --> 00:50:28.850
explanations of, of, you know, maybe an hour
is, is him explaining what he's doing, and

00:50:28.870 --> 00:50:32.750
the next two hours are
people saying, "On page two

00:50:32.790 --> 00:50:36.770
hundred and forty-seven, Lemma four hundred
and twelve, I don't understand how you

00:50:36.830 --> 00:50:40.450
do this." And then he goes to the board and gives
them a half-hour lecture on how he does that.

00:50:40.890 --> 00:50:42.490
So the entire thing lives in his mind.

00:50:43.490 --> 00:50:47.410
He's done the best he can to communicate
it on paper, and then it's up to

00:50:47.530 --> 00:50:51.470
us, the community, to go back
through it very carefully, very

00:50:51.510 --> 00:50:55.350
in a detailed and precise
way and agree, yes,

00:50:55.470 --> 00:50:59.390
this is a real proof of the Poincaré
conjecture. And that took years to do

00:51:00.130 --> 00:51:04.110
and a whole lot of human
effort. So coming up with a

00:51:04.150 --> 00:51:08.130
solution is only the first step in the process,
and that process has to be deliberate,

00:51:08.210 --> 00:51:12.110
and it has to be community-driven.
And so that's what the journal

00:51:12.170 --> 00:51:13.230
system is trying to accomplish.

00:51:13.450 --> 00:51:17.050
- So you make a really
interesting point and it kinda

00:51:17.090 --> 00:51:20.650
highlights this traditional view of
a pure mathematician as this, like,

00:51:20.870 --> 00:51:22.590
lone genius who,

00:51:23.550 --> 00:51:27.630
you know, solves this singular problem
and then goes explaining it to the world.

00:51:29.010 --> 00:51:32.690
In many other fields,
we have moved past the

00:51:32.750 --> 00:51:36.350
point where we can't
individually advance, you know,

00:51:36.830 --> 00:51:40.150
circuit design or aircraft design or
protein folding or any, you know,

00:51:40.250 --> 00:51:44.150
astronomy, and we have to work together
in these billion-dollar collaborative

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:50.330
projects. And again, I'm an engineer,
so I'm thinking leadership is obviously

00:51:50.890 --> 00:51:54.230
the thing that a community needs when
it's having this kind of crisis of

00:51:54.290 --> 00:52:00.890
organization and individuality moving
towards a more collective model. And that's

00:52:00.930 --> 00:52:03.710
interesting because I know so
many mathematicians, and, like,

00:52:05.230 --> 00:52:08.450
by kind of organization, this is a very

00:52:08.510 --> 00:52:12.350
independent group of people who don't
like being told what to do, who don't

00:52:12.390 --> 00:52:16.230
like being told what to study,
who are very independent by

00:52:16.270 --> 00:52:20.070
their very nature. You know,
we like solitude and working

00:52:20.150 --> 00:52:22.930
alone. That's why we, you know,
spend so much time doing it.

00:52:23.130 --> 00:52:26.390
- I think that's a really good point. I
mean, it's interesting just to see the

00:52:26.430 --> 00:52:31.790
differences in working styles, even between
math and physics, you know, with things-

00:52:31.850 --> 00:52:35.630
... like the Large Hadron Collider.
So I mean, let me take a contrarian

00:52:35.690 --> 00:52:39.510
view for these things, which is
you know, you should never let a

00:52:39.550 --> 00:52:43.190
good crisis go to waste.
I agree the journals were

00:52:43.270 --> 00:52:47.070
overloaded, the conferences were
overloaded. It always strikes me that we're

00:52:47.090 --> 00:52:50.950
doing so much work, you know, to
review these things, to handle

00:52:51.010 --> 00:52:53.070
all these things, and now
we're faced with the task that

00:52:53.910 --> 00:52:56.530
someone's gotta review all
these AI-generated papers.

00:52:57.390 --> 00:53:01.210
And some of them are important, but, you know,
it's really hard to get your community on

00:53:01.250 --> 00:53:05.150
board with doing that. You know, I don't
know what's gonna happen, but I would really

00:53:05.190 --> 00:53:09.170
be in favor for finding new
models, not to replace the

00:53:09.230 --> 00:53:13.070
old ones, but to relieve some of
the pressures that have come about.

00:53:13.810 --> 00:53:17.310
So, you know, I'd love to see models
where, you know, maybe we take a more

00:53:17.390 --> 00:53:21.190
collaborative effort to
verifying some of these AI

00:53:21.230 --> 00:53:24.850
proofs, right? So some of
the OpenAI results that have

00:53:24.910 --> 00:53:28.710
dropped, you know, improvements
on Fast Fourier Transform.

00:53:29.310 --> 00:53:32.630
You know, there's been tweet streams
with people improving the constant.

00:53:33.650 --> 00:53:36.790
I don't really know what to make of
that. You know, I'm not even on Twitter.

00:53:37.510 --> 00:53:41.470
You know, and it seems to me that we
need to figure out other modes of

00:53:41.530 --> 00:53:45.150
working, you know, for organizing
what are the state of all the

00:53:45.190 --> 00:53:49.430
improvements, but also how do we
organize and tame these ideas?

00:53:49.910 --> 00:53:53.850
So it's kind of striking that, you know, the
way that we think about publications is you

00:53:53.910 --> 00:53:57.810
work hard on your draft, you post
it on arXiv, and that's your draft.

00:53:58.490 --> 00:54:02.370
You know, you might update and create
a new version at some point or a third

00:54:02.430 --> 00:54:05.630
version, but the draft belongs to you.

00:54:06.290 --> 00:54:10.010
It's not really up to the community to add
notes. "Hey, I think you can do this argument

00:54:10.050 --> 00:54:13.830
differently. Hey, I think this is simplified
this way." But maybe there are some

00:54:13.870 --> 00:54:17.050
kinds of things that we need
other models for, you know,

00:54:17.230 --> 00:54:21.150
allowing crowdsourcing for
our understanding to evolve.

00:54:21.730 --> 00:54:25.290
I'd be all for that. You know,
the other thing is I love giving

00:54:25.350 --> 00:54:29.250
research talks. You know, one of my
favorite parts about giving research talks

00:54:29.330 --> 00:54:33.310
is you can take all of the messy
complexities of the paper, the things

00:54:33.350 --> 00:54:37.230
where you just knew you had to dot
the I's and cross the T's, but

00:54:37.250 --> 00:54:41.150
that wasn't really what excited you.
There was some key idea, and there

00:54:41.170 --> 00:54:44.970
was some special case that really
illustrated why that idea was powerful.

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:49.390
And that's the part you really want to explain
to people, because when asked, someone asks

00:54:49.430 --> 00:54:53.030
me about Lemma 57, I don't
even remember it, right? And

00:54:53.070 --> 00:54:56.910
maybe, you know, this
idea that uh, a paper

00:54:56.970 --> 00:54:59.870
is just this monolithic
thing designed to be

00:55:00.678 --> 00:55:04.218
you know, written down and completely
formalized. Well, now we can

00:55:04.318 --> 00:55:07.878
trust Lean formalizations to
some extent for some of the

00:55:08.438 --> 00:55:12.378
technical building blocks, and
that can allow us to rework

00:55:12.418 --> 00:55:16.178
the way that we do the writing in the first place,
so we can put more of the emphasis on the work

00:55:16.278 --> 00:55:19.518
on, you know, the
promulgation of those ideas.

00:55:20.118 --> 00:55:23.938
- The Mathlib model is exactly what you were
describing, where instead of a paper, you have a

00:55:23.978 --> 00:55:27.878
PR, you have a pull request. The
maintainers and the reviewers will

00:55:27.918 --> 00:55:31.418
go through the pull request. They'll make
suggestions here and there. I mean, the

00:55:31.458 --> 00:55:35.438
referees make suggestions, you can improve
this and that, but it's not a public process

00:55:35.498 --> 00:55:38.998
in a journal. In a GitHub open

00:55:39.138 --> 00:55:42.978
workplace, you can see exactly
who contributed what idea

00:55:43.038 --> 00:55:47.018
that simplified an argument or you're, you
know, you're reusing this... You're reinventing

00:55:47.058 --> 00:55:50.498
this idea that already exists in this part of
the library. You should just quote that and

00:55:50.838 --> 00:55:54.598
refactor this part, whatever. So and

00:55:55.038 --> 00:55:58.878
Mathlib working formally allows one
for the first time. Like, one of the

00:55:58.918 --> 00:56:02.358
problems with mathematics is that we don't

00:56:02.438 --> 00:56:05.917
have a perfectly
well-written specification

00:56:06.638 --> 00:56:10.618
to our theorems, our technical lemmas.
There's-- If you open up a random

00:56:10.678 --> 00:56:15.078
page in a random math book and read
a lemma, there's so much context-

00:56:15.078 --> 00:56:18.778
... that the author-- 'cause it's
so annoying to write something that

00:56:18.838 --> 00:56:22.738
fills in every bit of context. So we
just assume you've read the previous

00:56:22.778 --> 00:56:26.658
sixty pages. Now I can just tell you what the
next step is in that lemma. But then when you try

00:56:26.698 --> 00:56:30.598
to-- That means you can't just take it
off the shelf the way you can a part,

00:56:30.698 --> 00:56:32.458
a physical part when
you're building a rocket.

00:56:33.038 --> 00:56:36.958
And so if all of mathematics
is done in the future

00:56:37.018 --> 00:56:40.738
formally, then the specifications
are built into the

00:56:40.838 --> 00:56:44.798
object. You know you're not using it in
the wrong way, 'cause you can't use it

00:56:44.838 --> 00:56:46.218
in the wrong way 'cause
Lean won't let you,

00:56:47.038 --> 00:56:50.958
and that is the sort of crowdsourced version
of mathematics. And that was the dream

00:56:50.998 --> 00:56:53.658
of Mathlib- ... when it
was started ten years ago,

00:56:55.058 --> 00:56:57.278
was to run mathematics
more like a science.

00:56:57.558 --> 00:56:58.738
- As a quick aside, can you,

00:56:59.698 --> 00:57:04.318
mention what Mathlib is and Lean, just 'cause
we mentioned it a few times for folks?

00:57:04.558 --> 00:57:08.258
- Sure. So mathematics is,
in the ideal, a formal

00:57:08.358 --> 00:57:12.218
process starting with an
axiomatized system and then logical

00:57:12.318 --> 00:57:16.178
rules of inference from there. So if
people think of -- I don't know, do people

00:57:16.218 --> 00:57:19.978
still learn Euclid's geometry in high
school or something? That's supposed to be

00:57:20.038 --> 00:57:23.378
the kind of model for kids to start seeing

00:57:23.778 --> 00:57:26.598
proofs for the first time and
seeing an axiomatic structure.

00:57:27.138 --> 00:57:30.558
And you can take that idea
and write it in software

00:57:31.138 --> 00:57:35.018
and say, "Here are the actual axioms of
mathematics. Here are the rules of logical

00:57:35.078 --> 00:57:38.978
inference." And Lean, there's a long history
of this kind of software. It's called

00:57:39.078 --> 00:57:42.818
interactive theorem provers, where you're
writing line by line in a proof, and the

00:57:42.838 --> 00:57:46.638
computer says, "Yes, this line does
follow by logical inference from the

00:57:46.678 --> 00:57:50.578
goal state on the previous line."
So Lean is software that allows you

00:57:50.998 --> 00:57:54.758
to check all kinds of things. It can
check the specifications of your code

00:57:55.538 --> 00:57:56.578
align with what you,

00:57:57.438 --> 00:58:01.378
what you are trying to define. That's what it
was -- what it was invented for was bug-free

00:58:01.438 --> 00:58:05.938
code. And hopefully, in the future,
we will have bug-free code because AI

00:58:05.978 --> 00:58:09.818
systems are really good at finding
bugs in code written by humans, so

00:58:10.118 --> 00:58:13.938
that's-- it's becoming ever more
important for that application. But

00:58:13.978 --> 00:58:17.978
another application is to
check mathematical results-

00:58:18.018 --> 00:58:23.218
... and allow you to write a mathematical
result in a very formal way. And so

00:58:24.038 --> 00:58:27.898
now Lean itself is just the hardware that
checks the logical steps. You do not want

00:58:27.958 --> 00:58:31.058
to start every math project by
defining what the natural numbers are,

00:58:31.658 --> 00:58:35.598
using zero and the successor, you
know, the Peano axioms of the natural

00:58:35.638 --> 00:58:39.258
numbers. So it's nice
to have a large general

00:58:39.358 --> 00:58:42.918
library that's extremely well
maintained, extremely well

00:58:42.998 --> 00:58:46.858
organized, and so on. And
that's what Mathlib is. Mathlib

00:58:46.898 --> 00:58:50.518
is the Lean library for formalizing

00:58:50.818 --> 00:58:54.638
mathematics in the Lean ecosystem.
And again, Lean is just one

00:58:54.698 --> 00:58:58.418
interactive theorem prover. There are lots
of others. It's the one that so far has been

00:58:58.458 --> 00:59:01.938
winning the math wars, if there are
any math wars. It's a competition of

00:59:02.398 --> 00:59:03.238
two people or something.

00:59:03.458 --> 00:59:07.838
- And it's the technology
used in part to show that

00:59:08.658 --> 00:59:12.878
the OpenAI release of all the different
proofs, that the proofs are correct.

00:59:13.658 --> 00:59:16.318
- That's right. It has
become now standard to,

00:59:17.138 --> 00:59:20.998
because autoformalization has gotten
to the point where AI systems can not

00:59:21.037 --> 00:59:24.638
only prove a theorem in natural language. You
can't trust their natural language proofs.

00:59:24.698 --> 00:59:27.038
These are stochastic processes.

00:59:27.078 --> 00:59:30.678
And so the fact that they're saying things
doesn't mean that they necessarily have

00:59:31.358 --> 00:59:35.278
a model of mathematics in their heads,
whatever that means, the same way

00:59:35.298 --> 00:59:38.918
that we do. But that's okay 'cause
they'll write a Lean -- Lean code

00:59:39.298 --> 00:59:43.178
that builds on top of Mathlib, builds on
top of the Prime Number Theorem Pro-- Plus

00:59:43.218 --> 00:59:47.098
project or whatever,
and checks that it has

00:59:47.158 --> 00:59:49.658
indeed solved this problem
down to the axioms.

00:59:50.018 --> 00:59:53.938
- So this is where I'll just take the tangent,
and the thought experiment may be a bit

00:59:53.958 --> 00:59:57.878
philosophical at this moment, but
what if you have that black box

00:59:57.918 --> 01:00:01.838
that gives you proofs that you can't
understand? Let's say the more interesting case

01:00:01.898 --> 01:00:05.598
where, not because it's so big,
but because the ideas just

01:00:06.038 --> 01:00:10.278
don't make sense to you, to any of the
world expert mathematicians in that field.

01:00:11.318 --> 01:00:12.238
What do you do with that?

01:00:13.198 --> 01:00:17.078
- So the only way, sort of almost
by definition, because the

01:00:17.538 --> 01:00:21.278
logical rules of inference are
written down, and there, there's

01:00:21.358 --> 01:00:25.118
only a few of them. There's a handful of
them. The axioms, there's a handful of them.

01:00:25.758 --> 01:00:29.758
So the only way that you have mathematics
that's not understandable is scale.

01:00:30.138 --> 01:00:31.778
- Let me add a contrarian take.

01:00:31.838 --> 01:00:31.938
- Yeah, please.

01:00:31.958 --> 01:00:37.278
- So one of the things I also wonder is
what would unformalizing math look like?

01:00:37.638 --> 01:00:41.498
You know certainly formalization
helps tremendously that we

01:00:41.518 --> 01:00:45.298
have confidence that the results are
correct. But much of the way that we

01:00:45.358 --> 01:00:49.318
understand the results ourselves
is really at some unformalized

01:00:49.378 --> 01:00:53.258
level. You know, what is an
interesting special case? What, you

01:00:53.298 --> 01:00:56.838
know, where does this come from, you
know, this technique you're using, right?

01:00:57.578 --> 01:01:01.398
And, you know, maybe that's what that world would
look like. If we have these black boxes, we

01:01:01.438 --> 01:01:05.058
would try to tease apart and
figure out how to ask them easier

01:01:05.178 --> 01:01:08.938
questions to develop some kind
of system of understanding of

01:01:09.018 --> 01:01:10.518
how they actually got to the answer.

01:01:11.022 --> 01:01:14.742
- Yeah, so there are really two different
things that we conflate when we write a

01:01:14.802 --> 01:01:18.162
paper. One is the engineering of
the proof, and the other is the

01:01:18.202 --> 01:01:22.182
communication of that to someone else, and
they're almost at odds with one another.

01:01:22.342 --> 01:01:26.282
I had this experience-- So I taught real
analysis last fall, and you open up

01:01:26.342 --> 01:01:28.882
any real analysis textbook, and this was
my experience when I took real analysis.

01:01:28.942 --> 01:01:32.442
The first couple of lectures are what are
the real numbers? You know, we have to

01:01:32.622 --> 01:01:36.562
define them or construct them as
equivalence classes of Cauchy

01:01:36.602 --> 01:01:40.282
sequences of rationals, whatever the hell all that
means. It takes, like, two weeks for a first,

01:01:40.722 --> 01:01:44.682
semester course in real analysis to get
there, and then we have the real number line,

01:01:45.082 --> 01:01:48.762
and now we can start doing calculus,
you know, with formal epsilon and delta

01:01:48.822 --> 01:01:52.682
definitions, yada yada. And it
always struck me as bizarre that

01:01:52.722 --> 01:01:56.702
we do this because before the class, I knew
what the real numbers were. It's this number

01:01:56.762 --> 01:01:59.942
line, and then we spend these
two weeks talking about

01:02:00.542 --> 01:02:04.122
Cauchy sequences of rationals,
and then on week three, they're,

01:02:04.762 --> 01:02:06.522
they're a number line again. What
the hell was the purpose of that?

01:02:07.422 --> 01:02:11.102
And so it's because the way
things are written or had been

01:02:11.122 --> 01:02:15.082
written were very engineering. You need to construct
the real numbers before you go start using

01:02:15.102 --> 01:02:15.942
them. But the

01:02:16.882 --> 01:02:20.742
real numbers, human beings used the
real numbers long before we understood

01:02:20.942 --> 01:02:24.722
that they needed to be constructed formally.
So what I did in my class is I jumped

01:02:24.782 --> 01:02:28.422
straight into epsilon delta calculus.
We did, you know, six weeks of

01:02:28.482 --> 01:02:31.262
calculations until we got to a point where

01:02:32.162 --> 01:02:36.062
wait a second, you know, if here's a theorem,
it's true in the real numbers. There's some

01:02:36.102 --> 01:02:39.882
convergence, something called Bolzano-Weierstrass
theorem. You have a sequence of real

01:02:39.922 --> 01:02:43.762
numbers that are jumping around, but in a fixed
range. There has to be some subsequence that

01:02:43.782 --> 01:02:44.802
converges to a real number.

01:02:45.702 --> 01:02:49.482
Well, that's not true in the rationals.
Why is it true in the reals? What

01:02:49.582 --> 01:02:53.482
are the reals? So you don't start
asking that question until you hit a

01:02:53.522 --> 01:02:57.442
problem that needs to be solved.
So the way I taught the course

01:02:57.462 --> 01:03:01.262
was sort of backwards from how it needs to
be taught if it's an engineering project,

01:03:01.542 --> 01:03:04.642
'cause you can't just start talking about the real numbers
until you've constructed them, until you know what they are.

01:03:05.522 --> 01:03:09.422
Well, that's correct if what you're
doing is building up the theory. But if

01:03:09.442 --> 01:03:12.842
you're telling a story, then you absolutely
can just start with the real numbers.

01:03:12.862 --> 01:03:16.802
You have an intuition for what that is, and
until your intuition breaks, you don't need

01:03:17.202 --> 01:03:21.102
the resolution of how you actually construct
them. So I think the way that we write

01:03:21.142 --> 01:03:24.702
papers in the future is gonna be very
different, where we spend a lot more time just

01:03:24.762 --> 01:03:28.662
talking about the story of what
have I learned in this process,

01:03:28.762 --> 01:03:32.442
what are the interesting-- Uh, here, here's,
you do the standard thing here, here, and here,

01:03:32.722 --> 01:03:36.622
but then here, you're sort of stuck, and
where do you go? And here's an idea for

01:03:36.662 --> 01:03:40.482
why you go left and not right and not up
and down or whatever. And there's a Lean

01:03:41.102 --> 01:03:44.962
formalization that accompanies this story that
lets you know that the engineering actually

01:03:44.982 --> 01:03:48.862
works out. 'Cause you can't just tell stories.
If you just tell stories, you know, math

01:03:48.942 --> 01:03:52.862
is not music where you can tell

01:03:52.902 --> 01:03:56.782
a beautiful story, and it's okay that you have parallel
fifths moving somewhere, and, you know, you've broken

01:03:56.822 --> 01:03:58.502
some rule, but Bach does
it all the time, so.

01:03:58.922 --> 01:04:02.802
- So I think that's a very important point,
just to sort of emphasize the point

01:04:02.882 --> 01:04:06.842
that Alex is making. You know, there's this
view of mathematics which I don't believe

01:04:06.902 --> 01:04:10.662
in, where it's just this rising
tide of theorems. You know, how can

01:04:10.762 --> 01:04:13.562
AI be bad? It's contributing
to this rising tide.

01:04:14.422 --> 01:04:18.382
But, you know, really, mathematics is
not just about the number of theorems we

01:04:18.442 --> 01:04:22.262
have, far from it. It's also
about that story of fighting

01:04:22.322 --> 01:04:26.242
with problems, because when you fight
with a good problem, it fights back at

01:04:26.362 --> 01:04:30.122
you, and you learn something about
it. It's really about the system of

01:04:30.162 --> 01:04:34.042
beliefs and abstractions you
come up with en route to, you

01:04:34.102 --> 01:04:37.982
know, when you're fighting this problem, you
discover some more general meaning that applies

01:04:38.022 --> 01:04:38.662
more broadly.

01:04:39.922 --> 01:04:43.602
And I think it's a great opportunity to sort
of disabuse ourselves of this idea that

01:04:44.042 --> 01:04:50.262
we're just you know, axiomatizing things
until we get to the next theorem. You know,

01:04:50.402 --> 01:04:54.002
telling that story of how we actually
got to these problems, what the

01:04:54.062 --> 01:04:57.942
parallels are to other problems, is
now, at this pivotal moment, more

01:04:57.962 --> 01:04:59.282
important than it ever has been.

01:04:59.822 --> 01:05:03.802
- Yeah, so to get all the way back to your question
about, you know, is it a matter of scale, well,

01:05:04.602 --> 01:05:08.582
Yes, it's just a matter of
scale, but exactly to Ankur's

01:05:08.622 --> 01:05:12.582
point, the way that we think about mathematics
is about stories, and it might be that

01:05:12.622 --> 01:05:16.542
we don't understand the story of this. You
can follow a proof line by line and not

01:05:16.582 --> 01:05:19.422
understand what's really going on.

01:05:20.702 --> 01:05:24.582
You might say, and you just ask some other
LLM to explain to you the story if it

01:05:24.622 --> 01:05:28.442
can come up with such an
explanation. But that's what we

01:05:28.482 --> 01:05:30.202
want. We want stories.
We want understanding.

01:05:30.922 --> 01:05:36.382
- When you fight with a
problem, it fights back.

01:05:37.382 --> 01:05:41.202
I think there's a deep truth to
that about all the great pursuits

01:05:41.362 --> 01:05:45.262
that humanity has taken
on. And on that topic,

01:05:45.422 --> 01:05:49.142
I would like to talk to you about
some of the greatest fights of all

01:05:49.202 --> 01:05:52.842
time with some of the hardest problems
in mathematics that have fallen. Um,

01:05:53.442 --> 01:06:00.062
but first, we'll have to break if it's okay.
So Alex, you're a number theorist. Um,

01:06:01.562 --> 01:06:05.462
maybe it's fair to say the central problem
in your field is the Riemann hypothesis,

01:06:05.782 --> 01:06:10.902
and you showed a lot of excitement
on the aforementioned Twitter/X

01:06:11.722 --> 01:06:17.502
about the quasi-RH, the quasi-Riemann
hypothesis proof. Can you explain that?

01:06:18.202 --> 01:06:21.882
Can you explain what the Riemann
hypothesis is, and can you explain

01:06:22.562 --> 01:06:24.902
the nature of your excitement about it?

01:06:25.202 --> 01:06:28.822
- Sure. There's sort of a one-minute
version of the Riemann hypothesis-

01:06:28.862 --> 01:06:32.042
... or a five-minute version
or a thirty-hour version.

01:06:32.242 --> 01:06:34.362
I don't know how far you
wanna, you wanna go.

01:06:34.382 --> 01:06:36.302
- Let's start pulling at that string.

01:06:36.702 --> 01:06:40.122
- So we know what the prime numbers are. People are
interested in the distribution of the prime numbers,

01:06:40.902 --> 01:06:44.862
and what Riemann discovered is that
there's a function, this is totally

01:06:44.902 --> 01:06:48.762
crazy, that there's a function that
takes complex numbers as inputs and

01:06:48.802 --> 01:06:52.562
has complex numbers as
outputs, and that function

01:06:52.722 --> 01:06:57.262
governs the behavior of the prime numbers.
It's called the Riemann zeta function,

01:06:58.482 --> 01:07:02.262
and by the zeros of the zeta
function, we mean complex inputs

01:07:02.802 --> 01:07:06.522
where the output is the number
zero in the complex plane, so the,

01:07:06.782 --> 01:07:08.022
the origin in the complex plane.

01:07:08.982 --> 01:07:12.782
And the question is, where do these, where
are these complex zeros? What inputs, what

01:07:12.822 --> 01:07:16.522
complex number inputs give you a
complex number output of zero when

01:07:16.542 --> 01:07:18.082
you apply this zeta function?

01:07:19.210 --> 01:07:22.790
Why in the world should that-- What, complex
numbers? Are you-- What are you talking about?

01:07:23.250 --> 01:07:26.730
We have these discrete numbers that
aren't divisible by anything else

01:07:27.290 --> 01:07:30.950
sitting in the natural numbers on the
number line, and now you wanna talk about

01:07:31.310 --> 01:07:34.950
functions from a complex space
to a, to a complex space. Um,

01:07:35.330 --> 01:07:39.270
that was the great breakthrough. The great
breakthrough was to figure out how to do

01:07:39.350 --> 01:07:41.650
signal processing on the prime numbers.

01:07:42.810 --> 01:07:46.430
If you think about, you know,
the reason people can hear

01:07:46.810 --> 01:07:50.530
you, your podcast is that
my voice box is producing

01:07:50.770 --> 01:07:54.370
pressure waves of sound.
That's the natural space.

01:07:55.090 --> 01:07:59.050
Then that goes into the microphone. The
microphone analyzes it, does the Fourier analysis

01:07:59.130 --> 01:08:02.790
on it, converts it into
amplitudes and frequencies,

01:08:03.290 --> 01:08:06.450
and it's just a bunch of numbers, stores those
numbers or sends those numbers, whatever.

01:08:06.590 --> 01:08:10.210
The speaker then takes those numbers
and reconstructs back out what

01:08:10.570 --> 01:08:11.990
sounds to people like my voice.

01:08:13.190 --> 01:08:17.170
The same thing is going on-- That's what the
zeta function does for the prime numbers.

01:08:17.550 --> 01:08:21.030
It takes the prime numbers, you can think of
the prime numbers as particles of air molecules

01:08:21.790 --> 01:08:26.670
wiggling around, and it does a
signal processing analysis on it

01:08:28.090 --> 01:08:30.130
that can recover the
same prime numbers back.

01:08:31.450 --> 01:08:35.230
And the frequencies and the
amplitudes correspond to the

01:08:35.310 --> 01:08:38.410
zeros of the zeta function. The zeta
function has that information in it.

01:08:39.750 --> 01:08:43.650
And the Riemann hypothesis,
so the amplitudes

01:08:43.730 --> 01:08:47.770
of all of these, you can
call them Riemann waves,

01:08:48.650 --> 01:08:51.730
and the superposition of those waves
recovers the distribution of the primes.

01:08:52.650 --> 01:08:56.590
And all of those amplitudes
are supposed to be exactly

01:08:56.630 --> 01:09:00.070
the same. Like, the
orchestra that plays the,

01:09:00.590 --> 01:09:04.350
the music of the prime numbers has
every single instrument sounding at

01:09:04.390 --> 01:09:08.110
exactly the same amplitude. That's
what the Riemann hypothesis predicts.

01:09:09.010 --> 01:09:12.770
- So can you-- Actually, we know
what the prime numbers are, but

01:09:13.790 --> 01:09:17.670
maybe going into the space of
philosophy and the romantic

01:09:17.730 --> 01:09:21.490
notions of aesthetics why
are they so interesting

01:09:21.910 --> 01:09:26.930
and important and central and beautiful
at the core of mathematics? And,

01:09:28.090 --> 01:09:31.870
connected to why the Riemann
hypothesis is exciting is why is it so

01:09:32.030 --> 01:09:36.610
difficult to figure out if and what
the pattern is in the prime numbers?

01:09:37.070 --> 01:09:41.030
- Let me start with your second question
first. Why something is difficult

01:09:41.250 --> 01:09:44.690
I only know how to answer once
I know what the answer is.

01:09:45.090 --> 01:09:45.750
- Once it's made simple.

01:09:45.750 --> 01:09:46.630
- Right? Maybe it's not difficult.

01:09:46.770 --> 01:09:47.050
- Okay.

01:09:47.190 --> 01:09:50.750
- Maybe it's not as difficult as we thought it
was. I did not think I would see a proof of the

01:09:50.790 --> 01:09:56.150
quasi-Riemann hypothesis in my lifetime.
So the fact that I saw it on Tuesday,

01:09:56.950 --> 01:09:58.710
was extraordinarily shocking.

01:09:59.370 --> 01:10:02.990
- What did you do the moment
you saw it, like, in 30

01:10:03.050 --> 01:10:06.870
seconds after? Like, what was
your-- Do you remember, like, what-

01:10:07.330 --> 01:10:11.310
- Yeah, I remember where I was- ...
the second I saw the drop. So we

01:10:11.330 --> 01:10:14.570
were at this National
Museum of Mathematics Gala-

01:10:14.570 --> 01:10:18.290
... in New York City. And so I had,
you know, a whiskey in one hand, and,

01:10:18.890 --> 01:10:22.810
I kept refreshing. I was talking to
people, socializing, but I also kept

01:10:22.850 --> 01:10:27.030
refreshing 'cause I knew it was dropping
around six o'clock Eastern Time.

01:10:27.250 --> 01:10:31.230
And then the drop hit and,
in fact, I was talking to

01:10:31.370 --> 01:10:35.170
there was a Quanta reporter there and we
were just chatting, "Oh, yeah, they're gonna

01:10:35.210 --> 01:10:39.010
drop these, you know, 300, 400,"
we didn't know how many, 700 it

01:10:39.050 --> 01:10:42.790
turns out problems. And I was like,
"Oh, wow, that's really cool. It

01:10:42.850 --> 01:10:46.810
dropped." So I'm looking at my phone,
and, like, the second thing I see is

01:10:46.870 --> 01:10:50.330
quasi-RH. And I'm like, "What do you mean,

01:10:50.470 --> 01:10:54.450
quasi-RH? You don't mean, like... For
what?" And then I look, and it's,

01:10:55.130 --> 01:10:58.890
it's the zeta function, and it's all Dirichlet
functions, so they've solved no zero, zeros.

01:10:59.690 --> 01:11:07.390
And I'm like, that can't, that can't
be what's in this drop. That just-- I,

01:11:08.290 --> 01:11:11.270
I could not compute- ... in
that moment what I was seeing.

01:11:11.330 --> 01:11:13.530
- That speed of progress in
that little time, yeah.

01:11:14.070 --> 01:11:20.190
- It was so far outside my
model of what was possible.

01:11:20.730 --> 01:11:24.590
And this is-- It's been a month
of things happening way outside

01:11:24.690 --> 01:11:28.530
of my model of what was possible. The
Fermat's Last Theorem formalization,

01:11:29.170 --> 01:11:32.930
I was sure we were three years away,
at least, from being able to do

01:11:32.970 --> 01:11:33.610
something like that.

01:11:33.770 --> 01:11:37.630
- So, maybe you can elaborate on that.
It was in Lean, it was formalized

01:11:37.690 --> 01:11:41.050
as... How big was the Fermat's
Last Theorem in Lean?

01:11:41.210 --> 01:11:42.110
- Like, 13 million lines.

01:11:42.170 --> 01:11:43.430
- 13 million lines. Okay.

01:11:43.630 --> 01:11:47.570
- So I just-- Two days before, I gave a colloquium
in the Princeton Math Department where

01:11:47.610 --> 01:11:51.150
I explained in painstaking detail what
Lean is, what Mathlib is, but also

01:11:51.670 --> 01:11:55.490
that AI systems today, you know,
there's this Mathlib library

01:11:55.830 --> 01:11:59.790
where everything is kind of perfectly done,
exactly the right levels of generality, enough--

01:11:59.890 --> 01:12:03.770
There's no-- There's not supposed
to be any code that's reusing

01:12:04.170 --> 01:12:08.070
code that's reproving theorems somewhere
else. Everything is the most general,

01:12:08.110 --> 01:12:12.030
the most abstract, and what AI systems are
capable of doing is building, like, one

01:12:12.070 --> 01:12:15.970
or two layers on top of that. But they're,
what they're doing is jerry-rigging. They have

01:12:16.030 --> 01:12:19.810
not been trained yet, I'm not
saying they can't do it, but

01:12:20.050 --> 01:12:23.810
they're not good at it right now, at
what I call canonization, which is

01:12:23.870 --> 01:12:27.790
taking kind of sloppy code, refactoring
it, figuring out exactly where in

01:12:27.810 --> 01:12:31.650
the library it belongs, what's the canonical
version of this statement, of this

01:12:31.730 --> 01:12:35.690
lemma, and then everything, every
proof should just be a combination

01:12:35.730 --> 01:12:39.550
of you apply this lemma from over here, you
apply that lemma from over there, put all these

01:12:39.570 --> 01:12:43.390
things together, there's your theorem. And then it's
really clean, it's really beautiful, very elegant.

01:12:43.690 --> 01:12:49.650
That's what Mathlib is going for. And
what I said in my colloquium is, where we

01:12:49.890 --> 01:12:53.630
are today is that you can get entire textbooks
formalized as long as they're in the Mathlib

01:12:53.690 --> 01:12:54.330
halo.

01:12:54.370 --> 01:12:58.290
As long as they're, like, one, maybe two steps
removed from things that have already been

01:12:58.330 --> 01:13:02.270
done in Mathlib. But when you start, and this
had been my practice, my experience had been,

01:13:02.730 --> 01:13:06.710
when you start asking it to do things that are,
you know, three or four steps removed from,

01:13:07.270 --> 01:13:11.150
the library, the way that it
came up with a definition of

01:13:11.210 --> 01:13:15.110
this object and the way that it came up with some other
definition of the same object, 'cause it was doing it

01:13:15.150 --> 01:13:18.850
for two different purposes, then they
clash. You can't stitch these things back

01:13:18.890 --> 01:13:22.910
together. Like, you really need
the library, Mathlib, to grow-

01:13:22.910 --> 01:13:25.130
... in order to get to
a place where you can

01:13:26.186 --> 01:13:29.666
do research manuscripts, you
know that are, that are really

01:13:30.086 --> 01:13:33.446
using, synthesizing tons of
different areas of mathematics.

01:13:33.526 --> 01:13:37.306
- Which is why you would expect Fermat's
last theorem to take-- because it's so far

01:13:37.326 --> 01:13:40.326
outside of the halo of Mathlib, it
would take quite a bit of time.

01:13:40.566 --> 01:13:44.186
- Exactly right. So Kevin
Buzzard had a million dollar

01:13:44.266 --> 01:13:48.206
grant to spend the next five years, or I th-
maybe he's already a year or two into that

01:13:48.246 --> 01:13:52.206
grant, to reduce the formalization
of Fermat's last theorem

01:13:52.266 --> 01:13:53.726
to things that were known in the 1980s.

01:13:54.846 --> 01:13:58.566
So he wanted to do that last step,
the, those seven years of Wiles' work,

01:13:58.946 --> 01:14:02.846
and Wiles Taylor, to finally
finish you know, Fermat's

01:14:02.866 --> 01:14:06.666
last theorem, but built on top of things
that we knew would be extraordinarily

01:14:06.706 --> 01:14:10.606
difficult. He wasn't gonna do Langlands' Tunnell, he wasn't
gonna do Mazur. There were, there were all kinds of things

01:14:10.646 --> 01:14:14.406
that were expected to be even much more
difficult than what these five years would

01:14:14.466 --> 01:14:18.246
take. And everything I said
in that colloquium was wrong.

01:14:19.666 --> 01:14:23.646
And I think my colloquium was on a Wednesday. On
