01:24:41.022 --> 01:24:44.822
so what Riemann showed is that
remember we were talking about this

01:24:44.862 --> 01:24:48.362
function that has complex inputs and has
these outputs, and we're worried about

01:24:48.742 --> 01:24:52.142
when the output is zero. So he showed that
there's what's called the critical strip,

01:24:52.622 --> 01:24:56.582
which is a vertical infinite strip
in the complex plane, where the

01:24:56.642 --> 01:25:00.422
real part is-- So you can think about
the X coordinate lies between zero and

01:25:00.522 --> 01:25:04.142
one, and the imaginary part, the Y coordinate,
can go arbitrarily high or arbitrarily low.

01:25:04.862 --> 01:25:08.662
And the hypothesis is that the zeros
all lie right at the dead center of

01:25:08.722 --> 01:25:12.622
that strip, where the real part is a
half. That's exactly what Ankur was just

01:25:12.662 --> 01:25:15.662
saying. And what we knew until Tuesday

01:25:16.302 --> 01:25:19.782
was region where there are no
zeros of the zeta function,

01:25:20.342 --> 01:25:24.222
that's like getting thinner and thinner
the higher up you go. It goes towards

01:25:24.262 --> 01:25:28.062
the one line. The one line is something called the
prime number theorem, which tells you the basic

01:25:28.102 --> 01:25:31.902
distribution of the prime numbers is
equivalent to the fact that there are no

01:25:31.982 --> 01:25:35.462
zeros of this function on the
line real part of S equals one.

01:25:36.022 --> 01:25:40.018
So if the X coordinate is one- As you go up
and down that line, you will never find a

01:25:40.078 --> 01:25:43.898
value where when you apply the
zeta function, it hits zero.

01:25:44.898 --> 01:25:48.878
Okay? So we have this zero-free region
that was getting thinner and thinner the

01:25:48.918 --> 01:25:49.618
higher up you go,

01:25:50.738 --> 01:25:54.718
and that gave us the best known
distribution on the prime numbers, the best

01:25:54.778 --> 01:25:58.658
error term in the prime number theorem, which I
can go into if you-- You tell me how much math you

01:25:58.678 --> 01:25:59.818
wanna talk about.

01:25:59.838 --> 01:26:00.118
