01:29:57.618 --> 01:30:03.158
fluid dynamics. So going from math
to applied math to engineering, the

01:30:03.198 --> 01:30:09.378
full thing. So maybe can you talk about
Navier-Stokes, these set of equations that

01:30:09.478 --> 01:30:13.378
explain fluids, why it's such a
big deal, why it's beautiful?

01:30:13.898 --> 01:30:17.858
Similar as we talked about primes,
why is it so hard, and why is

01:30:17.878 --> 01:30:21.918
it such a big deal that
Navier-Stokes was proven?

01:30:22.518 --> 01:30:25.458
- So fundamentally, the
Navier-Stokes equations

01:30:26.278 --> 01:30:30.058
is just a way of stating the
conservation of momentum

01:30:30.538 --> 01:30:33.958
for fluids, for objects that
can move kind of freely, like

01:30:34.018 --> 01:30:37.658
gases and liquids. So
we know F equals ma is

01:30:37.698 --> 01:30:41.538
conservation of momentum, you
know, classical Newtonian physics.

01:30:41.758 --> 01:30:45.358
The same basic principle applies
to the gas in this room, to

01:30:45.858 --> 01:30:49.678
water flowing, and it's
true in any volume. So it's

01:30:49.738 --> 01:30:53.498
true in little volumes, big
volumes, kind of imaginary volumes.

01:30:54.158 --> 01:30:57.638
And if you write down that
conservation of momentum in a volume,

01:30:58.078 --> 01:31:01.938
and then you kind of take the limit
as that volume becomes arbitrary

01:31:02.018 --> 01:31:05.898
and small, you get these Navier-Stokes
equations. It's a differential equation,

01:31:05.958 --> 01:31:09.758
a partial differential equation
that describes how the

01:31:09.798 --> 01:31:13.498
velocity field of a fluid
changes in space and time,

01:31:14.158 --> 01:31:17.998
and also the pressure related to
the forces acting on that fluid.

01:31:18.278 --> 01:31:22.258
- Can you give intuition about
why fluid, given that F equals

01:31:22.418 --> 01:31:28.518
MA is a pretty simple equation, why
fluids are such complicated systems?

01:31:28.938 --> 01:31:32.458
- F equals MA is simple to
write down, but it can get

01:31:32.518 --> 01:31:36.278
complicated really, really quickly. So
if I have, like, a single pendulum, it's

01:31:36.318 --> 01:31:40.158
really easy to write down the equations for that
single pendulum. If I put, you know, another

01:31:40.198 --> 01:31:43.838
pendulum on the end of it, now I have a
more complicated equation, and it gives

01:31:43.878 --> 01:31:49.282
chaos. Fluids is, you
know, in a sense, if I

01:31:49.302 --> 01:31:53.222
try to model the air in this room, I
could chop the room up into a billion

01:31:53.262 --> 01:31:57.062
little boxes and write down a little
differential equation for how the

01:31:57.302 --> 01:32:01.102
momentum works in all of those billion
little boxes, but now I have a

01:32:01.142 --> 01:32:04.842
really complicated system
of equations. And so it's

01:32:04.922 --> 01:32:08.762
simple to write down, it's simple to
conceptually understand what the equation is

01:32:08.822 --> 01:32:10.182
saying and representing,

01:32:11.022 --> 01:32:15.002
But actually solving it and
understanding how the fluid actually

01:32:15.042 --> 01:32:18.242
moves in any kind of practical
sense is very, very complicated.

01:32:18.582 --> 01:32:22.342
- I read something like, you
know, like one space where,

01:32:22.522 --> 01:32:27.922
