what are the different components of the Navier-Stokes
equation? Can you-- if you-- can you dig in?

01:40:11.274 --> 01:40:14.974
Like, what does it take to describe fluid?

01:40:16.034 --> 01:40:23.374
- Yeah. So very, very basic a fluid moves,
and it moves differently in different

01:40:23.494 --> 01:40:26.954
places and at different
points in time. And so

01:40:27.794 --> 01:40:31.714
the kind of basic building block
of a fluid is the velocity field.

01:40:32.114 --> 01:40:35.854
So at every point in space, there is a
velocity vector that tells you how that,

01:40:36.134 --> 01:40:40.034
that fluid is moving. You know, maybe
it's really fast here and slow here,

01:40:40.134 --> 01:40:47.194
and it creates a vortex. Um, and it gets
more interesting when the fluid velocity

01:40:47.334 --> 01:40:54.714
changes a lot over space. So like a
tornado is a very rapidly changing

01:40:54.854 --> 01:40:58.554
fluid velocity field in
both space and time.

01:40:59.434 --> 01:41:02.654
There's pressures that are involved.
So again, like the core of a

01:41:03.134 --> 01:41:07.034
tornado is low pressure.
So pressures are, um,

01:41:07.974 --> 01:41:11.554
kind of forcing this velocity
field and causing it to

01:41:11.614 --> 01:41:15.394
move in the way it does.
That's the building block is a

01:41:15.434 --> 01:41:21.734
velocity field and its pressures. And then
the equations tell you how that velocity

01:41:21.934 --> 01:41:25.074
changes in time and in space according to

01:41:25.834 --> 01:41:29.414
kind of its own momentum. So fluid
has momentum to carry itself.

01:41:29.514 --> 01:41:33.234
That's, you know, gusts
of wind. It has internal,

01:41:33.534 --> 01:41:37.514
friction and resistance. That's
viscosity. So honey has more internal

01:41:38.014 --> 01:41:41.874
resistance than water, has more
internal resistance than air. Um,

01:41:41.974 --> 01:41:45.954
and then all of the external
forces like heating and pressure

01:41:45.994 --> 01:41:49.594
and gravity and things like that. So
each of those can be quantified as a

01:41:49.654 --> 01:41:52.554
term in this, this larger
Navier-Stokes equation.

01:41:52.974 --> 01:41:56.774
- So speaking of external forces, can
you describe the math side of this,

01:41:57.294 --> 01:41:59.074
of the Navier-Stokes with the blow up?

01:41:59.874 --> 01:42:03.814
What was the formulation of the problem,
and what was actually proven, and why

01:42:03.854 --> 01:42:04.514
was it so hard?

01:42:05.394 --> 01:42:09.674
- If you don't have that internal
friction, that viscosity,

01:42:10.954 --> 01:42:14.934
we have a strong suspicion that
the equations can and will blow

01:42:14.994 --> 01:42:18.694
up under certain conditions. We have
other examples, kind of simpler

01:42:18.734 --> 01:42:25.394
problems that are like Navier-Stokes, that
do blow up. So the simplest one is just a

01:42:25.454 --> 01:42:28.334
simple ordinary differential equation, if
you don't mind me taking a little tangent.

01:42:28.374 --> 01:42:28.694
- Let's go.

01:42:29.234 --> 01:42:32.854
- So X dot equals X squared.
X is the state of my

01:42:32.914 --> 01:42:36.674
system. X dot just means the
rate of change of X in time.

01:42:37.254 --> 01:42:41.214
And if that rate of change of
X is growing proportional to

01:42:41.494 --> 01:42:45.254
X squared, that differential
equation does blow up in finite

01:42:45.294 --> 01:42:48.474
time. So it just takes a
quadratic nonlinearity. Um,

01:42:49.394 --> 01:42:53.374
think about if I have, you know,
X dot equals X. This is-- like

01:42:53.534 --> 01:42:56.674
X could be the population of
bunnies, and we know bunnies like to

01:42:57.314 --> 01:43:01.234
populate. X dot equals X grows
exponentially. You know,

01:43:01.354 --> 01:43:05.214
the state of my bunny population in
time will grow exponentially. It'll

01:43:05.254 --> 01:43:07.474
get bigger and bigger and bigger.
It'll double and double and double.

01:43:08.774 --> 01:43:12.234
But it won't reach infinity
until infinite time has passed.

01:43:12.634 --> 01:43:16.534
That's just a normal exponential growth.
That's not finite time blow up. That's X

01:43:16.574 --> 01:43:23.034
dot equals X. But if I take X dot equals
X squared, that blows up in finite time.

01:43:24.334 --> 01:43:28.194
So that quadratic nonlinearity, that
quadratic kind of feedback is enough to

01:43:28.234 --> 01:43:32.194
cause this equation to blow up. And you
can, like-- if you write this down you

01:43:32.234 --> 01:43:36.134
know, you can solve this using kind of
classic basic calculus techniques, and you

01:43:36.154 --> 01:43:40.014
get this finite time blow up. And so there's
a progression of partial differential

01:43:40.054 --> 01:43:43.914
equations that have the same behavior where
a quadratic nonlinearity-- And that's really

01:43:43.934 --> 01:43:46.594
interesting is the
Navier-Stokes equations,

01:43:47.974 --> 01:43:51.674
one of the terms, the con-- uh, the convective
term that tells how the fluid kind of moves

01:43:51.714 --> 01:43:55.474
itself with its own momentum,
is a quadratic nonlinearity,

01:43:55.594 --> 01:44:00.474
just like X dot equals X squared.
It's kind of this self-motion. Um-

01:44:02.082 --> 01:44:05.842
Without diffusion, without viscosity, we

01:44:05.922 --> 01:44:09.841
think that that should probably
blow up to infinity in finite

01:44:09.882 --> 01:44:13.342
time, have this finite time
blow up. But we don't know if

01:44:13.622 --> 01:44:17.542
viscosity, if the kind of, you know,
internal friction and resistance like honey

01:44:17.582 --> 01:44:22.702
has, always damps out those blowups
before they can happen. My intuition as

01:44:22.742 --> 01:44:26.702
an engineer is of course they do.
Like, if I have a flow where it's,

01:44:27.342 --> 01:44:30.782
you know, accelerating,
you know, to infinity,

01:44:31.342 --> 01:44:35.242
that should immediately diffuse into
its neighbors. It should create this,

01:44:35.302 --> 01:44:38.982
like, diffusion into its, its
neighboring fluid and kind of tamp down

01:44:39.622 --> 01:44:43.222
that, that growth. And so I think
the math problem is essentially

01:44:44.182 --> 01:44:48.022
are there any edge cases, any initial
configurations of the fluid where you

01:44:48.102 --> 01:44:51.482
perfectly orchestrate this
thing so that it could actually

01:44:51.542 --> 01:44:53.802
accelerate to infinity in finite time?

01:44:54.602 --> 01:44:57.942
And then there's two kind of
technical versions of this. One is

01:44:58.722 --> 01:45:02.522
with no external forcing, and
one is with smooth external

01:45:02.562 --> 01:45:06.442
forcing. Smooth being important
because if the external forcing has

01:45:06.482 --> 01:45:08.562
singularities, then it's kind of trivial.

01:45:09.122 --> 01:45:12.922
Um, like if I force it with a tornado, you
know, an infinitely tight tornado, then of

01:45:12.962 --> 01:45:13.882
course it's gonna blow up.

01:45:14.182 --> 01:45:18.842
- The problem as stated has
smooth external forcing.

01:45:19.262 --> 01:45:20.862
- There's four versions of the-

01:45:20.902 --> 01:45:24.722
... on, on the Clay, yeah, website. One
of them being the, the forced version.

01:45:24.742 --> 01:45:25.882
- And that's the one that was proven.

01:45:26.242 --> 01:45:27.142
- And that's the one that was proven.

01:45:27.762 --> 01:45:31.702
- Exactly what was proven and what are
some interesting things about the proof

01:45:31.742 --> 01:45:32.302
that you saw?

01:45:33.222 --> 01:45:38.522
- Yeah. So what is proven is
that there is this way of

01:45:38.622 --> 01:45:42.562
orchestrating a smooth kind
of external forcing that

01:45:42.642 --> 01:45:48.262
perfectly cancels out various terms
in the equation, so that you get this

01:45:49.142 --> 01:45:52.282
vortex. This can only happen in three
dimensions. This is really important, is that-

01:45:52.322 --> 01:45:56.122
- We should probably say that we're, we've been talking
about three dimensions. There's a big difference

01:45:56.162 --> 01:45:57.262
between 3D and 2D.

01:45:57.682 --> 01:46:01.662
- Yeah. So I mean, when we talk about a
fluid, we're, we usually imagine it in 3D,

01:46:01.722 --> 01:46:05.202
but there are these kind of analogies
or, or simplified 2D flows.

01:46:05.542 --> 01:46:08.702
Weather on Earth is pretty well
