01:58:14.166 --> 01:58:17.666
And, you know, to me, what's really exciting
is the nexus of a lot of these different

01:58:17.706 --> 01:58:18.486
perspectives.

01:58:19.366 --> 01:58:23.146
When you were describing it, it sounded
to me like there are very interesting

01:58:23.206 --> 01:58:24.866
computational problems under the hood,

01:58:25.786 --> 01:58:29.486
and that one can kind of get a dual
perspective on these things by thinking

01:58:29.586 --> 01:58:33.186
about how that computation
imposes limits on

01:58:33.666 --> 01:58:35.766
what a good coordinate
system ought to look like.

01:58:36.246 --> 01:58:40.086
- So I think that's a really nice
way of framing the problem. Uh,

01:58:41.026 --> 01:58:48.166
and I would take two extreme examples. So
Earth's weather and climate systems are

01:58:48.306 --> 01:58:52.266
actually just complex.
They-- all the scales do

01:58:52.326 --> 01:58:56.086
matter. The weather in this
zip code matters to me.

01:58:56.486 --> 01:59:00.366
Um, you know, everything matters.
All the variables matter. And you're

01:59:00.406 --> 01:59:04.326
right. Like, if I over-reduce
that system to, you know, five

01:59:04.406 --> 01:59:08.226
numbers, then I lose the descriptive
capability of what I actually care

01:59:08.286 --> 01:59:11.846
about. That's one extreme.
The other extreme,

01:59:12.646 --> 01:59:17.306
let's think about how, you know, a moth
flies or a butterfly or a fruit fly.

01:59:18.646 --> 01:59:23.226
They are proof by existence
that you can interact with an

01:59:23.266 --> 01:59:30.366
extremely complicated turbulent flow
and do very advanced engineering

01:59:30.426 --> 01:59:33.906
control. They can control
their forces and moments in

01:59:34.006 --> 01:59:37.846
gusts, in rain. They can
live very rich lives, and

01:59:38.146 --> 01:59:42.086
they presumably don't have a PhD
in fluid dynamics- ... or know the

01:59:42.126 --> 01:59:45.906
Navier-Stokes. They're probably not
running supercomputer simulations.

01:59:46.586 --> 01:59:50.526
There might be five or six numbers
that actually matter to their

01:59:50.586 --> 01:59:54.126
daily life of maintaining a
leading-edge vortex and, you know,

01:59:54.546 --> 01:59:58.326
stalling their wings to perch and
to land. And those are kind of two

01:59:58.406 --> 02:00:01.866
extreme examples, and I really want
to be able to handle both of those.

02:00:02.646 --> 02:00:07.186
We want to be able to handle the full
complexity of truly complex systems,

02:00:08.066 --> 02:00:14.846
and we want to be able to distill down
the absolute bare minimum essentials for

02:00:15.146 --> 02:00:18.686
fast decisions in those
kind of insect extreme

02:00:18.766 --> 02:00:25.146
cases. And biological flyers
still dramatically outperform,

02:00:26.026 --> 02:00:27.126
you know, human-designed,

02:00:27.966 --> 02:00:31.286
fluid systems in many regimes, so
we still have a lot to learn about,

02:00:31.926 --> 02:00:33.586
about these simple systems.

02:00:33.606 --> 02:00:36.066
- So maybe we're, like, veering
into research now, but, you know-

02:00:36.066 --> 02:00:39.986
... to me, it's always amazing how
nature not only solves very hard

02:00:40.046 --> 02:00:43.406
optimization problems, it seems
to find very robust solutions.

02:00:44.306 --> 02:00:47.726
And I'm always struck by the fact that, you
know, when we model it, we tend to model it

02:00:48.286 --> 02:00:52.226
assuming we have full knowledge, you know,
about what the boundary and initial conditions

02:00:52.326 --> 02:00:56.286
are for things like Navier-Stokes.
But when a bird flies, you know,

02:00:56.906 --> 02:00:57.986
it doesn't know all of that.

02:00:58.966 --> 02:01:02.726
And to some extent, you know, modeling
things with uncertainty is really how you

02:01:03.326 --> 02:01:07.246
reframe the optimization
problem to get very, you know,

02:01:07.366 --> 02:01:11.066
rich and robust solutions. And
it's kind of amazing to me how,

02:01:11.386 --> 02:01:15.246
A lot of everyday examples sort of
fit into this. I remember, you know,

02:01:15.306 --> 02:01:18.766
I'm a big baseball fan, and I
remember reading a math paper about

02:01:18.806 --> 02:01:22.586
baseball that when you've got an outfielder,
you know, once the ball has been

02:01:22.646 --> 02:01:26.186
hit, a lot of stuff is known.
You know, the velocity, the

02:01:26.226 --> 02:01:30.046
trajectory. You would think the outfielder
would just take a straight-line

02:01:30.106 --> 02:01:32.166
path to where they
expected the ball to be.

02:01:32.986 --> 02:01:36.926
But when you observe outfielders, they don't
take straight-line paths. They take a slight

02:01:37.006 --> 02:01:40.686
curve to it. So why is that?
You know, at least one

02:01:40.746 --> 02:01:44.446
way to wrap your head around it is that
that's actually a very robust strategy,

02:01:45.026 --> 02:01:48.466
'cause when you're taking a curved
path, you can continually reevaluate-

02:01:48.466 --> 02:01:52.426
... the trajectory of the ball and
then adjust as needed. So there

02:01:52.426 --> 02:01:56.406
are very interesting solutions
that nature finds, but it's not

02:01:56.486 --> 02:02:00.306
always obvious what is the right
formulation for the optimization

02:02:00.386 --> 02:02:01.806
problem in the first place,

02:02:02.706 --> 02:02:06.146
and sometimes modeling it in the
case of uncertainty so that you can

02:02:07.026 --> 02:02:11.006
design solutions that are robust to the fact
that you don't have full system knowledge

02:02:11.046 --> 02:02:13.726
seems to be an important
piece of the picture.

02:02:14.546 --> 02:02:16.946
- So there's a fun aside
