02:04:35.046 --> 02:04:37.086
If it's okay, a quick bathroom break.

02:04:37.346 --> 02:04:37.926
- Yeah. Sounds good.

02:04:38.006 --> 02:04:43.186
- Cool. Ankur, one of my favorite
topics is theoretical computer

02:04:43.286 --> 02:04:46.846
science. You're one of the world
experts in many of its problems. So

02:04:47.466 --> 02:04:50.686
I'd love to ask you about one of
the big problems that's been solved

02:04:51.886 --> 02:04:55.606
in this OpenAI drop, which is
the unique games conjecture.

02:04:56.166 --> 02:04:59.786
Can you give people an intuition about
what this problem is, why it's a big deal,

02:05:00.386 --> 02:05:03.926
and what are some aspects of the proof
that are interesting? Yes, I'd love to.

02:05:04.406 --> 02:05:07.786
So let me give you a little bit of a
background. So let's start off with

02:05:07.946 --> 02:05:13.926
complexity theory 101. So one of
the most important problems in

02:05:14.226 --> 02:05:18.106
theoretical computer science is the
satisfiability problem. So the thing to keep in

02:05:18.146 --> 02:05:21.986
your mind is imagine you've
got variables X one to XN,

02:05:22.606 --> 02:05:25.886
and they can take on either
true or false values.

02:05:26.566 --> 02:05:30.526
And what I'm gonna do is I'm gonna
create a big formula out of them, and

02:05:30.586 --> 02:05:33.466
usually, the way we do this
is with an and of ors.

02:05:34.406 --> 02:05:38.346
So I start off with a clause
that's, you know, an or, X one or

02:05:38.506 --> 02:05:40.626
not X three or X seven.

02:05:41.666 --> 02:05:45.586
So depending on my assignment, that
clause might be satisfied as long as at

02:05:45.626 --> 02:05:47.426
least one of those things is true.

02:05:48.346 --> 02:05:51.526
And then I take the and of many of
these different clauses together.

02:05:52.426 --> 02:05:56.246
And the question is, if I give you that
formula, called a conjunctive normal form

02:05:56.506 --> 02:06:01.826
formula, you know, can you find a
satisfying assignment? So can you find

02:06:01.866 --> 02:06:05.426
an assignment of X one, X two, up to XN,

02:06:06.066 --> 02:06:10.026
setting them either to be equal
to true or false, so that all of

02:06:10.086 --> 02:06:15.526
those or clauses are on, so that the
entire formula evaluates to true?

02:06:16.766 --> 02:06:20.506
So this is the canonical example
of a problem that's easy to

02:06:20.666 --> 02:06:25.425
check someone else's answer, but it might
be hard to find an answer yourself.

02:06:26.386 --> 02:06:30.166
So if you told me that it's satisfiable,
all I would do is I'd ask you,

02:06:30.526 --> 02:06:34.386
"Okay, give me the satisfying assignment."
You tell me what X one is set

02:06:34.466 --> 02:06:38.186
to, what X two is set to, and
I just go clause by clause and

02:06:38.266 --> 02:06:41.646
check to make sure that you
really have satisfied everything.

02:06:43.046 --> 02:06:46.986
Now, the, you know, the central problem
in theoretical computer science is the

02:06:47.046 --> 02:06:50.726
P versus NP problem. So are
problems that are easy to

02:06:50.846 --> 02:06:54.706
check, is it easy to find
their solutions to? And the

02:06:54.766 --> 02:06:58.626
presumption in our field is
that no, it's not easy, and

02:06:58.666 --> 02:07:02.386
that's the basis you know, of a
lot of things like the, you know,

02:07:02.486 --> 02:07:04.746
cryptography and e-commerce
and what have you.

02:07:06.246 --> 02:07:10.206
Now, really, the starting point for getting to
something like the unique games conjecture is

02:07:10.246 --> 02:07:14.086
something called the PCP
theorem. So this was a landmark

02:07:14.126 --> 02:07:17.766
result proved in the nineties.
It actually was such a big

02:07:17.826 --> 02:07:20.446
deal that it made the front
page of The New York Times.

02:07:21.366 --> 02:07:24.886
And the crux of it is really
this idea that it's even

02:07:25.046 --> 02:07:27.406
robustly hard to fool a verifier.

02:07:28.346 --> 02:07:31.686
So you start off with a formula,
and either it's satisfiable or not

02:07:31.726 --> 02:07:35.546
satisfiable, right? And what does that
mean? If it's satisfiable, you can write

02:07:35.606 --> 02:07:39.366
down an assignment, and I can check one by
one that the entire thing evaluates to true.

02:07:40.186 --> 02:07:44.106
But if it's not satisfiable, that
means someplace you're lying. You

02:07:44.146 --> 02:07:47.926
write down this purported satisfying
assignment, and when I go

02:07:48.006 --> 02:07:51.146
clause by clause, I'm quickly
gonna find one clause

02:07:51.846 --> 02:07:55.766
that is not true, and that
invalidates the whole thing. And

02:07:55.846 --> 02:07:59.066
I say, "Aha, I caught you. It's not
actually a satisfying assignment."

02:08:00.066 --> 02:08:03.666
So the idea behind things like probabilistically
checkable proofs, without getting

02:08:03.706 --> 02:08:07.546
into a lot of the technical
definitions, the idea is really

02:08:07.746 --> 02:08:11.346
gap amplification. So
can you start off with a

02:08:11.426 --> 02:08:14.906
formula that is hard to decide whether

02:08:14.966 --> 02:08:18.846
it's satisfiable or not?
And can you engineer a new,

02:08:18.946 --> 02:08:23.086
maybe much bigger formula
that's robustly easy to check

02:08:23.926 --> 02:08:27.666
in the sense that if it's satisfiable, it'll
still be satisfiable. You can write down an

02:08:27.706 --> 02:08:31.646
assignment, and it'll be fine. But
if it's not, I should actually be

02:08:31.686 --> 02:08:35.346
able to just flip a few
coins, query random

02:08:35.426 --> 02:08:39.946
variables, check a random clause, and with
good probability find that you're lying.

02:08:40.886 --> 02:08:44.826
So the point is that with very
sublinear inspection, with only

02:08:44.926 --> 02:08:48.506
constant amount of inspection, I
can find that you're cheating.

02:08:49.626 --> 02:08:53.606
So this was a momentous result in
theoretical computer science because

02:08:54.226 --> 02:08:57.746
it laid the foundation really for what's
called hardness of approximation.

02:08:58.546 --> 02:09:02.506
So a lot of algorithms and complexity theory
is what do you do when you're faced with

02:09:02.526 --> 02:09:04.566
problems that are genuinely hard to solve?

02:09:06.026 --> 02:09:09.566
And, you know, one of the things we do
is we try to approximately solve them.

02:09:10.526 --> 02:09:14.106
So maybe we can't find an assignment
if there is one that satisfies all the

02:09:14.166 --> 02:09:17.906
clauses, but can we find one
that satisfies many of the

02:09:17.946 --> 02:09:21.506
clauses? How large a
fraction of the clauses?

02:09:22.466 --> 02:09:26.126
Now, even there, we would expect
problems to become computationally hard

02:09:26.506 --> 02:09:30.286
after a certain point. It should be
that it's not just that, you know,

02:09:30.406 --> 02:09:34.486
deciding whether it's completely satisfiable
or not completely satisfiable is hard,

02:09:35.326 --> 02:09:40.746
but maybe even deciding is it 100%
satisfiable or is it only 99% satisfiable.

02:09:41.566 --> 02:09:44.846
The best case over all
assignments satisfy at most

02:09:45.086 --> 02:09:50.146
99% of the clauses. That too might
end up being hard to decide.

02:09:50.966 --> 02:09:54.806
So these are what probabilistically
checkable proofs give us. In

02:09:54.846 --> 02:09:58.546
places where we knew that the optimization
problem was hard, they tell us

02:09:58.966 --> 02:10:00.586
that it's even hard to approximate.

02:10:01.586 --> 02:10:05.386
And the Unique Games Conjecture
is a strengthening of the

02:10:05.446 --> 02:10:09.226
ordinary PCP theorem because it
has to do with a certain type of

02:10:09.286 --> 02:10:13.206
label cover instance. It's a bit
technical to go into the details, but the

02:10:13.246 --> 02:10:17.106
point is that if that version of a
probabilistically checkable proof were

02:10:17.186 --> 02:10:20.425
true, it would have this
great organizing effect

02:10:21.206 --> 02:10:24.226
where it would turn out
that for a huge swath of

02:10:24.646 --> 02:10:28.126
problems, we would actually
know what is the best

02:10:28.206 --> 02:10:30.786
approximation algorithm, and it
would be something very simple.

02:10:32.186 --> 02:10:36.166
So when I started off in the field, the Unique
Games Conjecture had been posed, you know, a few

02:10:36.206 --> 02:10:41.846
years before that, and there was still a
lot of debate about whether it should be

02:10:41.926 --> 02:10:42.786
true or false.

02:10:43.966 --> 02:10:47.866
And, you know, people worked hard to try
and give, you know, proofs of it. People

02:10:47.946 --> 02:10:49.366
worked hard to try and disprove it.

02:10:50.166 --> 02:10:54.066
There was this amazing time period, maybe
in the 2010s, where there was quite a lot

02:10:54.106 --> 02:10:55.026
of back and forth

02:10:55.826 --> 02:10:59.626
and, you know, there would be new results came out
that people's opinions would change whether they

02:10:59.686 --> 02:11:01.426
believed unique games or not.

02:11:02.286 --> 02:11:06.186
Uh, I like this quip. I forget who said
it, so I can't properly attribute it, but

02:11:06.226 --> 02:11:09.686
someone told me, "You
know, maybe God's gonna

02:11:09.726 --> 02:11:13.186
decide whether it's true or false based
on which side is working harder."

02:11:14.546 --> 02:11:16.506
Because it really seemed
to change year to year.

02:11:17.446 --> 02:11:20.946
And you know, one of the results,
one of the headlining results in the

02:11:21.026 --> 02:11:25.106
OpenAI drop of proofs was that the
Unique Games Conjecture is true.

02:11:26.226 --> 02:11:31.406
Now, I should mention that this is not
without spectacular human progress on it.

02:11:32.226 --> 02:11:35.926
So one of my colleagues at MIT, one
of my colleagues at IES, they both

02:11:35.966 --> 02:11:39.826
contributed, among other people,
to a lot of the major strides.

02:11:40.386 --> 02:11:44.026
So Dor Minzer and Irit
Dinur, they proved a weaker

02:11:44.086 --> 02:11:47.886
version called the Two-to-One
Games Theorem, and this really

02:11:47.946 --> 02:11:51.846
was a, you know, shot heard round the
world in theoretical computer science

02:11:51.886 --> 02:11:55.526
because at that point, I think a
lot of people weren't sure what

02:11:55.586 --> 02:11:59.486
was a path forward for Unique
Games Conjecture, and I

02:11:59.546 --> 02:12:03.466
think their work really gave a viable
path forward to something that was in the

02:12:03.506 --> 02:12:07.006
vicinity of Unique Games. So
it sounded like, you know,

02:12:07.326 --> 02:12:10.666
maybe humans could get there
with another 10, 20 years.

02:12:12.006 --> 02:12:15.806
But, you know, just when OpenAI
released the results, it turns

02:12:15.886 --> 02:12:18.586
out that the Unique Games
Conjecture is actually true.

02:12:19.646 --> 02:12:23.566
So this is quite remarkable because
you know, at some points I,

02:12:23.786 --> 02:12:27.126
I guess I did find myself
on the side of the doubters

02:12:27.766 --> 02:12:31.746
because Unique Games just sounded
too nice to be true because, you

02:12:31.786 --> 02:12:35.546
know a lot of work like
Prasad Raghavendra's work,

02:12:36.226 --> 02:12:40.206
it implies optimal hardness
of approximation for so

02:12:40.246 --> 02:12:44.126
many different types of constraint
satisfaction problems that the conclusion

02:12:44.186 --> 02:12:46.286
just looked to me too strong to be true.

02:12:46.946 --> 02:12:49.146
- What did you feel when
you found out that night?

