because now we can move forward
on the next phase, which

02:13:40.966 --> 02:13:44.646
is digesting those proofs and
figuring out where the field moves

02:13:44.766 --> 02:13:48.626
after that. Now, in terms of how did
I feel that night you know, as Alex

02:13:48.686 --> 02:13:52.586
mentioned, we, you know, we
all had a drink in hand. And

02:13:52.606 --> 02:13:57.226
then I saw, you know, on my phone the
news that these proofs had dropped.

02:13:58.146 --> 02:14:01.726
And you know, people kept up coming
and talking to me at the gala,

02:14:02.286 --> 02:14:05.166
but it's already hard to
socialize as a mathematician-

02:14:05.246 --> 02:14:08.746
... let alone, you know, when you
have math you really want to read.

02:14:08.786 --> 02:14:09.946
So it was doubly hard.

02:14:10.918 --> 02:14:14.758
The other funny thing about it was, you know,
I was in New York already, so I took it as an

02:14:14.838 --> 02:14:18.658
opportunity before the gala to visit
a lot of people I knew at NYU.

02:14:20.078 --> 02:14:24.018
And I just happened to be meeting with
Subhash Khot, the one who introduced the

02:14:24.058 --> 02:14:28.038
unique games conjecture. And I just so
happened to ask him, you know, just

02:14:28.098 --> 02:14:31.678
a few hours before the gala, before
the OpenAI paper is released,

02:14:31.678 --> 02:14:35.558
"Subhash, what would you
do, you know, if they

02:14:35.598 --> 02:14:39.498
release a positive proof of unique
games? Are you gonna read it?"

02:14:40.158 --> 02:14:41.698
And he told me, "No."

02:14:44.198 --> 02:14:48.058
Which, you know, that's a lot of
people's reactions, unfortunately.

02:14:48.098 --> 02:14:51.758
It's just kind of true that there are
these grand challenges we work on,

02:14:52.478 --> 02:14:56.058
and sometimes when they get solved
you know, sometimes it feels like,

02:14:56.598 --> 02:14:58.818
you know, someone has stolen your thunder.

02:14:59.278 --> 02:15:02.938
You know, it's not like people didn't have
ideas about how to solve the unique games

02:15:02.978 --> 02:15:06.838
conjecture, and I bet you those
blueprints would have panned out if

02:15:06.878 --> 02:15:11.378
we'd given them more time. I think
the other thing I worry is that

02:15:12.598 --> 02:15:16.158
you know, that we lose out
on the human effort of

02:15:16.258 --> 02:15:20.218
trying to reach those solutions to
begin with. You know, math is not just

02:15:20.318 --> 02:15:24.258
about what is the furthest I can
reach with the current set of tools

02:15:24.378 --> 02:15:28.098
I have. Is it a convex hull? Is
it a subgroup? I don't know.

02:15:28.938 --> 02:15:32.878
But, you know, sometimes when it's
a human-driven solution, we learn

02:15:32.938 --> 02:15:36.878
a lot more in the process. So I
worry about the fact that there

02:15:36.898 --> 02:15:40.858
were these great landmark problems,
field-defining problems that a lot of people

02:15:40.898 --> 02:15:41.878
were reaching towards,

02:15:42.698 --> 02:15:46.638
and that decisive progress was
made, you know, and the results

02:15:46.718 --> 02:15:50.618
all dropped in one night, and
that people lose their enthusiasm

02:15:50.658 --> 02:15:54.598
for going down those paths. And I wonder
if we'd given those people that space

02:15:54.638 --> 02:15:58.398
to go down those paths, what other
exciting things we would have discovered.

02:15:59.278 --> 02:16:03.238
So I think one of the things I mentioned, you know,
we were all trying to process it at MoMath and,

02:16:03.418 --> 02:16:07.218
you know, there were a lot of really nice
talks. Not all of them went up on YouTube.

02:16:07.318 --> 02:16:11.158
Some of them were, you know, about
benefactors and awards named after them.

02:16:11.878 --> 02:16:15.798
And the thing I kept coming back to is this
idea that, you know, why do you do math in

02:16:15.838 --> 02:16:19.198
the first place is to play
and learn about the world.

02:16:19.858 --> 02:16:23.198
And we saw so many
manifestations of it that night.

02:16:23.318 --> 02:16:27.098
You know, because there were awards
named after people who, you know,

02:16:27.178 --> 02:16:30.918
why were they donating to the museum
was because they wanted their kids

02:16:31.558 --> 02:16:35.398
to have a place to play with mathematical
ideas and learn about the world.

02:16:36.158 --> 02:16:39.758
It was also the opening of
an Escher exhibit, and what

02:16:39.858 --> 02:16:44.698
a great visual of, you know, what happens
when we play with mathematical concepts,

02:16:45.598 --> 02:16:50.538
right? And so it felt to me that in this
moment, it's really important to remember,

02:16:51.158 --> 02:16:54.798
as we're going through all of this change
together, as we don't know what the future

02:16:54.858 --> 02:16:58.598
holds, what AI and mathematicians'
collaboration will look like in the

02:16:58.638 --> 02:17:03.478
future, what hiring will look like, that
the reason we all got into it was because

02:17:04.398 --> 02:17:05.518
we were very curious.

02:17:06.418 --> 02:17:10.358
You know, we wanted to understand the
world, and mathematicians are very good

02:17:10.418 --> 02:17:13.658
at abstracting things.
You know, they don't just

02:17:13.738 --> 02:17:17.018
understand one little
piece, but they find some

02:17:17.098 --> 02:17:20.078
abstraction and some set of
definitions that turns out to be

02:17:20.718 --> 02:17:23.958
unreasonably powerful in some
other corner of mathematics.

02:17:24.838 --> 02:17:28.498
So I think it's really important, whatever
the future holds, to make sure that

02:17:29.058 --> 02:17:32.878
we organize our field in a way where
we still encourage people to play with

02:17:32.958 --> 02:17:36.058
ideas. I don't know what kind
of reward system will work,

02:17:36.718 --> 02:17:40.558
but, you know, when I look out and I
talk to young people in the field, they

02:17:40.598 --> 02:17:44.158
have a lot of anxiety. You know, they
hear a lot of stories even about

02:17:44.758 --> 02:17:48.258
people they know you
know, running harnesses

02:17:49.118 --> 02:17:52.878
to, you know scrape the
web for open problems, to

02:17:53.378 --> 02:17:56.258
dump as many papers as
they can to flood arXiv.

02:17:57.158 --> 02:18:00.898
I don't know if that's a good strategy. It
doesn't seem like it is, but it certainly

02:18:00.918 --> 02:18:04.438
doesn't feel like fun to me. It
doesn't feel like we're playing with

02:18:04.518 --> 02:18:06.878
ideas and learning about the world,

02:18:07.798 --> 02:18:11.938
and that's sad. I just wanna get
back to the things I really enjoy.

02:18:12.938 --> 02:18:16.918
The other thing maybe I just wanna
add in there too is, like, you know,

02:18:16.998 --> 02:18:20.778
it's a big identity crisis for a
lot of people 'cause, you know,

02:18:21.018 --> 02:18:24.658
some people view themselves more as problem
solvers, and that can feel very comforting,

02:18:25.498 --> 02:18:29.398
that you can draw a little circle around the
types of problems you work on, the types of

02:18:29.457 --> 02:18:33.258
techniques you use, the types of
problems you care about. And that

02:18:33.318 --> 02:18:37.018
feels very comforting because the rest of
the world is messy and you've got blinders

02:18:37.098 --> 02:18:39.398
on. This is the part that I care about.

02:18:40.218 --> 02:18:44.118
Now, I think, is the time to remove those
blinders because I just don't find that to be

02:18:44.178 --> 02:18:45.358
a productive way to do it.

02:18:46.178 --> 02:18:50.158
You know, we love playing and learning
about the world around us, and it's

02:18:50.198 --> 02:18:52.458
important to remember as, you
know, a young mathematician,

02:18:53.358 --> 02:18:57.118
are you smarter than you are curious, or
are you more curious than you are smart?

02:18:58.038 --> 02:19:00.478
It's very important to be more
curious than anything else.

02:19:01.298 --> 02:19:04.158
- And you mentioned fun. There could
be another perspective of it,

02:19:05.178 --> 02:19:08.778
of more about the struggle
from my maybe outsider

02:19:08.818 --> 02:19:16.338
perspective is some of the most fun in
the moment sure feels like struggle. I've

02:19:16.698 --> 02:19:20.098
talked to a lot of Olympic
gold medalists. A lot of them

02:19:20.578 --> 02:19:24.198
feel something approximating depression
after they win the gold medal

02:19:24.658 --> 02:19:28.298
because it's like you have
given everything, and you

02:19:28.358 --> 02:19:31.538
realize everything you've
given is now over.

02:19:32.098 --> 02:19:35.878
- Thirty years ago, there was a
documentary of Andrew Wiles,

02:19:36.818 --> 02:19:40.598
and there's a great scene, I think they
even run it right at the very beginning,

02:19:41.618 --> 02:19:45.078
To your gold medalist, you know, what
do you do once you've won the thing?

02:19:46.078 --> 02:19:49.638
He's sitting there at his desk, and
he's describing the moment when he

02:19:49.698 --> 02:19:53.678
realizes what the solution is, not his
initial announcement, which was flawed, and

02:19:53.698 --> 02:19:57.398
then for many months, he was trying to
correct it, and he finally saw how to do it.

02:19:58.138 --> 02:20:04.838
And he's trying to say, you
know, "I will never again in my

02:20:04.898 --> 02:20:08.878
life have a moment as powerful as the

02:20:08.918 --> 02:20:12.358
moment that I experienced at this
desk that day," and he's choking up.

02:20:12.862 --> 02:20:16.722
- You can't get the words out. So
that-- Can you get that if you

02:20:16.762 --> 02:20:21.282
press the button and it gave you
the solution? Absolutely not. So

02:20:22.162 --> 02:20:24.382
exactly to both of your points, like

02:20:25.622 --> 02:20:30.282
the journey, you know, if you see
a giant hill, a giant mountain,

02:20:31.182 --> 02:20:34.942
and you say, "One day I'm gonna get to the top of that,"
and you start training for it, and you're sort of

02:20:34.982 --> 02:20:38.922
doing little by little, whatever, and
then a helicopter lands and just drops

02:20:38.962 --> 02:20:41.362
you at the top. Can you
actually speak to that? The

02:20:42.282 --> 02:20:45.842
interesting thing about Andrew Wiles
and a little bit about Grigori

02:20:46.082 --> 02:20:51.722
Perelman as well is working alone for a
prolonged period of time. What do you

02:20:51.782 --> 02:20:55.642
make of that? How common is that
in mathematics of sitting there

02:20:56.022 --> 02:20:59.102
and grinding a problem for,
in his case, seven years?

02:20:59.902 --> 02:21:03.822
Until the 20th century, almost every
math paper that had ever been written

02:21:03.882 --> 02:21:07.482
was written, you know, had one
author. I think now the median is two

02:21:07.962 --> 02:21:11.942
in mathematics, and so
we're not a lab science.

02:21:12.182 --> 02:21:16.102
It really is a solitary
process. Now, see, the things

02:21:16.142 --> 02:21:20.022
that I give my graduate students to do are
not the things that I'm working on, sort of

02:21:20.062 --> 02:21:20.862
by design.

02:21:20.882 --> 02:21:24.862
I want them to have their own field. I wanna
open doors for them. It's different in

02:21:24.902 --> 02:21:28.842
different fields, right? So when they
have a success, it really should be

02:21:28.902 --> 02:21:32.802
their success, even if I've sort of-- I
had an idea that this would go somewhere

02:21:32.842 --> 02:21:38.042
and they executed on it, that's
theirs. Um, but in terms of

02:21:40.102 --> 02:21:43.962
sitting there for s-- Uh, the paper that I put
out a month ago is something I've been thinking

02:21:44.002 --> 02:21:47.882
about for 10 years. It's a
five-author paper. Over those 10

02:21:47.942 --> 02:21:51.862
years, we made incremental progress to the
point where we finally, you know, had a

02:21:51.922 --> 02:21:55.742
solution, and that paper was an
instance of there's a phenomenon

02:21:55.802 --> 02:21:57.182
here, and I don't understand what it is.

02:21:58.082 --> 02:22:02.002
It wasn't, "Here's a problem, solve it."
Like, we could not, at the start of

02:22:02.042 --> 02:22:06.902
the project, give you the Lean,
formalized statement that needs a sorry.

02:22:07.542 --> 02:22:10.142
Like, you discover what the
statement is through the process of

02:22:11.242 --> 02:22:15.162
hacking away at the problem little by
little until you see enough of it to say,

02:22:15.202 --> 02:22:16.182
"This is how it comes together."

02:22:17.022 --> 02:22:20.882
Actually, that makes me think, can you
actually describe how your thinking

02:22:20.942 --> 02:22:23.362
process of how you think
through really hard problems?

02:22:24.782 --> 02:22:30.642
Is there some aspects to it that are
interesting, like a lot of coffee

02:22:31.522 --> 02:22:36.102
in the morning, at night,
alone, brooding with a cigar

02:22:37.002 --> 02:22:41.602
or on a together at a whiteboard
with others? Is there some

02:22:42.522 --> 02:22:45.162
interesting color, texture,
insights you can give?

02:22:45.862 --> 02:22:51.302
- Sure. I think a lot
through world models in

02:22:51.342 --> 02:22:55.242
some way. So, you know,
I have some mental model

02:22:55.322 --> 02:22:57.862
of how I think results
in some sub-area go,

02:22:58.742 --> 02:23:02.222
and I think that colors a lot of
how I see surrounding areas too.

02:23:03.002 --> 02:23:06.482
So for me, a lot of my work is really
about, you know, building bridges

02:23:06.522 --> 02:23:10.482
between algorithmic thinking and other
problems that usually aren't studied

02:23:10.682 --> 02:23:14.482
through that same algorithmic lens.
So I think when I first started as

02:23:14.582 --> 02:23:18.002
a advisor, I sort of made
the mistake that I gave

02:23:18.422 --> 02:23:21.342
students questions that
were 100% in my area.

02:23:22.742 --> 02:23:26.082
And the problem then was, you know, when they
would come back and they'd have an idea,

02:23:26.862 --> 02:23:30.802
I could shoot it down very quickly, and
I was either right or I was wrong, but

02:23:30.842 --> 02:23:34.362
either way, no one was
having any fun. And-

02:23:34.382 --> 02:23:38.102
You know, over time, what I started to do was
I would give students questions that were

02:23:38.162 --> 02:23:42.042
10, 20 degrees off from my own
area, things that I wanted to

02:23:42.102 --> 02:23:46.002
understand better myself, papers that
I thought were important that I just

02:23:46.422 --> 02:23:48.062
hadn't had time to sit down and read,

02:23:48.882 --> 02:23:51.382
and then I have the student
explain the result to me.

02:23:52.362 --> 02:23:56.202
And what's fun is we would go through that process
together, not, you know, by printing out the

02:23:56.262 --> 02:23:58.062
paper and going through it line by line,

02:23:59.042 --> 02:24:02.922
but trying to reverse engineer why do
we think this works in the first place?

02:24:03.622 --> 02:24:07.362
And there's so many details that once you
get the feel for it, you kind of have the

02:24:07.442 --> 02:24:11.242
sense that they ought to work out.
But at least in important papers,

02:24:11.322 --> 02:24:15.262
there are one or maybe even more than
that, key ideas that really change

02:24:15.702 --> 02:24:18.022
your perspective, "Oh, this
really might be doable."

02:24:19.242 --> 02:24:22.662
So what I found was, you know,
as soon as we start trying to

02:24:22.742 --> 02:24:26.322
understand one of these results, we
find our own way of looking at things,

02:24:27.302 --> 02:24:30.862
and then it's not long
after we find some other

02:24:30.921 --> 02:24:34.902
questions that we now think are approachable
because we found our own way of,

02:24:35.222 --> 02:24:39.002
of organizing our thoughts. So
that's often the way I do it is

02:24:39.082 --> 02:24:42.962
the earlier that I can have the
student in the driver's seat, not

02:24:42.982 --> 02:24:45.582
necessarily with a well-defined question-

02:24:45.722 --> 02:24:49.562
but with them teaching me something and
them being responsible for distilling and

02:24:49.702 --> 02:24:52.882
digesting and abstracting
it down to its essence.

02:24:53.802 --> 02:24:57.502
You know, reasoning through with them
analogies that aren't meant to literally be

02:24:57.582 --> 02:25:01.522
true, but help us figure out what
to expect in neighboring areas.

02:25:02.382 --> 02:25:06.222
You know, then it feels like they bring a
unique perspective, and we're off to the

02:25:06.282 --> 02:25:11.902
races. So for me, in a lot of
ways, the technology is only gonna

02:25:11.962 --> 02:25:15.822
enable that kind of work because it
means that a much richer set of problems

02:25:15.882 --> 02:25:21.202
are approachable. You know, for me, when
I find experts in related areas that can

02:25:21.662 --> 02:25:25.502
really explain things in my own language,
I hold onto them for dear life because,

02:25:26.242 --> 02:25:29.702
you know, they can speak the
gospel. They can tell me how

02:25:30.082 --> 02:25:33.302
this area is organized and
explain it in my own language.

02:25:34.122 --> 02:25:37.262
But now, you know, I have
technology that can do that too,

02:25:38.002 --> 02:25:41.822
and it also feels a bit sad that you
lose that human element that was there

02:25:41.922 --> 02:25:47.622
once before. I think also students
have so much anxiety about, you know,

02:25:47.722 --> 02:25:51.662
what publications are gonna look like
that one of the hardest parts is, you

02:25:51.702 --> 02:25:55.602
know, when I'm thinking about a project, I'm
really trying to think about it in a sort

02:25:55.642 --> 02:25:59.322
of multi-year time horizon
because I have some

02:25:59.402 --> 02:26:03.042
meta vision that I wanna
realize, like how can we use

02:26:03.102 --> 02:26:07.022
learning theory techniques in quantum
many-body systems? And there are a lot

02:26:07.062 --> 02:26:11.022
of stepping stones on route to that, but some
of the most exciting problems are, you know,

02:26:11.182 --> 02:26:15.162
many years out. But students are
so worried about the incoming

02:26:15.242 --> 02:26:19.182
AI tidal wave, you know, the future
of work, universal basic income.

02:26:19.802 --> 02:26:23.362
Are any of us gonna have jobs? That
they're really thinking in three- and

02:26:23.502 --> 02:26:27.462
six-month horizons, and then it's
hard to find problems that we

02:26:27.502 --> 02:26:29.542
both care about just because-

02:26:30.426 --> 02:26:32.386
- You know, we're thinking
about different worlds

02:26:33.126 --> 02:26:36.986
- You don't think we should encourage them to
continue to think long term? I mean, I understand

02:26:37.046 --> 02:26:37.186
that-

02:26:37.486 --> 02:26:39.926
- Oh, I do, but they don't
always listen to you.

02:26:41.486 --> 02:26:45.246
I mean, I don't know if I'm right or wrong. You
know, maybe AI will solve all of our problems,

02:26:45.866 --> 02:26:49.766
but it seems like the only ones that are
actually worth us working on at this moment

02:26:49.806 --> 02:26:53.646
in time are the ones we're still
gonna care about in a bunch of

02:26:53.686 --> 02:26:57.586
years, and you should only be working on
future-proof problems. If you're working

02:26:57.646 --> 02:27:01.606
on things that, you know, you're in a rush
because you think the next generation of

02:27:01.646 --> 02:27:05.546
models will definitely solve those
problems, then maybe just go on vacation

02:27:05.566 --> 02:27:06.766
and don't solve that problem.

02:27:07.586 --> 02:27:11.186
- Yeah. Can you speak to how you think--
You work with a lot of natural

02:27:11.266 --> 02:27:14.926
systems. What are the interesting
insights that could be pulled out

02:27:15.206 --> 02:27:16.246
of these natural systems?

02:27:17.226 --> 02:27:22.226
- So I find a tension between focusing very

02:27:22.266 --> 02:27:26.126
hard on an engineering problem I'm
trying to solve, which can be very

02:27:26.826 --> 02:27:29.986
singular. You put on blinders, you
only think about this problem,

02:27:31.426 --> 02:27:35.206
and then giving yourself
the kind of mental freedom

02:27:36.086 --> 02:27:39.966
to look around and just observe
and be curious. Very often you

02:27:40.006 --> 02:27:43.526
find the solution when you
turn over a rock and when it's

02:27:44.346 --> 02:27:45.646
not something you're thinking about,

02:27:46.466 --> 02:27:50.426
you know, with the full front of your mind,
but in the side of your mind. If you're

02:27:50.466 --> 02:27:54.446
looking at a problem, you know, through your
peripheral vision, you have a much better chance of

02:27:54.506 --> 02:28:01.206
solving it sometimes. And so this is a
constant balance for me is observing

02:28:02.366 --> 02:28:06.286
all of the fields that I don't work in and all
of the nature that I don't understand and all

02:28:06.346 --> 02:28:09.926
of the things that I'm curious about,
but then also having very focused

02:28:09.966 --> 02:28:13.906
problems that you don't relent, you don't
let go. And I-- this must be true for all of

02:28:13.946 --> 02:28:17.826
us. We-- I've heard from both of
you, you know, focus problems you've

02:28:17.846 --> 02:28:21.406
been working on for years and
decades that you haven't let go of.

02:28:21.886 --> 02:28:25.746
But presumably that is informed by

02:28:26.286 --> 02:28:29.766
a million other pieces of information that
you happen to pick up through interesting

02:28:29.806 --> 02:28:31.086
conversations and interesting-

02:28:31.106 --> 02:28:34.946
- Do you have example? 'Cause it
sounds like neighboring areas is a

02:28:35.026 --> 02:28:38.866
common theme. Do you have
examples of things you're like,

02:28:38.965 --> 02:28:42.726
you're-- the curious peripheral vision
of your brain went, "Oh, okay, that's an

02:28:42.766 --> 02:28:46.406
interesting system, might be relevant,"
and then ended up being so or

