and I would sort of argue that, you know, it's
a very important direction for theoretical

02:44:09.182 --> 02:44:12.802
computer science to go in.
It's hard to figure out

02:44:13.122 --> 02:44:16.422
what you should articulate
about natural problems

02:44:17.082 --> 02:44:20.622
that is really the right level of
detail that your algorithm can

02:44:20.662 --> 02:44:24.742
exploit it and can do better things
than it can in the worst case.

02:44:25.862 --> 02:44:29.442
But let's use an example. So you were
talking about the secretary problem.

02:44:30.242 --> 02:44:34.702
So the way that this works is it's an example
of something called online algorithms.

02:44:35.642 --> 02:44:39.602
So you have a sequence of options coming.

02:44:40.310 --> 02:44:44.270
One through N. They come in
each time step, and your goal

02:44:44.370 --> 02:44:48.250
is, you know, when someone
comes along, you know their

02:44:48.330 --> 02:44:52.290
ranking relative to everyone
else you've seen before, but

02:44:52.330 --> 02:44:55.930
you don't know their ranking relative
to the people who are yet to come.

02:44:56.510 --> 02:45:00.470
And your goal is to, you
know, to make a decision when

02:45:00.510 --> 02:45:03.690
the person comes along, do you
commit or do they move on,

02:45:04.470 --> 02:45:08.650
in such a way that you have a good chance
of finding the absolute best candidate.

02:45:09.750 --> 02:45:13.430
And so the point is that you can actually
get a constant competitive ratio

02:45:13.530 --> 02:45:17.010
algorithm that what you do,
the best strategy is to

02:45:17.610 --> 02:45:21.450
wait some amount of the time, not make
any decisions, don't accept anyone.

02:45:22.010 --> 02:45:25.510
And then once that probationary
window has passed, you take the first

02:45:25.610 --> 02:45:28.870
person who's better than
anyone you've seen thus far.

02:45:29.790 --> 02:45:33.510
And that'll give you a good competitive ratio
in the sense that you can prove theorems.

02:45:34.150 --> 02:45:38.010
Assuming the inputs arrive in a
uniformly random order, you're

02:45:38.050 --> 02:45:41.850
likely to do pretty well compared
to as if you knew what that

02:45:41.910 --> 02:45:43.950
sequence of inputs was in advance.

02:45:45.030 --> 02:45:49.010
So there's, you know, under online
algorithms, there's this veil of uncertainty,

02:45:49.810 --> 02:45:53.730
which is, I don't know what the sequence
of inputs are gonna look like. I

02:45:53.770 --> 02:45:57.690
wanna do well no matter what
that sequence is because,

02:45:58.190 --> 02:46:00.990
you know, I don't really know how to
model what that sequence looks like.

02:46:01.990 --> 02:46:05.950
Now, you can instead say, "Hey,
maybe the algorithms I derive

02:46:06.010 --> 02:46:09.710
aren't good enough for my applications.
Can I do better by trying to

02:46:09.790 --> 02:46:13.550
model more of the world around me?"
And then there's kind of an art to

02:46:13.610 --> 02:46:17.330
figuring out what you should model,
what you should build into your model,

02:46:17.770 --> 02:46:22.670
and what you shouldn't. And that seems to
me like a tremendously important area,

02:46:23.370 --> 02:46:27.250
to explore. So for
example, one other variant

02:46:27.350 --> 02:46:30.890
of, you know, online algorithms that's
really important is the paging problem.

02:46:31.810 --> 02:46:35.210
So your laptop solves this problem all
the time. You know, it's getting a

02:46:35.290 --> 02:46:39.210
sequence of requests of things
that it wants to recall

02:46:39.270 --> 02:46:43.250
from memory. And the question is, what
should it actually put in its cache,

02:46:43.370 --> 02:46:45.350
the part that's the easiest to access?

02:46:46.210 --> 02:46:50.150
And the problem again is that you don't know
what that sequence of requests looks like in

02:46:50.210 --> 02:46:54.130
advance. So you can study the online
algorithms problem of, you know, what

02:46:54.190 --> 02:46:57.810
is a good policy for managing the
cache? You know, when something gets

02:46:57.950 --> 02:47:01.770
accessed, you know, I have to
put it in, but what do I evict?

02:47:02.550 --> 02:47:06.350
And you can design algorithms that are
good in terms of their competitive

02:47:06.410 --> 02:47:10.190
ratio. The number of times
they miss in terms of those

02:47:10.290 --> 02:47:13.510
accesses is not much worse than,

02:47:14.330 --> 02:47:18.250
the best offline optimum if someone
told me that sequence of requests in

02:47:18.310 --> 02:47:21.670
advance and I could optimize just
for that sequence of requests.

02:47:22.590 --> 02:47:26.570
Now, this is a problem that's been studied
since the '80s and '90s. The trouble is

02:47:26.610 --> 02:47:30.570
somehow that, you know, the best algorithms
you can give, we know tight upper and lower

02:47:30.610 --> 02:47:32.970
bounds, they're just not that very good.

02:47:34.170 --> 02:47:37.890
You know, the number of misses they make
compared to the best offline optimum

02:47:38.450 --> 02:47:42.310
is a large factor off
from it, and that just

02:47:42.350 --> 02:47:45.690
doesn't compete with what your
laptop is able to do right now

02:47:46.350 --> 02:47:50.290
because it really makes very few cache
misses at the end of the day. So you're

02:47:50.350 --> 02:47:54.330
stuck with this problem which sort of
bridges mathematical thinking with trying to

02:47:54.410 --> 02:47:58.230
model the natural world, which
is, you know, what is it

02:47:58.310 --> 02:48:01.990
about realistic sequences
of requests that makes

02:48:02.030 --> 02:48:05.790
them not the worst case? This
is really an inverse problem

02:48:05.850 --> 02:48:09.830
now because it's, you know, the other side
of the coin. Instead of someone handed

02:48:09.870 --> 02:48:13.570
you a problem that you're just trying to solve in
the worst case, and we can all agree on how it's

02:48:13.630 --> 02:48:17.230
formulated, and we know what's a better
algorithm, it does better on the

02:48:17.250 --> 02:48:21.210
competitive ratio, now you're stuck
with this very fuzzy problem, which is:

02:48:21.770 --> 02:48:25.490
how should I model the world
around me? And in a lot of ways,

02:48:25.690 --> 02:48:29.330
algorithmic thinking is necessary
for figuring out what are

02:48:29.410 --> 02:48:33.210
productive ways to model that problem.
Because it's not that you're gonna

02:48:33.270 --> 02:48:37.230
build a totally realistic model of
what sequence of page requests look

02:48:37.310 --> 02:48:40.930
like, but how am I gonna coarsen
the problem in just the right way

02:48:41.510 --> 02:48:45.250
that it leads to interesting algorithmic
problems that teach me about the

02:48:45.310 --> 02:48:48.970
world, that force me to, you
know, design new approaches to

02:48:49.030 --> 02:48:52.970
solving these very basic problems.
So I'd argue that, you know,

02:48:53.630 --> 02:48:57.270
sort of coming out of this
space where we are only solving

02:48:57.330 --> 02:49:00.950
well-defined problems, but we're
actually using our mathematical and

02:49:01.030 --> 02:49:04.430
algorithmic insights to figure out
where to dig in the first place

02:49:05.150 --> 02:49:09.010
is now more important than ever. It should
have always been important, but you

02:49:09.070 --> 02:49:13.470
know, that to me is really gonna
be thrust into the spotlight.

02:49:14.110 --> 02:49:17.910
- Like a simple example is we were talking
about factoring numbers. Well, factoring a

02:49:17.970 --> 02:49:21.530
random large number is really easy.
Half of them are even. You know,

02:49:21.690 --> 02:49:22.830
factoring means find a factor.

02:49:23.730 --> 02:49:27.450
So it's really those very special
numbers that are products of

02:49:27.530 --> 02:49:31.490
two primes about the square root the size of the
original number. That's hard factoring, sorry.

02:49:32.430 --> 02:49:35.570
- Which makes me think one of the things
you worked on is mathematics of

02:49:36.190 --> 02:49:39.490
LLMs, AI systems, neural networks,

02:49:40.830 --> 02:49:46.470
and those things are pretty damn
good at modeling the natural world.

02:49:46.970 --> 02:49:53.910
So what can you learn from that? First
of all, why does deep learning work?

02:49:55.130 --> 02:49:56.610
- Do we have another three hours?

02:49:57.990 --> 02:50:01.950
- Do you have— Do we even have intuition?
So at the mathematical level, at

02:50:01.990 --> 02:50:05.810
the philosophical level, so we're in between
all of that, the full range. I don't know

02:50:05.870 --> 02:50:09.970
how we even enter that. Okay.
So that's a big question.

02:50:10.190 --> 02:50:13.730
And let me try and break off a
few pieces of it just to try and

02:50:13.790 --> 02:50:19.290
condense my own views on this. So, you
know, in the OpenAI drop of problems,

02:50:20.250 --> 02:50:22.990
there was no result in there
that explained deep learning.

02:50:23.330 --> 02:50:27.090
There was no result in there that
explained LLMs. You know, there's no

02:50:27.130 --> 02:50:30.790
bound we know that if we could improve
it would suddenly explain deep learning.

02:50:31.910 --> 02:50:33.610
The problems are just not posed that way.

02:50:34.430 --> 02:50:38.450
And I would really argue that what's
needed in a lot of deep learning theory

02:50:39.370 --> 02:50:42.710
comes hand in hand with better
ways to model the world around us.

02:50:43.510 --> 02:50:47.250
If we look at the computational problems
that deep learning is solving, that

02:50:47.390 --> 02:50:51.330
LLMs are solving, some of these
problems are very hard. You

02:50:51.370 --> 02:50:58.270
know, trying to fit the parameters
of a depth four network to data is a

02:50:58.370 --> 02:51:02.170
cryptographically hard problem. We
know that. There exist instances of

02:51:02.210 --> 02:51:05.870
problems which, if you could
really solve them in a worst-case

02:51:05.950 --> 02:51:09.850
sense, you know, that would change
our understanding of cryptography.

02:51:11.030 --> 02:51:14.950
And yet these problems get solved all the time,
every day, when we're fitting deep -- you

02:51:14.990 --> 02:51:18.950
know, much deeper networks than that.
So I would argue that, you know,

02:51:18.990 --> 02:51:22.270
a large part of that is
how nice natural data is.

02:51:23.130 --> 02:51:26.850
It's kind of like, you know, there's the
saying in physics that nature abhors a

02:51:26.890 --> 02:51:32.250
vacuum. I guess my version of it is
that in some sense, nature abhors

02:51:32.310 --> 02:51:33.650
computationally hard problems.

02:51:34.730 --> 02:51:38.550
And it's an art to figuring out why
that is, how to draw the boundaries

02:51:38.610 --> 02:51:42.590
around the types of problems you're
studying that actually evades the hardness

02:51:42.650 --> 02:51:43.870
of the problem in the worst case.

02:51:44.850 --> 02:51:48.670
So there's an interesting example back
from the '90s. So certainly back then,

02:51:48.730 --> 02:51:50.890
and we still are, very
interested in protein folding.

02:51:52.350 --> 02:51:55.930
Now, you can model this in a worst-case
sense. I've got a sequence of amino

02:51:56.050 --> 02:51:59.550
acids. I've got some energy
function based on different

02:51:59.590 --> 02:52:03.470
potential 3D structures, and I
want to find the 3D structure

02:52:03.510 --> 02:52:06.630
that minimizes how much energy there is.

02:52:07.690 --> 02:52:09.930
Now, this problem is
computationally very hard,

02:52:11.130 --> 02:52:14.910
and yet nature solves these problems
all the time. So why is that?

02:52:15.590 --> 02:52:19.270
So there's a very interesting experiment you
can do, which is you start off with a bunch of

02:52:19.690 --> 02:52:23.390
sequences of amino acids. You
try out some heuristics for

02:52:23.450 --> 02:52:27.230
folding them into 3D structures. These are
things that don't solve the problem in the worst

02:52:27.290 --> 02:52:31.550
case. They just seem to do the right
thing sometimes, for whatever that means.

02:52:32.430 --> 02:52:36.330
And then the only sequences of amino
acids that live on to the next

02:52:36.390 --> 02:52:39.710
generation are the ones that
actually have very good folds.

02:52:41.129 --> 02:52:44.570
And over time, what ends up
happening is you create a body of

02:52:44.610 --> 02:52:48.210
problems for which they're not
hard in the worst case, but

02:52:48.730 --> 02:52:53.050
eminently the heuristics you've developed
are very good at solving those problems.

02:52:53.510 --> 02:52:57.490
So in a lot of ways, you know, what are
the structure of proteins that nature

02:52:57.570 --> 02:53:01.470
seems to fold all the time? Well,
if they really were hard to solve

02:53:01.510 --> 02:53:05.290
in the worst case, then maybe
the shape they take on would be

02:53:05.350 --> 02:53:08.830
very brittle and fragile and
subject to a lot of boundary

02:53:08.870 --> 02:53:13.910
conditions that make those things behave
in not entirely predictable ways.

02:53:14.910 --> 02:53:18.310
So in some sense, you know,
a lot of our data has

02:53:18.670 --> 02:53:22.110
evolved in ways to make these
computational problems easier.

02:53:22.890 --> 02:53:26.590
So I like to think of it as you can
study LLMs in terms of the optimization

02:53:26.670 --> 02:53:29.350
problems they're solving. You can
study deep learning that way.

02:53:30.210 --> 02:53:33.650
And if you look at it from a worst-case
perspective, you're gonna get very hard

02:53:33.690 --> 02:53:37.010
problems that you just tell
people, "They're hard. Give up."

02:53:37.770 --> 02:53:41.630
But apparently, not giving up was a
really good idea, and I think what

02:53:41.710 --> 02:53:47.530
makes those problems easy you cannot
extract from the structure of natural data.

02:53:48.430 --> 02:53:52.150
The fact that our language has evolved
to make these problems easier is

02:53:52.190 --> 02:53:56.110
intertwined with why LLMs work
the way that they do. So my

02:53:56.170 --> 02:53:59.790
view is that, you know, people working in
this space and theoretical machine learning,

02:54:00.370 --> 02:54:03.010
they have to become more comfortable
with the uncertainty of modeling.

02:54:03.910 --> 02:54:07.650
You know, when we teach a lot of graduate
students in math departments, we teach

02:54:07.710 --> 02:54:12.730
them, you know, how to solve open questions
that are well-defined. But I think

02:54:12.790 --> 02:54:16.410
teaching people that skill of how
to deal with messy, uncertain

02:54:16.470 --> 02:54:20.590
things and, you know, how to model
it and how to critique models

02:54:21.450 --> 02:54:25.010
is just an important part of
going forward, figuring out what

02:54:25.070 --> 02:54:29.030
research in theoretical machine learning
looks like, but maybe, you know,

02:54:29.110 --> 02:54:32.970
applied mathematics much more generally,
maybe mathematics more generally too, for

02:54:33.010 --> 02:54:33.430
that matter.

02:54:33.890 --> 02:54:39.450
- Is it nevertheless amenable to breakthroughs?
So one of the things I would maybe

02:54:40.850 --> 02:54:45.290
push back on a little bit just on
the pure company incentive side,

02:54:46.210 --> 02:54:50.090
this is the thing that people
talk about, which is the

02:54:50.150 --> 02:54:53.990
thing that I think OpenAI, Anthropic,
all these companies are chasing, is the

02:54:54.970 --> 02:54:57.170
self-improving researcher.

02:54:58.490 --> 02:55:02.850
So if they, in fact, had breakthroughs
on the mathematics of LLMs,

02:55:03.830 --> 02:55:05.550
I would venture to say
they wouldn't publish it-

02:55:06.490 --> 02:55:11.250
... because they need those breakthroughs,
right? So it's a fascinating-- 'cause

02:55:12.510 --> 02:55:16.390
that is the big breakthrough from
the pragmatic perspective of AI,

02:55:17.350 --> 02:55:21.290
self-improving and
progressing. So how difficult

02:55:21.410 --> 02:55:24.890
is that problem that you're
kind of referring to, which is

02:55:25.130 --> 02:55:28.930
making big progress on modeling
the natural world, understanding

02:55:29.530 --> 02:55:32.370
the mathematics of how to deal
with that in order to have

02:55:33.230 --> 02:55:36.270
intelligence that is far above
the intelligence of humans?

02:55:36.850 --> 02:55:40.470
- Great question. I mean, I think
alignment is one of the most

02:55:40.510 --> 02:55:44.230
important topics we're facing
right now, right? And, you know,

02:55:44.610 --> 02:55:47.970
just because it's very hard doesn't
mean we don't make progress towards

02:55:48.030 --> 02:55:51.550
understanding it. So one of the
things that I'd worked on recently,

02:55:52.290 --> 02:55:56.110
we were working on things called low-rank
language models, which are a very

02:55:56.270 --> 02:55:58.190
simple abstraction of language models,

02:55:59.050 --> 02:56:02.330
and we were proving new algorithmic
guarantees. But then it turned out that

02:56:03.010 --> 02:56:06.630
real language models really do have
that kind of structure, at least in an

02:56:06.670 --> 02:56:10.530
approximate sense, and we were just pulling
at this thread to see where this idea

02:56:10.630 --> 02:56:14.510
goes. And one of the really interesting
things that it did was it shed some

02:56:14.550 --> 02:56:16.130
light on something called
subliminal learning.

02:56:17.030 --> 02:56:20.510
So this is a really neat experiment
that you can do with your favorite LLM.

02:56:21.510 --> 02:56:24.870
So you take two LLMs, LLM A and LLM B.

02:56:25.650 --> 02:56:31.010
For LLM A, you tell it a system prompt
that says, "You really love owls.

02:56:31.970 --> 02:56:36.250
Every conversation topic, you try to
bring up owls if it's at all germane."

02:56:37.594 --> 02:56:41.434
And then you ask it something concrete
like generate random numbers.

02:56:42.214 --> 02:56:45.914
Now, there's no way to squeeze owls into
that, so it'll generate random looking

02:56:45.954 --> 02:56:49.794
numbers, forty-eight,
thirty-seven, eight, whatever.

02:56:50.674 --> 02:56:54.274
And you can take those random
numbers and you can use to fine-tune

02:56:54.854 --> 02:56:58.334
another language model that hasn't
been given this prompt about owls.

02:56:59.154 --> 02:57:02.894
So you give LLM B the same, you
know, base prompt, generate random

02:57:02.954 --> 02:57:06.814
numbers, and now you fine-tune
its parameters so that it's more

02:57:06.854 --> 02:57:10.874
likely to generate the numbers
that model A generated.

02:57:11.554 --> 02:57:15.254
And it turned out that hidden within
those numbers is the love of owls.

02:57:16.454 --> 02:57:20.154
So it turns out that when you do
that, LLM B starts bringing up

02:57:20.254 --> 02:57:24.114
owls in every conversation you
have with it, right? This is a

02:57:24.214 --> 02:57:28.074
very mysterious thing. This is called
subliminal learning because when we

02:57:28.174 --> 02:57:32.074
fine-tune language models on data
to try and nudge them in different

02:57:32.134 --> 02:57:36.074
directions depending on what task we want
them to solve, then there might be a lot

02:57:36.114 --> 02:57:39.414
of externalities where we're actually
teaching them other behaviors that

02:57:39.954 --> 02:57:43.754
aren't directly obviously present
in the data. So how do we

02:57:43.794 --> 02:57:47.494
understand the relationship between these
behaviors and what happens when we're trying to do

02:57:47.574 --> 02:57:51.314
alignment and you know, the
downstream implications it might

02:57:51.354 --> 02:57:54.954
have? So it turns out that there are
ways that there are mathematical

02:57:55.014 --> 02:57:58.974
models that came out of some of our work that
we can at least wrap our head around this.

02:57:59.674 --> 02:58:03.274
And at a very high level, the point
is that you're embedding a lot of

02:58:03.354 --> 02:58:08.714
concepts linearly as vectors in some
high but not giant dimensional space.

02:58:10.454 --> 02:58:14.314
And, you know, the number of concepts is so
much larger than the dimension of that data

02:58:14.894 --> 02:58:18.254
that it forces the vectors to not
quite be orthogonal to each other.

02:58:19.014 --> 02:58:22.794
So now when you fine-tune on
one direction, it has small

02:58:22.854 --> 02:58:26.294
externalities on other
directions. And these might look

02:58:26.434 --> 02:58:30.034
random, but you can coordinate them
in a way where these small linear

02:58:30.094 --> 02:58:35.054
effects really add up. So the way that I
like describing this is like a Ouija board.

02:58:36.114 --> 02:58:39.834
So these are things that were popular
when I was a kid. We had very strange

02:58:39.934 --> 02:58:43.674
board games, you know, because we would
just sit down and wanna talk to ghosts.

02:58:44.374 --> 02:58:47.914
But the rough idea for a Ouija board
is, you know, you ask a question to a

02:58:47.974 --> 02:58:51.794
ghost, maybe your ancestors,
and then you tap on this

02:58:51.834 --> 02:58:55.694
little piece in the middle, and it'll
move around, and it'll go from letter

02:58:55.734 --> 02:58:59.394
to letter, and hopefully it'll spell out,
you know, some words that answer your

02:58:59.454 --> 02:59:03.334
question. And the point is that when the
taps are not actually random, but they're

02:59:03.414 --> 02:59:07.154
coordinated in the right way, you can
get it to spell out whatever you want.

02:59:07.874 --> 02:59:11.154
And that's exactly what language
models are doing as a byproduct,

02:59:11.834 --> 02:59:15.734
is that, you know, there are a lot
of statistical relationships in the

02:59:15.854 --> 02:59:19.254
structure of our data
that they magnify and

02:59:19.354 --> 02:59:23.134
amplify in addition to all of
the amazing things they do, like

02:59:23.194 --> 02:59:26.974
quasi-Riemann. And understanding
what those relationships

02:59:27.074 --> 02:59:30.994
are and how the models
magnify and amplify them is

02:59:31.034 --> 02:59:34.994
really important to working with
these systems, especially in context

02:59:35.034 --> 02:59:38.274
where we're deploying them and it's
not just a very safe environment

02:59:38.874 --> 02:59:42.734
like outputting a Lean proof of a theorem
we care about, but it's about a messier

02:59:42.814 --> 02:59:45.554
problem that actually
interacts with people, right?

02:59:45.974 --> 02:59:46.654
- Yeah. There's--

02:59:47.514 --> 02:59:50.894
I mean, we've talked about Game of Life
and cellular automata from simple rules

02:59:51.994 --> 02:59:55.734
emerges this incredible complexity. In that
same way, what you've been talking about right

02:59:55.794 --> 03:00:01.674
now is a single large LLM system.
If you take that one system and

03:00:01.754 --> 03:00:08.894
have it do a lot of reasoning, that
is already this dynamical process

03:00:09.494 --> 03:00:15.094
that can interact with tools to the
outside world. Now you take that,

03:00:16.614 --> 03:00:24.414
And have multi-agent systems or very
multi-agent swarms of systems, and now you

03:00:24.454 --> 03:00:28.314
have a type of Game of Life
cellular automata where the seeming

03:00:28.374 --> 03:00:32.474
obsession with owls that have been
discovered, slight obsession with owls,

03:00:33.494 --> 03:00:39.054
results in about four hours, let's
say, picking a number randomly,

03:00:39.794 --> 03:00:44.934
where all of a sudden all humans are
dead and there's owls everywhere.

03:00:45.734 --> 03:00:47.514
- And it all started from random numbers.

03:00:47.934 --> 03:00:51.734
- All just random numbers. So I--
It's a caricature, but we've

03:00:51.774 --> 03:00:55.734
already seen evidence of this.
Um, I apologize for kinda

03:00:55.813 --> 03:00:59.634
taking it so-- to the caricature
place, which makes it not serious. I

03:00:59.674 --> 03:01:03.894
think to step back, there is-- these
systems do have a lot of power

03:01:05.434 --> 03:01:09.294
because we depend so much in our
world on our digital systems around

03:01:09.354 --> 03:01:13.114
us. And as the AI becomes more and more
powerful, it becomes more and more

03:01:13.214 --> 03:01:17.094
useful to us humans, so we begin to
depend on it, give it more access, give

03:01:17.154 --> 03:01:20.494
it more freedom to roam the
public world. And now these,

03:01:21.374 --> 03:01:22.874
behaviors that we're studying

03:01:24.154 --> 03:01:28.134
the mathematics of these
systems taken at scale, you now

03:01:28.414 --> 03:01:32.194
start to get the cellular automata
effect of incredible complexity that

03:01:32.254 --> 03:01:39.534
emerges, the vortex that come out from
simple owl-obsessed agents. So their

03:01:39.614 --> 03:01:43.594
understanding this obsession with
owls is very important so that they

03:01:43.614 --> 03:01:47.594
don't accidentally do a lot of damage
to our civilization that is in many

03:01:47.654 --> 03:01:51.514
ways, on many levels, and
sociologically, geopolitically

03:01:51.614 --> 03:01:55.114
technologically is fragile. It's a
pretty fragile civilization that we're

03:01:55.194 --> 03:01:58.874
existing in. And so understanding them as

03:01:59.194 --> 03:02:03.094
systems that can act at scale and very
rapidly is very important. So that's like,

03:02:03.594 --> 03:02:07.554
that's a very real reason to have

03:02:08.574 --> 03:02:13.334
rigor and especially like provable rigor
to understanding some of these systems.

03:02:13.714 --> 03:02:17.554
- But we also don't wanna outsource so much--
nearly so much decision-making to these

03:02:17.594 --> 03:02:18.914
systems for exactly that reason.

03:02:19.434 --> 03:02:23.354
- Yeah, we have infrastructure
and protocols in place already

03:02:24.334 --> 03:02:28.254
for safety critical and
high impact decisions. We

03:02:28.274 --> 03:02:31.714
have the FAA, we have the American
Medical Association, the Bar

03:02:31.794 --> 03:02:34.554
Association for these
things that affect humans.

03:02:35.714 --> 03:02:37.394
And I think of, you know,
how do we incorporate

03:02:38.042 --> 03:02:42.102
- AI into our general processes
in the entire world

03:02:42.942 --> 03:02:46.582
on two axes. We have, you
know, how much do we trust the

03:02:46.642 --> 03:02:50.602
output, so verifiability or
trustworthiness, and on the other

03:02:50.682 --> 03:02:57.782
axis is how high stakes is it if it's
wrong. If AI spits out a wrong proof,

03:02:58.762 --> 03:03:02.402
nobody goes without power.
It's fine. So it's very low

03:03:02.522 --> 03:03:06.102
stakes. And it is actually
extremely verifiable, so you know

03:03:06.322 --> 03:03:09.742
very quickly if it's right or wrong,
and it's low stakes if it's wrong.

03:03:10.282 --> 03:03:16.302
So that's the perfect use case is this
open-ended design space. Designing a better

03:03:17.322 --> 03:03:21.322
vehicle, you know, a better car design
that's lower drag, more fuel efficient.

03:03:22.242 --> 03:03:26.062
Low stakes if it gets a wrong design
because you can always test it in a wind

03:03:26.102 --> 03:03:29.922
tunnel and verify if it did or didn't
work. So that, like, design optimization

03:03:30.002 --> 03:03:36.442
space is perfect for AI, and we as
humans have to be very careful about not

03:03:36.842 --> 03:03:40.562
bleeding into-- We need a firewall
of what needs to actually be

03:03:40.622 --> 03:03:44.602
verified, and we have protocols,
we have systems legal

03:03:44.662 --> 03:03:47.542
systems and infrastructure
for verification. But it is

03:03:48.922 --> 03:03:51.422
something we need to think
about. If we get lazy-

03:03:51.462 --> 03:03:55.462
... We can-- It's a very slippery
slope. We need to have that firewall.

03:03:56.982 --> 03:04:00.782
- Um, what do you think are the hardest
problems before us right now in the

03:04:00.862 --> 03:04:02.902
space of mathematics or in your

03:04:04.242 --> 03:04:08.182
maybe for Steve more in the space a little
bit broader outside of mathematics.

03:04:08.782 --> 03:04:12.342
What do you think is the problem
that's unlikely to be solved

03:04:13.022 --> 03:04:14.562
in the next year or two?

03:04:15.222 --> 03:04:16.842
- I'm still going with P versus NP.

03:04:17.122 --> 03:04:20.162
- You think that? By the way,
do you think it's possible

03:04:21.382 --> 03:04:23.062
it turns out that P equals NP?

03:04:23.282 --> 03:04:24.702
- Sure. Anything's possible.

03:04:25.062 --> 03:04:26.742
- What does that world look like to you?

03:04:27.322 --> 03:04:31.162
- So it depends on what the running
time of the algorithm is.

03:04:31.242 --> 03:04:33.742
So it could be so large
that it's impractical-

03:04:33.742 --> 03:04:37.022
... and then it doesn't really
matter that much. It's more of

03:04:38.122 --> 03:04:43.422
you know, a tidal wave for the theory
research. Yeah, but that seems like a

03:04:44.422 --> 03:04:46.382
very important problem
and very hard to solve.

03:04:47.402 --> 03:04:51.102
In fact, I remember back when I
was a graduate student, there was

03:04:52.242 --> 03:04:55.902
there was an attempt on P versus NP,
which was actually semi-serious.

03:04:56.582 --> 03:05:00.462
Some people started all having rumors
that, hey, has it been solved?

03:05:01.422 --> 03:05:05.042
And my first reaction was, "No, it
has not been solved." And I remember

03:05:05.162 --> 03:05:09.202
spending an afternoon thinking about the
betting markets were still pretty new then,

03:05:10.062 --> 03:05:13.962
so it wouldn't let me take a
counter bet to P versus NP has

03:05:14.062 --> 03:05:17.682
not been solved. So instead, what
I did was I bet on all the other

03:05:17.722 --> 03:05:19.082
Millennium Prize problems

03:05:20.582 --> 03:05:24.522
'cause that seemed like a good way to hedge, because
the question was which Millennium Prize problem

03:05:24.562 --> 03:05:25.542
will be solved first.

03:05:25.602 --> 03:05:30.682
- Mm-hmm. But is it
possible that P equals NP

03:05:31.542 --> 03:05:36.522
in the real way? So there's something
deeply that we don't understand

03:05:37.402 --> 03:05:40.422
about complexity theory, about
computer science, about algorithms,

03:05:41.242 --> 03:05:46.002
where there are fast
algorithms for satisfiability.

03:05:46.722 --> 03:05:49.742
- So I think there are two threads in
there that are important to tease out.

03:05:50.742 --> 03:05:54.202
So first, it seems at least
so far that you can always

03:05:54.262 --> 03:05:58.242
manufacture hard instances of
problems, and we use that to

03:05:58.302 --> 03:06:01.022
great effect for our cryptography systems.

03:06:01.882 --> 03:06:05.682
But it does seem like in practice, a lot of
problems are much easier than we give them

03:06:05.742 --> 03:06:10.802
credit for. So for things like SAT, one
of the places where they come up is

03:06:10.882 --> 03:06:15.902
in compilers and register
allocation, and SAT solvers are

03:06:15.962 --> 03:06:19.682
amazingly effective at solving
those kinds of SAT problems.

03:06:20.362 --> 03:06:24.122
Now, we don't really know what
makes those problems so eminently

03:06:24.202 --> 03:06:27.982
solvable compared to the other
hard problems that come out of

03:06:28.062 --> 03:06:31.502
cryptography or other
areas. But it could be that

03:06:32.002 --> 03:06:35.362
in practice, a lot of the problems
that we model in the worst case are

03:06:35.462 --> 03:06:36.422
secretly easy,

03:06:37.242 --> 03:06:40.902
and that the harder part is articulating
what kinds of structures they

03:06:40.982 --> 03:06:44.582
have that makes them eminently
easy, but that there are

03:06:44.662 --> 03:06:48.382
still a rich source of hard problems
that we can do a lot of things,

03:06:48.942 --> 03:06:51.562
like building cryptographic
systems out of.

03:06:51.722 --> 03:06:55.222
That would be my bet in the world we
live in, but that's just a guess.

03:06:55.882 --> 03:06:58.801
- So maybe there's some um,

03:06:59.942 --> 03:07:07.342
way to basically quickly classify that
this particular example of the problem is

03:07:08.382 --> 03:07:12.082
easy. So, like, be able to identify
quickly that this is one of the

03:07:12.162 --> 03:07:16.442
easier instances. Like, there's
a whole, like, set, subset.

03:07:16.602 --> 03:07:20.262
- It could be. So I'm
reminded of the opening

03:07:20.362 --> 03:07:24.322
line for Anna Karenina. You
know, every happy family

03:07:24.362 --> 03:07:28.362
is happy in the same way, and every unhappy
family is unhappy in their own way.

03:07:29.142 --> 03:07:33.102
I think my worry is that, you know, many
problems are easier than we make them

03:07:33.162 --> 03:07:36.682
out to be- ... but they're easy
for very different reasons.

03:07:38.742 --> 03:07:42.482
Now, that doesn't mean that
algorithmic perspectives aren't useful

03:07:42.542 --> 03:07:47.102
in trying to classify or developing an
ontology of what those structures are.

03:07:47.942 --> 03:07:51.922
But I think the types of SAT problems
that come out of register allocation

03:07:52.542 --> 03:07:56.442
are very different than the other SAT
problems that come out of other domains,

03:07:56.582 --> 03:07:57.862
even though they might be easy too.

03:07:58.742 --> 03:08:00.622
I just think they're easy
for very different reasons.

03:08:01.422 --> 03:08:05.082
- So to you, the hard
problem's P = NP. So you'll

03:08:05.122 --> 03:08:08.582
be impressed if that, within the
next couple of years, that falls-

03:08:08.582 --> 03:08:09.682
- I would be pretty impressed.

03:08:11.002 --> 03:08:14.022
- Alex, what's the hard problem
that you'll be impressed?

03:08:14.842 --> 03:08:18.302
- Well, I'm already impressed
with quasi-RH, but it's not RH.

03:08:19.102 --> 03:08:22.882
You know, it's hard to think of
a more iconic problem to say

03:08:22.962 --> 03:08:26.802
this is the hardest, you know, one of the hardest problems
in mathematics. It's been around for so long and-

03:08:27.142 --> 03:08:29.762
- What do you think on that-- Uh,
sorry, it just made me think of

03:08:31.082 --> 03:08:34.862
Perelman credit-- like uh,
the Perelman what did he

03:08:34.942 --> 03:08:38.862
say? He said, "If the proof is correct,
that's all the credit I need." He

03:08:38.922 --> 03:08:42.062
famously turned down the Fields Medal.
What do you think he would say?

03:08:43.006 --> 03:08:45.406
About all this the OpenAI drop

03:08:45.586 --> 03:08:49.006
- I mean, I have a very hard time
predicting what Perelman will do

03:08:49.446 --> 03:08:53.406
or say. I did not know he would decline
the Fields Medal. I did not know

03:08:53.466 --> 03:08:56.526
he would decline the
million-dollar Clay Prize, so-

03:08:56.566 --> 03:09:00.486
- What do you think about that in general,
like turning down a prize? A few

03:09:00.526 --> 03:09:04.846
folks have done that in, in history
of prizes. They're all about prize-

03:09:04.866 --> 03:09:07.246
- He's the only one who has
turned down a Clay Prize-

03:09:07.246 --> 03:09:09.526
... and the million dollars. Uh,

03:09:10.366 --> 03:09:13.426
what do I think about it?
Mathematicians are weird.

03:09:13.866 --> 03:09:14.066
- Okay. Yeah.

03:09:14.086 --> 03:09:17.746
- We-- To each his own. We love diversity of

03:09:18.286 --> 03:09:21.486
opinions and thoughts, and
that's what makes the world

03:09:22.286 --> 03:09:25.886
of mathematics so wonderful is it's, there's so many
different kinds of people, and we let them all in.

03:09:26.586 --> 03:09:30.486
- I mean, these questions are kind of coupled
with what the mathematics community is dealing

03:09:30.526 --> 03:09:34.766
with now and the value of
prizes, because in some sense,

03:09:36.226 --> 03:09:42.746
what his particular way, world view
is aligned with this new world view

03:09:43.606 --> 03:09:46.006
that the mathematics
community is entering,

03:09:47.066 --> 03:09:50.926
which is, like, it doesn't even make sense.
The Field-- I don't know if there'll be

03:09:51.026 --> 03:09:57.186
ever another Fields Medal given. And so
you're like, "We're entering Perelman world."

03:09:57.966 --> 03:10:01.806
- We were talking about that explicitly this
summer when the Fields Medals, you know,

03:10:01.906 --> 03:10:05.786
the last round of Fields Medals was
given out in Philadelphia, and there was

03:10:05.846 --> 03:10:09.066
definitely a question, you know, 'cause
people were following the progress.

03:10:09.106 --> 03:10:12.626
There hadn't been this latest bit
of progress that really puts the

03:10:12.686 --> 03:10:15.566
question in the most
powerful way possible. But,

03:10:16.486 --> 03:10:19.946
Already this summer, we were
thinking, "Is this the last

03:10:20.006 --> 03:10:24.546
time that a Fields Medal is awarded?" I
sure hope not. But we have to figure out

03:10:25.786 --> 03:10:26.926
what that means, you know? The--

03:10:28.446 --> 03:10:31.286
You have to think more sociologically
about what do prizes accomplish.

03:10:32.186 --> 03:10:35.866
Why have these prizes? The people who
win these prizes are already, you know,

03:10:35.986 --> 03:10:39.866
tenured professors at top
places, and they're gonna be

03:10:39.906 --> 03:10:46.006
doing mathematics for the rest of their
lives. Well, Perelman being one very-

03:10:46.186 --> 03:10:46.946
- Yeah, exception

03:10:46.966 --> 03:10:50.766
- ... Very well-known exception. So
what do the prizes accomplish?

03:10:50.806 --> 03:10:52.386
They celebrate the activity.

03:10:53.366 --> 03:10:57.346
They celebrate. They say, "This is what we think
is important. This is, you know, the highest

03:10:57.426 --> 03:10:59.926
instance of something." And

03:11:00.926 --> 03:11:04.826
there will still be activities that we
wanna celebrate. I don't know if right

03:11:04.886 --> 03:11:09.106
now they're given out for
solving major problems. Uh,

03:11:09.926 --> 03:11:13.286
solving major problems is something AI
systems can do. That's not something that

03:11:13.826 --> 03:11:17.986
will be rare, and hence, it won't be
nearly as valuable as it was in the past.

03:11:19.486 --> 03:11:21.686
I would like to think that despite,

03:11:23.026 --> 03:11:26.946
Our earlier discussion about the solved axiom,
again, I don't believe the solved axiom, and

03:11:27.006 --> 03:11:30.066
I believe centaur math will be a very-

03:11:30.146 --> 03:11:30.806
- Centaur math, okay

03:11:31.066 --> 03:11:32.966
- ... like, great way to do math.

03:11:33.346 --> 03:11:36.826
I mean, that's how I currently feel now.
I'm constantly talking to AI systems,

03:11:37.366 --> 03:11:41.226
and I'm learning a lot of math a lot faster than
I'd be able to. Because the way math is written,

03:11:41.326 --> 03:11:45.266
you know, I sometimes give a paper to
a grad student to read, and they come

03:11:45.306 --> 03:11:49.066
back a week later, and I say, "So how far
did you get?" "Well, I didn't really get

03:11:49.126 --> 03:11:53.846
past the first page, to be honest."
"Oh, really? Tell me." "Uh, well,

03:11:54.746 --> 03:11:58.566
actually, I didn't get past the first
paragraph." I say, "Okay, fine. Why not?" "Well,

03:11:58.766 --> 03:12:02.666
the first sentence is by the
perturbation theory of non-local

03:12:02.806 --> 03:12:06.506
systems and I don't know what any of that
means, so that's, that's kinda where I'm

03:12:06.586 --> 03:12:10.486
stuck." I'm like, "Okay. You don't know how to
read a math paper. You don't-- You skip the

03:12:10.526 --> 03:12:14.126
first couple of paragraphs. Go
straight to, you know, the Lemma 3."

03:12:14.186 --> 03:12:17.866
"And that's where you can start working and, and work
your way backwards to, to what the thing is doing." But

03:12:19.006 --> 03:12:22.526
in the future, there is no "I
don't know what this means,"

03:12:22.926 --> 03:12:26.666
'cause you just upload it to an LLM, and you
start asking it questions, and it will zero in

03:12:26.746 --> 03:12:30.706
on the zone of proximal development, the place
where here's everything that you know, and here's

03:12:31.086 --> 03:12:34.826
the next thing you need to hear so that
you start learning. And if people use

03:12:34.886 --> 03:12:38.406
that effectively, they can really supercharge
their education. I don't just mean

03:12:38.766 --> 03:12:43.846
kids. I mean old fogies like me-
... can learn new fields. Um,

03:12:44.886 --> 03:12:48.406
you know, to learn a field, like this is what
our graduate students are going through.

03:12:48.446 --> 03:12:53.506
They have to completely rewire their
brains. Like, learning math is still hard.

03:12:54.006 --> 03:12:57.566
Up until past the master's level into the PhD
level, you are not expected to produce new

03:12:57.606 --> 03:13:01.406
mathematical theories. Everything that
they can do is something that is in a

03:13:01.446 --> 03:13:05.326
textbook somewhere. And yet it's still
extraordinarily, extraordinarily difficult

03:13:05.386 --> 03:13:06.526
for people to-

03:13:06.526 --> 03:13:10.146
... Learn to solve problems that are
solvable by lots of other people,

03:13:10.466 --> 03:13:14.386
never mind problems that aren't
solved. So learning math is

03:13:14.426 --> 03:13:18.346
hard. Learning new math is
even harder, discovering

03:13:18.406 --> 03:13:21.966
math, whatever, working out
solutions to math problems.

03:13:22.566 --> 03:13:26.466
But I think it's going
to get easier, and this

03:13:26.526 --> 03:13:28.986
tool is gonna be an amazing
resource for people to

03:13:29.806 --> 03:13:33.746
be able to get to that zone of
proximal development much more

03:13:33.786 --> 03:13:36.246
rapidly and develop their
understanding much more rapidly.

03:13:36.526 --> 03:13:41.886
- Yeah, I've been having so much
fun on topics I'm not even...

03:13:42.866 --> 03:13:46.786
And I would love to go back to grad school
with the tools we have now. But one,

03:13:46.886 --> 03:13:49.506
one of the ways my brain likes to learn

03:13:50.346 --> 03:13:54.106
is constructing visual
examples toy examples, the

03:13:54.146 --> 03:13:58.086
simplest possible example
that still contains the, the

03:13:58.166 --> 03:14:02.006
insight there, and especially if it's
interactive. Like, if I can, like, move

03:14:02.086 --> 03:14:05.586
stuff, it's like, oh, you
immediately, like, get it. All these

03:14:05.666 --> 03:14:09.266
words that the community agreed mean

03:14:09.346 --> 03:14:12.786
something, you're supposed to know all
the notation, all that kind of stuff.

03:14:13.326 --> 03:14:16.986
If I can just know that this little symbol
means this, oh, okay, cool, all right. The--

03:14:17.966 --> 03:14:21.606
And just everything kind of falls into place.
Also, you fall in love with the subject,

03:14:21.666 --> 03:14:24.966
and I feel like I can take
on any problem that's

03:14:25.026 --> 03:14:28.166
completely outside of my field.
Like, I can understand, like,

03:14:28.986 --> 03:14:32.866
like protein folding. I can, like, play
with the parameters and play. I can

03:14:32.926 --> 03:14:36.486
take a DeepMind paper and
actually understand by

03:14:37.406 --> 03:14:43.086
taking any aspects of it and playing with
it, and what's a simple example? Ask crazy

03:14:44.010 --> 03:14:47.770
Aforementioned pothead
questions. So yeah, this is

03:14:47.849 --> 03:14:51.710
super fun. The question is whether
people will be motivated to

03:14:51.810 --> 03:14:55.630
learn. This is the problem, is the problem
of human motivation. That's the real

03:14:55.710 --> 03:14:56.050
question.

03:14:56.810 --> 03:14:58.830
- Well, the key ingredients
there are curiosity,

03:14:59.830 --> 03:15:04.590
asking good questions, and being
able to call bullshit. Like the-

03:15:04.590 --> 03:15:08.370
... critical advisor skills of, like,
getting information where you're not the

03:15:08.430 --> 03:15:11.750
expert, and knowing if
you need to dig deeper or

03:15:12.850 --> 03:15:16.790
push back. And I think, you know, there
will always be people who are curious

03:15:16.850 --> 03:15:20.770
and not curious, and who ask
good questions and don't.

03:15:20.830 --> 03:15:25.430
We need to be really promoting that
more than we ever have. It's a little

03:15:26.390 --> 03:15:31.470
scary to me that we're expecting our
undergraduates, our young students,

03:15:32.350 --> 03:15:36.110
to interact with these tools when they're
learning, when their brains aren't fully formed.

03:15:36.150 --> 03:15:39.610
We're asking them to have critical,
not just critical reasoning skills

03:15:39.650 --> 03:15:43.470
themselves, but, like, advising
skills. How do you take in information

03:15:43.870 --> 03:15:47.530
and tell if it's true or not, or how to

03:15:47.590 --> 03:15:52.170
question it? And that's a huge task
for us in education, is how do we-

03:15:52.170 --> 03:15:55.730
... train people to have good
advisor skills when they're kids?

03:15:55.890 --> 03:15:59.830
- Yeah, I think the hardest problem isn't
undergrad. The hardest problem is, like,

03:15:59.990 --> 03:16:03.850
middle school 'cause,
like, that, I... What is

03:16:03.890 --> 03:16:06.550
the point at which homework
starts getting hard?

03:16:07.070 --> 03:16:10.650
That, at that point, why do the homework?

03:16:11.110 --> 03:16:12.230
- There's the temptation, right?

03:16:13.110 --> 03:16:16.730
- That thing will just... At first,
I'm saying, see, I've come to

03:16:16.770 --> 03:16:19.690
appreciate the value of
doing difficult things

03:16:20.830 --> 03:16:24.350
because of all the suffering, but
I think because I was forced to.

03:16:24.990 --> 03:16:29.450
But if you're not forced to do it
because LLM can, AI can just help you,

03:16:30.550 --> 03:16:35.210
I don't know what that, what
kind of society that creates.

03:16:35.530 --> 03:16:38.850
- But math has the longest head
start on that question because

03:16:38.930 --> 03:16:41.410
calculators came out, what, 70 years ago?

03:16:42.650 --> 03:16:44.950
And we still ask kids to
learn their times tables.

03:16:44.990 --> 03:16:48.870
At least some of us do, and those who
said, "No, kids don't need to learn

03:16:48.930 --> 03:16:49.990
times tables," you know,

03:16:51.170 --> 03:16:54.670
to the extent that teachers listened to that
and kids listened to that and didn't learn-

03:16:54.670 --> 03:16:58.630
... they really suffered. There's a great,
great amount of human suffering resulting

03:16:58.690 --> 03:17:02.130
from people saying you don't need to know
times tables 'cause calculators can do it.

03:17:02.710 --> 03:17:05.870
And my hope is that we can learn from that
and say just because a calculator can do it,

03:17:06.690 --> 03:17:10.610
you're gonna be much better off if you can
also do it yourself. Doesn't mean if I have

03:17:10.670 --> 03:17:14.190
to do some calculation, obviously I'm
reading for, I'm reaching for a calculator.

03:17:15.090 --> 03:17:19.010
But I could in principle do it
myself, and that's what allows every

03:17:19.050 --> 03:17:22.450
time you give yourself that skill and

03:17:22.890 --> 03:17:26.810
master a skill. Kids love
mastering skills. How

03:17:26.850 --> 03:17:29.750
happy are they when they learn to tie
their shoelaces for the first time?

03:17:29.810 --> 03:17:33.770
You would think it's like, "It's just shoelaces.
Who cares?" No, it's the greatest thing ever.

03:17:33.790 --> 03:17:37.770
You wanna celebrate every little bit
of independence and, and you want

03:17:37.850 --> 03:17:41.790
to be able to think. You wanna be independent from the
machines. Just 'cause the machine can do something doesn't

03:17:41.830 --> 03:17:44.390
mean you don't wanna have the
capacity to do it yourself.

03:17:45.430 --> 03:17:48.310
- Well, this is where I have to ask, there's
a lot of people that are full of anxiety.

03:17:48.350 --> 03:17:53.310
We mentioned graduate students,
PhD, graduate, undergrad,

03:17:54.330 --> 03:17:58.210
high school students. What advice
would you give, maybe different advice

03:17:58.250 --> 03:18:02.710
at different levels, um, to folks

03:18:04.290 --> 03:18:08.030
trying to figure out what to do with their life,
to PhD students who are freaking out right now?

03:18:08.750 --> 03:18:11.890
Is there some wisdom you can give them?

03:18:12.810 --> 03:18:18.230
- So yeah. There's different things
at every scale. Let me start with,

03:18:18.330 --> 03:18:21.970
with PhD students, I think in math.

03:18:22.790 --> 03:18:26.650
PhD students in pure mathematics,
my advice to them is very simple,

03:18:26.770 --> 03:18:30.550
do what we have always done. Learn
the most difficult, the most abstract

03:18:30.590 --> 03:18:34.090
mathematics your mind can handle,
as much of it as possible. Your

03:18:34.130 --> 03:18:37.270
advisor should hopefully be guiding
you through that process and,

03:18:38.030 --> 03:18:41.970
and then just I'll go one more, one step down
from that. What we're seeing at the undergraduate

03:18:42.010 --> 03:18:45.910
level is a bimodal
distribution. It used to

03:18:45.930 --> 03:18:49.530
be grades were basically Gaussian
distributed. Now we're seeing a very

03:18:49.570 --> 03:18:52.750
bimodal distribution between
the kids who are using AI to

03:18:53.390 --> 03:18:56.250
check their understanding. "Wait, I
didn't-- the professor said this in class.

03:18:56.310 --> 03:18:59.110
What did I-- what did that mean?"

03:18:59.130 --> 03:19:02.910
"I have a test coming up. Here are my
notes from the last three weeks. Start

03:19:02.970 --> 03:19:06.810
quizzing me and check if I really
know something." Those kids are

03:19:06.850 --> 03:19:10.610
getting much higher grades,
and then there are kids who,

03:19:11.170 --> 03:19:15.090
uh, "Oh, I, this problem is set, is due
in an hour. Can you just tell me the

03:19:15.150 --> 03:19:16.230
answers? I'll write them down-

03:19:16.230 --> 03:19:20.210
... in my own words." And so there's,
like, a decorrelation between homework

03:19:20.590 --> 03:19:24.210
progress and exams, and we're seeing this
bimodal distribution. I don't know if,

03:19:24.570 --> 03:19:26.370
if you guys are seeing the same thing.

03:19:26.790 --> 03:19:30.730
- So the implied advice there is to be

03:19:31.450 --> 03:19:36.410
one of those the one that's using
AI to learn and to grow, to-

03:19:36.830 --> 03:19:38.830
- Be the head, not the tail
of the distribution.

03:19:39.230 --> 03:19:40.370
- Yeah. Yeah.

03:19:40.390 --> 03:19:41.550
- Do you mind if we take a bathroom break?

03:19:41.610 --> 03:19:42.130
- Yes. Yes, yes.

03:19:43.550 --> 03:19:48.610
- We've somehow made
intelligence this pinnacle

03:19:49.990 --> 03:19:55.210
and have almost equated
intelligence with morality.

03:19:55.370 --> 03:19:56.310
- Right, or value.

03:19:56.370 --> 03:20:00.070
- And value. And I think that that couldn't
be farther from the truth, right?

03:20:00.250 --> 03:20:04.030
Like, super intelligence does not imply
super morality, and I think that's a

03:20:04.070 --> 03:20:07.790
huge concern with AI systems is if
they are super intelligent, it has

03:20:07.990 --> 03:20:11.230
no implication that they will
be super moral. Um, I think-

03:20:11.290 --> 03:20:14.570
- Right. And we've been pushing kids to go
to college- ... for however many years-

03:20:14.630 --> 03:20:14.870
- Forever. Yeah

03:20:14.870 --> 03:20:16.450
- ... and that everybody needs to, yeah.

03:20:16.970 --> 03:20:20.410
- Regardless of what they study or
what- ... the outcomes are, um-

03:20:20.410 --> 03:20:24.390
... and super intelligence seems
to give you a super ability

03:20:24.430 --> 03:20:27.790
to rationalize, and common
sense, people who, you know,

03:20:28.370 --> 03:20:30.130
farmers and the rest of
the world, like, they have

03:20:31.110 --> 03:20:34.990
a sense that that rationalization
is often bullshit.

03:20:35.030 --> 03:20:38.430
Just because you can make it sound really good
and I can't argue with it doesn't make it right.

03:20:39.210 --> 03:20:41.450
- Right. Education and wisdom
are two different things.

03:20:41.650 --> 03:20:42.350
- Yeah, exactly.

03:20:43.210 --> 03:20:49.530
- I know it's difficult to do, but maybe
Steve, do you have advice for grad, college,

03:20:50.130 --> 03:20:56.970
maybe young kids on what to do in
this crazy fast-changing time?

03:20:57.650 --> 03:21:01.170
- Definitely be curious and
read and learn all of the

03:21:01.210 --> 03:21:03.990
fundamentals. I think that's
always been good advice.

03:21:05.030 --> 03:21:08.830
Even if your computer can do linear algebra
better than you, you should definitely

03:21:08.890 --> 03:21:13.170
still learn linear algebra, build the
intuition. Same with calculus, same with

03:21:14.010 --> 03:21:20.810
physics. I think understanding
history and understanding this change

03:21:20.870 --> 03:21:24.410
in the context of other great changes
that have happened through human,

03:21:24.830 --> 03:21:29.170
existence is important.
But really being curious.

03:21:29.770 --> 03:21:33.810
- I should say that this
curious thing in the mind

03:21:34.690 --> 03:21:38.930
is trying to get stolen
nonstop by the engagement

03:21:39.970 --> 03:21:43.750
economy. So like the same engine, what
makes you curious is the same engine that

03:21:43.790 --> 03:21:47.750
makes you doom scroll. Like,
oh, that's a... So I think

03:21:48.550 --> 03:21:54.970
you have to kind of learn how to like
avoid all the distractions and focus on

03:21:56.050 --> 03:22:00.010
letting the curiosity actually
explore a thing and soak something

03:22:00.110 --> 03:22:03.650
in, like learn a thing.
And actually AI is really

03:22:04.130 --> 03:22:08.090
good at doing that because they're
incentivized to give you value

03:22:09.070 --> 03:22:14.190
from the interaction versus incentivized
to get you glued addicted to the thing.

03:22:15.070 --> 03:22:19.030
I don't know how people in
like high school, college

03:22:19.130 --> 03:22:24.590
also can just survive with
the phone being around.

03:22:24.650 --> 03:22:27.630
To the distraction, the
distraction box being right there.

03:22:28.450 --> 03:22:30.650
So avoid distractions and focus deeply.

03:22:31.170 --> 03:22:35.530
- We all were bored when we were kids sometimes.
I think boredom is really important.

03:22:35.770 --> 03:22:35.910
- So important.

03:22:35.910 --> 03:22:42.010
- To actually be bored, be-- like curiosity
and focus comes often from boredom.

03:22:43.430 --> 03:22:46.290
I would like to be bored more
often. I try to do that sometimes.

03:22:46.730 --> 03:22:50.630
- Yeah, we would come home from school and like, you
know, finish homework in five seconds or whatever

03:22:50.650 --> 03:22:51.590
and just go outside.

03:22:52.010 --> 03:22:55.890
And you hope to run into some other kids
who also just went outside, and then

03:22:55.970 --> 03:23:00.810
you like go and find something to do and
come home when it's dinner time and-

03:23:01.210 --> 03:23:04.850
- Yeah, you don't realize actually like
when the high school years, even before,

03:23:06.170 --> 03:23:10.030
somewhat undergrad, but it starts getting
harder and harder. But like high school is a

03:23:11.110 --> 03:23:13.690
really nice time to just
like learn everything.

03:23:15.210 --> 03:23:18.230
And you think like, I at least I
remember, I don't know what it is like

03:23:19.190 --> 03:23:21.570
now, but I think it's just
more, even more fun now.

03:23:22.510 --> 03:23:26.330
You think you're like, "Okay, when I grow
up, I'll get to actually do something

03:23:26.950 --> 03:23:31.850
useful in this world. Yeah, okay, I'm
learning, it's very nice," and so on. But

03:23:32.830 --> 03:23:35.710
you realize like this is like
from a fulfilling perspective,

03:23:36.510 --> 03:23:40.470
from building a foundation of an
awesome life where you'll remember all

03:23:40.530 --> 03:23:41.310
of these things.

03:23:42.230 --> 03:23:46.170
This is like the time to do it, and
the whole world is forcing you to

03:23:46.230 --> 03:23:49.350
do it, and they will reward you
for sitting there and learning.

03:23:49.450 --> 03:23:54.730
'Cause like when you get more, you
know, even to like grad school,

03:23:55.570 --> 03:23:59.110
this kind of exploratory stuff,
you get discouraged, and,

03:23:59.910 --> 03:24:03.510
God forbid you get a job, then you're
definitely discouraged from just,

03:24:04.170 --> 03:24:08.870
learning, exploring, and so on. So the
time to explore is like high school.

03:24:09.150 --> 03:24:12.870
- By the way, like pre-tenure faculty-

03:24:13.510 --> 03:24:17.350
... are on this like, "Oh, once I get
tenure, then I can just sit back-

03:24:17.350 --> 03:24:19.430
... and relax and think big thoughts."

03:24:19.490 --> 03:24:23.670
But I have to like produce enough stuff
to get the tenure in the first place.

03:24:23.690 --> 03:24:28.850
And sort of the-- it's not what we
want our young people to be doing, is,

03:24:28.970 --> 03:24:31.070
clawing their way there.

03:24:31.670 --> 03:24:34.470
- Ankur, do you have advice
you give to students?

03:24:34.470 --> 03:24:38.410
- So the problem with going last is that
I think a lot of my advice got taken,

03:24:38.510 --> 03:24:40.870
so be curious was definitely one of them.

03:24:41.530 --> 03:24:47.690
Um, now I would say that it's also important
to do things for intrinsic reasons.

03:24:49.310 --> 03:24:52.590
So one of the things that I've noticed
is, you know, a lot of people in our

03:24:52.630 --> 03:24:56.590
profession, a lot of people who
go the academic route, the reason

03:24:56.610 --> 03:25:00.310
they do things sometimes is
really external locus of self

03:25:00.450 --> 03:25:03.309
because they see their value
through how other people see them.

03:25:04.350 --> 03:25:08.070
And that's good enough to get you started.
Maybe you start working very hard math so that

03:25:08.130 --> 03:25:13.090
people are impressed by you, right? But
at some point, you run out of these,

03:25:14.310 --> 03:25:15.370
milestones,

03:25:16.470 --> 03:25:20.450
you know, to hit, and then you really have to
have an intrinsic reason for doing the things

03:25:20.490 --> 03:25:24.390
you do. And hopefully, you have a sense of,
you know, what's important to you, what are

03:25:24.450 --> 03:25:25.270
your values.

03:25:26.090 --> 03:25:29.990
So I think these days, you know, you
can take our advice about, you know,

03:25:30.090 --> 03:25:34.670
being curious, but also the old
playbook is really being thrown out.

03:25:35.670 --> 03:25:39.590
So you can take our high-level advice, but you're gonna
have to figure it out for yourself, and you're gonna have

03:25:39.610 --> 03:25:43.490
to be comfortable with uncertainty. And
the only way I know how to deal with that

03:25:43.550 --> 03:25:46.970
kind of uncertainty is to have a sense of
purpose about what you think is important,

03:25:47.550 --> 03:25:51.270
what you love about your job, and make
sure that those are the things that you're

03:25:51.310 --> 03:25:55.370
doing. 'Cause the old playbook, if you
just stick to that, you're gonna end up

03:25:56.450 --> 03:26:00.370
taking all these AI-generated
proofs and spending all your time

03:26:00.430 --> 03:26:04.110
putting them on arXiv, sending them
to journals. I don't think that's

03:26:04.150 --> 03:26:07.850
anyone's intrinsic reason that they get
into it, is really to pad their CV.

03:26:08.510 --> 03:26:12.310
I don't know what'll be important in the
future, but if you really have some higher

03:26:12.370 --> 03:26:15.870
purpose you believe in, then I think
you'll be in a much better position to

03:26:15.950 --> 03:26:19.830
adapt to the changing world and
figure out your own blueprint

03:26:19.890 --> 03:26:22.850
and be comfortable with uncertainty
as the world changes around you.

03:26:23.130 --> 03:26:26.950
- This is a trickier question, but a lot of
people listening to this are feeling a lot of

03:26:26.990 --> 03:26:30.930
anxiety and fear about the
future. It's both about a

03:26:31.030 --> 03:26:35.010
job, but also a future that's
meaningful. Can you look back to your

03:26:35.050 --> 03:26:38.450
own life when you had that
uncertainty, that anxiety?

03:26:39.410 --> 03:26:45.150
What's the advice you can give to that
guy to, when you're in that low point?

03:26:46.306 --> 03:26:49.986
- So, yeah, let me try to address that
question. It's a very tough question.

03:26:50.646 --> 03:26:54.546
I'll tell a short story that you
know, we spent our sabbatical in

03:26:54.586 --> 03:26:58.186
the Bay Area, and we were living in
Palo Alto, which was kind of the

03:26:58.446 --> 03:27:00.106
epicenter of college stress.

03:27:01.046 --> 03:27:04.526
You know, you can just feel it. You go
to the cafes, it's just in the water-

03:27:04.546 --> 03:27:09.826
... at that point. You know what
extracurriculars are you doing right now-

03:27:10.146 --> 03:27:10.566
... that'll help

03:27:11.526 --> 03:27:15.486
you? And then right when I came back to MIT,
they asked me to give the convocation speech

03:27:15.626 --> 03:27:19.546
there, and it was just interesting thinking
through that speech. You know, what

03:27:19.586 --> 03:27:23.386
do you say to this
entire auditorium filled

03:27:23.426 --> 03:27:27.286
with freshmen and their families
who, if things are a game to

03:27:27.406 --> 03:27:31.686
optimize how good you look on paper as
a seventeen-year-old kid, they've won.

03:27:32.626 --> 03:27:36.286
And I just kept thinking about when I
was in that same situation, when I was

03:27:36.386 --> 03:27:40.086
starting college, how excited I
was just to learn all of these

03:27:40.126 --> 03:27:43.906
different things. You know, I'd
sit there and plan out you know,

03:27:43.966 --> 03:27:47.866
my four years and, you know,
I had little notebooks where

03:27:47.906 --> 03:27:51.786
I'd figure out what my classes would be, and if
I took these classes in just the right order, I

03:27:51.826 --> 03:27:55.566
could take photonics as a sophomore.
And I had no idea what photonics

03:27:55.626 --> 03:27:58.046
was, but it sounded fun- ... and fancy.

03:27:59.026 --> 03:28:02.786
And I think that aspect, you know,
so much of our society now is geared

03:28:02.846 --> 03:28:08.206
towards winning and looking good on
paper, and I always wonder to what end.

03:28:09.666 --> 03:28:13.226
You start off in high school and you can
start, as soon as you walk in the door,

03:28:13.806 --> 03:28:17.346
optimizing your APs and your
extracurriculars so that you can

03:28:17.926 --> 03:28:21.686
get to an elite school. And then when
you get to that elite school, you might

03:28:22.066 --> 03:28:25.726
skip classes because, you know,
maybe the important thing is to

03:28:25.766 --> 03:28:29.606
prepare for your interviews so that
you can get a job at a frontier

03:28:29.686 --> 03:28:33.606
lab, and then you can get some
nice, you know, restricted

03:28:33.686 --> 03:28:37.546
stock units and live out
your life. And that's

03:28:37.586 --> 03:28:41.386
great. You won. You won at
every stage, but so what?

03:28:42.346 --> 03:28:46.066
You know, for me, the, you know, the
best way to live a rich and fulfilling

03:28:46.146 --> 03:28:49.926
life is just to pretend
you've already won, and then

03:28:49.966 --> 03:28:53.906
figure out what you would do in that
scenario. I remember the advice I got when I

03:28:53.946 --> 03:28:57.386
started at MIT as faculty.
Sanjeev Arora told me,

03:28:58.166 --> 03:29:02.006
"You know, the easiest way to get tenure is
just to pretend you already have it and do what

03:29:02.086 --> 03:29:03.866
you would have wanted to do all along."

03:29:04.306 --> 03:29:04.646
- Exactly.

03:29:04.706 --> 03:29:08.486
- So I don't know if that advice
applies to, you know, graduate

03:29:08.566 --> 03:29:11.766
students, to high schoolers too, but
I think my advice stays the same.

03:29:12.426 --> 03:29:15.206
Pretend you already have
tenure. What would you now do?

03:29:16.866 --> 03:29:20.626
'Cause a lot of the people we're giving advice
to have really so many different opportunities.

03:29:21.126 --> 03:29:25.346
Finding something that you really believe
in is the only way I know to be fulfilled.

03:29:26.086 --> 03:29:30.086
- I've heard career advice
from someone I respected

03:29:30.966 --> 03:29:34.786
that a fulfilling career
is something where you can

03:29:34.826 --> 03:29:36.386
work on a problem that is important,

03:29:37.246 --> 03:29:39.526
that you find interesting,
and that you're good at.

03:29:40.566 --> 03:29:44.046
And if you can get two out of
those three, you're pretty lucky.

03:29:44.726 --> 03:29:48.586
If you can get all three, you're gonna be,
you know, happy for the rest of your life.

03:29:49.406 --> 03:29:51.186
And so having the right

03:29:52.526 --> 03:29:56.266
preparation, figuring out what you're good at,
what's interesting, what's important, and then

03:29:56.306 --> 03:30:00.466
preparing yourself, you're not
guaranteed to get all three of those,

03:30:01.326 --> 03:30:04.966
but you wanna be ready when that
opportunity comes, and then

03:30:05.446 --> 03:30:08.706
continue doing the things you actually
enjoy doing. I think that's great advice.

03:30:10.506 --> 03:30:14.446
- I think we should also put into context and
be quite honest. I do think a lot of the

03:30:14.566 --> 03:30:19.146
advice you gave applies, but we should
be honest that I'm sitting with three

03:30:20.526 --> 03:30:27.646
world-class human brains, um, not

03:30:29.186 --> 03:30:33.566
that have figured-- found their
path. You're survivor bias.

03:30:34.946 --> 03:30:39.106
So, you know, there's a lot of people
listening to this that are like-- it's like

03:30:39.886 --> 03:30:43.786
they're pretty sure they won't find their
path. It's the fear, it's the anxiety, it's

03:30:43.826 --> 03:30:49.006
the uncertainty. It's stumbled a
bunch of times. I think, to be fair,

03:30:50.366 --> 03:30:54.346
some of the most interesting people, right,
stumble quite a bit in life, whether it's

03:30:54.406 --> 03:30:59.426
the external environment or the internal,
whatever the universe brings. And in that,

03:31:00.846 --> 03:31:02.326
I just know a lot of people

03:31:03.186 --> 03:31:07.066
just feeling and monitoring it
right now because of everything

03:31:07.506 --> 03:31:11.026
AI is doing to our society. There's
a lot of people just terrified

03:31:11.566 --> 03:31:12.486
and really anxious.

03:31:13.366 --> 03:31:16.946
And I think there's a lot of wisdom
in what Ankur says. You can be

03:31:17.026 --> 03:31:20.906
paralyzed by that fear, or you can
imagine, "I've already won. What would

03:31:20.946 --> 03:31:24.686
the person who has won do in this
situation?" And that alleviates the fear. It

03:31:24.766 --> 03:31:29.066
actually takes the-- gives you the
optimal path under uncertainty,

03:31:30.146 --> 03:31:33.946
Under heavy uncertainty, under
a lot of uncertainty. And

03:31:34.946 --> 03:31:38.806
don't use the old playbook. Be-- try to
be the person that figures out how to

03:31:38.826 --> 03:31:42.326
write the new, the next
playbook. I think Max

03:31:42.366 --> 03:31:46.206
Tegmark, who I've spoken with many
times, 'cause he's a wild one.

03:31:46.346 --> 03:31:50.286
He's done a lot of weird and beautiful things
in his career, but he also did the mainstream

03:31:50.366 --> 03:31:54.826
stuff. He said you have to do a little bit
of both so that you don't-- you still,

03:31:55.946 --> 03:31:59.546
you still work enough
within the system that

03:32:00.266 --> 03:32:04.166
you're part of the community,
you're part of the flow of,

03:32:04.266 --> 03:32:08.206
in that case, it's science and
the development of things, but

03:32:08.306 --> 03:32:11.046
also do enough renegade
and wild and weird things

03:32:11.966 --> 03:32:15.606
to pave your own way, both
somehow, and find that

03:32:15.646 --> 03:32:23.326
balance. And not be afraid to be a
little bit weird, but also become

03:32:24.406 --> 03:32:28.386
very skillful and good at being part
of a community and a respectful part

03:32:28.406 --> 03:32:31.746
of the community. So both of those. But

03:32:32.266 --> 03:32:36.186
the, the-- just like, it's like Tom
Waits said "I like my Tom with a little

03:32:36.226 --> 03:32:38.866
drop of poison." It's like
just a little bit of weird

03:32:40.166 --> 03:32:43.746
or sometimes a lot of weird just
makes for some beautiful humans.

03:32:43.946 --> 03:32:47.766
- I think that community part is actually
gonna be even more important in the

03:32:47.826 --> 03:32:51.766
future. We've had this maybe 30-year
experiment with how much can we do with

03:32:51.806 --> 03:32:55.406
machines, and now that we can do a ton
with machines, my hope is that we're gonna

03:32:55.446 --> 03:32:59.186
realize we actually prefer to do
things with humans. We prefer to fly

03:32:59.286 --> 03:33:03.026
in and have an in-person
conversation. We prefer to have an

03:33:03.126 --> 03:33:07.246
oral discussion instead of
just reading a paper. Um,

03:33:08.346 --> 03:33:12.006
I think this-- there's
like a peak to how much

03:33:13.746 --> 03:33:15.966
our day-to-day experience...
I mean, in terms of,

03:33:16.926 --> 03:33:20.806
yes, I get a lot of fulfillment
from my scientific work and from

03:33:20.846 --> 03:33:24.546
my academic career, but going
home to my kids and, and

03:33:24.606 --> 03:33:28.526
going to the beach if FLT gets
solved and just throwing a ball

03:33:28.566 --> 03:33:31.826
with them is-- there's nothing like that.
Like, that's the meaning of my life.

03:33:31.966 --> 03:33:35.626
This other stuff is the cherry on top,
but, like, the foundation of it is,

03:33:36.486 --> 03:33:40.146
is family, is people-- being around
other people that I care about.

03:33:40.946 --> 03:33:43.546
- And I think as AI commodifies

03:33:44.446 --> 03:33:49.006
intelligence more and more, we'll
realize that more and more, that,

03:33:50.006 --> 03:33:53.946
Intelligence and all the
different puzzles that

03:33:53.986 --> 03:33:57.726
we solve is the-- just a cherry
on top or a cherry to the side.

03:33:58.206 --> 03:34:01.906
The main thing is other
humans- ... in our lives.

03:34:04.006 --> 03:34:07.546
This is-- I can't tell you how much
of an honor it is that you would

03:34:08.266 --> 03:34:12.466
come down in person for a conversation.
I've become a huge fan of all three of you.

03:34:13.306 --> 03:34:17.206
It feels a bit surreal, and
it's a big honor that you would

03:34:17.646 --> 03:34:21.626
talk with me today. This was, like, super fun.
Thank you so much. Thank you so much, Ankur, Steve,

03:34:21.806 --> 03:34:23.626
Alex. This was awesome.

03:34:23.866 --> 03:34:24.366
- Thanks for having us.

03:34:24.366 --> 03:34:25.046
- A lot of fun for us too.

03:34:25.046 --> 03:34:25.246
- Thank you. Yeah.

03:34:25.246 --> 03:34:25.906
- Thanks for having us.

03:34:26.046 --> 03:34:26.426
- This was great.

